window.GRADES = ["6th kyu", "5th kyu", "4th kyu", "3rd kyu", "2nd kyu", "1st kyu", "1st dan"];
window.TECHNIQUES = [{"id":"ashi-garami","name":"Ashi-Garami","kanji":"足絡","translation":"Leg Entanglement","category":"Katame-Waza","subcategory":"Kansetsu-Waza","difficulty":3,"grade":"1st kyu","grades":["1st kyu","1st dan"],"videos":[{"yt":"BWWb0GoAtZw","title":"足緘 /  Ashi-garami","channel":"KODOKAN"},{"yt":"ZC8EbfLmO9o","title":"Ashi garami Basics","channel":"Shintaro Higashi"},{"yt":"T0penb0MqrI","title":"Ashi Garami off the Grip and Get More Control","channel":"Brian Glick"}],"overview":"Ashi-Garami is a leg entanglement technique that locks uke's knee joint by entwining the leg with tori's legs and applying bending force against the natural direction of the knee. It is a recognized Kodokan technique but is illegal in IJF international competition due to the risk of catastrophic knee injury. It remains legal in some other competition rulesets and is studied as part of the complete Kodokan curriculum.","phases":[],"steps":[{"title":"Control uke's leg","text":"Gain control of one of uke's legs — typically the near leg — from a ground position."},{"title":"Entangle the leg with tori's legs","text":"Wrap your legs around uke's leg to create the entanglement. One leg hooks over the top of uke's leg, the other may hook under for the lock."},{"title":"Secure the leg position","text":"Tighten the entanglement so uke cannot pull the leg free. The entire lower limb is trapped."},{"title":"Apply force to the knee joint","text":"With the leg entangled, use your body mechanics — rotation, hip drive — to apply bending or twisting force to uke's knee against its natural direction."},{"title":"Apply pressure until submission","text":"Apply consistent pressure. This is an extremely dangerous technique — practice only with experienced partners and tap early."}],"principles":["IMPORTANT: Ashi-Garami is illegal in IJF international competition. Do not use in IJF-rules competition.","The knee joint is extremely vulnerable to this technique. Both partners must exercise extreme caution — the knee can be damaged quickly.","Ashi-Garami is a Kodokan-recognized technique studied for complete judo knowledge, not for competition application under IJF rules.","From the Ashi-Garami entanglement, other transitions are possible — understanding the position matters even if the straight leg lock is illegal in some contexts."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Applying in IJF competition","fix":"Never apply Ashi-Garami in IJF competition — it is banned and will result in disqualification."},{"mistake":"Applying too quickly without partner awareness","fix":"This technique must be drilled slowly and with complete partner cooperation. The knee can be injured before the partner can tap. Always warm up the joint and drill at a controlled pace."},{"mistake":"Not tapping immediately when feeling pressure","fix":"If you are in Ashi-Garami as uke, tap as soon as you feel joint pressure. Waiting to feel pain risks serious injury."},{"mistake":"Loose leg entanglement — uke escapes easily","fix":"The entanglement must be tight to prevent the leg from being pulled free. Tighten the leg wrap before applying pressure."}],"when":"Ashi-Garami is studied as part of the Kodokan syllabus for comprehensive judo knowledge. It is applicable in non-IJF competition contexts where leg attacks are permitted. In IJF competition, Ashi-Garami is explicitly prohibited. Practitioners should be aware of the competition rules governing their events before considering any leg-lock application.","setups":[],"followUps":["sankaku-garami","juji-gatame"],"counters":[],"related":["ude-garami","hiza-gatame","ude-gatame","sankaku-garami"],"source":"https://judolearn.com/techniques/ashi-garami/"},{"id":"ashi-guruma","name":"Ashi-Guruma","kanji":"足車","translation":"Leg Wheel","category":"Nage-Waza","subcategory":"Ashi-Waza","difficulty":3,"grade":"2nd kyu","grades":["2nd kyu","1st kyu","1st dan"],"videos":[{"yt":"ROeayhvom9U","title":"足車 / Ashi-guruma","channel":"KODOKAN"},{"yt":"gjMWgSssreI","title":"Ashi Guruma  - Quick Review","channel":"Shintaro Higashi"},{"yt":"Lrsae9Vpp_c","title":"How to do Ashi Guruma in Judo and BJJ | Leg Wheel | 足車","channel":"Sampson Judo"},{"yt":"ZC9GyCDJQZ0","title":"Judo Ashi-Guruma Compilation","channel":"Ochiru"}],"overview":"Ashi-Guruma is an advanced leg wheel throw in which tori places their leg horizontally across uke's thighs or lower body and uses a turning arm action to wheel uke over the extended leg. The leg acts as a fixed axle — like a wheel's axis — and the arm action rotates uke around it. It combines elements of hip throws and leg techniques, requiring precise positioning and timing. Visually similar to Harai-Goshi, but the key distinction is that the leg is placed horizontally across uke's body rather than sweeping through.","phases":[{"label":"Kuzushi · Off-balance","text":"Pull uke strongly forward and upward onto their right front corner. The lapel arm draws uke's upper body forward while the sleeve arm lifts and forward-extends. Uke's body should be tilted forward with weight on their toes before tori extends the blocking leg."},{"label":"Tsukuri · Entry","text":"Pivot to face the same direction as uke, stepping the right foot across and in front. As you complete the turn, extend your left leg horizontally across uke's thighs — the calf or lower shin contacts uke's thigh region. Your leg must be extended and firm, creating a horizontal bar across uke's forward path."},{"label":"Kake · Execution","text":"With the leg extended as the wheel's axis, execute a sharp forward bend and rotate your upper body. Pull the sleeve arm in a large downward arc while the lapel arm pushes upward. Uke's body pivots over your extended leg, rotating forward and sideward in a wide arc to land on the mat."}],"steps":[{"title":"Create forward imbalance","text":"Pull uke forward and upward with both arms, breaking their balance to their front. Uke rises onto their toes and leans forward."},{"title":"Pivot to face the same direction","text":"Step your right foot forward and across, then pivot your body to face the same direction as uke. Your pivot should be smooth and committed."},{"title":"Extend the blocking leg","text":"As you complete the pivot, extend your left leg horizontally across uke's thighs. Keep the leg firm and extended — this is the wheel axle."},{"title":"Lock body position and arms","text":"Your back should be pressed toward uke's front side. Confirm that your extended leg is making solid contact across uke's thighs."},{"title":"Rotate uke over the leg","text":"Bend forward sharply from the waist. Pull the sleeve arm in a large downward arc and push-rotate the lapel arm upward. Uke wheels over the extended leg."}],"principles":["The extended leg must be positioned horizontally and firmly across uke's thighs — any bend in the leg or wrong placement allows uke to step over it.","The wheel action is powered entirely by the arms and the body rotation — the leg is passive (a fixed axle) once placed.","Forward kuzushi must be established before placing the leg; a balanced uke will simply step around a placed leg.","The entry and leg placement must be fast — a slow entry gives uke time to post or step over."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Placing the leg at knee level instead of thigh level","fix":"The blocking leg should contact uke at mid-thigh level. At knee level, uke can bend their knee over the leg and escape; too high and the leverage is poor."},{"mistake":"Bending the blocking leg during kake","fix":"The leg must remain extended and firm throughout the throw. A bending leg collapses as uke's weight comes onto it."},{"mistake":"Insufficient kuzushi before leg placement","fix":"Uke must be leaning forward before the blocking leg is placed. Against a balanced uke, the throw cannot generate rotation."},{"mistake":"Confusing Ashi-Guruma with Harai-Goshi","fix":"In Harai-Goshi, the leg sweeps upward and through. In Ashi-Guruma, the leg is placed stationary as a horizontal bar. The throwing arm action is also wider and more circular in Ashi-Guruma."}],"when":"Ashi-Guruma is effective against opponents who have a strong forward lean or who step in aggressively. It is useful when uke's defensive posture keeps their hips back (preventing a hip throw entry), as the leg placement does not require deep hip contact. It can be executed from a lateral position relative to uke.","setups":["ko-uchi-gari"],"followUps":["tai-otoshi"],"counters":["tani-otoshi","sumi-otoshi"],"related":["harai-goshi","tai-otoshi","uchi-mata"],"source":"https://judolearn.com/techniques/ashi-guruma/"},{"id":"daki-age","name":"Daki-Age","kanji":"抱上","translation":"High-Lift Throw","category":"Nage-Waza","subcategory":"Koshi-Waza","difficulty":3,"grade":"1st dan","grades":["1st dan"],"videos":[{"yt":"_pC1F8SzmYQ","title":"Tatami Talk Kinshi-Waza: Daki Age","channel":"Tatami Talk"},{"yt":"jI45MdUdwt8","title":"DAKI AGE Hold and Lift Throw","channel":"welcomematstevescott"},{"yt":"5lFXv3crN30","title":"Daki-age - Demo","channel":"Efficient Judo"}],"overview":"Daki-Age is a powerful lifting throw in which tori wraps both arms around uke's torso — either from behind in a bear-hug position or from the front — lifts uke completely off the ground, and drops them backward or to the side. The technique requires exceptional physical strength and a large size or weight advantage. IMPORTANT: Daki-Age has been banned in IJF sanctioned competition since 2010 due to the severe risk of spinal injury — lifting uke inverted and dropping them risks catastrophic cervical or lumbar spine damage. It was also removed from the Kodokan Shinmeisho no Waza classification around 2017 and is now studied only for historical and technical context.","phases":[{"label":"Kuzushi · Off-balance","text":"Kuzushi is created by wrapping both arms tightly around uke's torso and squeezing, compressing uke's body and lifting their centre of gravity upward off the ground. Once uke is fully airborne, they have no base from which to resist — the kuzushi is absolute but requires complete lift to achieve."},{"label":"Tsukuri · Entry","text":"Enter close behind or in front of uke, wrapping both arms around their body at the waist or lower torso. For the rear version, your chest presses into uke's back. Bend your knees to get under uke's centre of gravity. Your arms lock tight in a bear-hug grip (wrists clasped or interlocked hands)."},{"label":"Kake · Execution","text":"Drive upward through your legs to lift uke entirely off the ground. Once airborne, lean backward or twist to one side, directing uke's fall to the mat — onto their back or side. The completion is uke landing with tori controlling the descent direction."}],"steps":[{"title":"Establish the bear-hug grip from behind or front","text":"Wrap both arms firmly around uke's torso at the waist level. Clasp your hands together or interlace your fingers for a secure lock. This grip is the sole mechanism of the throw — it must be tight and low enough to get under uke's center of mass."},{"title":"Bend your knees and sink under uke's center","text":"Drop your hips by bending your knees so your center of gravity is below uke's hips. This creates the leverage for the lift — you are pressing up from below uke's waist."},{"title":"Drive through the legs to achieve full lift","text":"Push explosively through both legs to straighten the knees and raise uke off the mat entirely. The lift must be complete — uke's feet must clear the ground for the throw to function."},{"title":"Lean backward or twist and direct the fall","text":"Once uke is airborne, lean backward (for the rear version) or twist to the side, using your body direction to guide where uke lands. Control the descent to avoid dropping uke vertically on their head."},{"title":"Land in a position of control","text":"Ensure uke lands on their back or side, not on their head or neck. Release or soften the grip before full impact to reduce injury risk."}],"principles":["WARNING: Daki-Age is banned in IJF competition (since 2010) due to the risk of spinal injury from dropped lifts. Study this technique only in a kata or historical context under qualified supervision.","The lift requires complete elevation of uke off the mat — a partial lift still leaves uke with ground contact and resistance.","Getting your hips below uke's center of mass is the mechanical requirement — the legs drive the lift, not the back.","The throw is only as effective as the arm wrap is tight — any gap allows uke to twist free or brace against a surface.","In the traditional context, Daki-Age demonstrated the ability to nullify any technique by lifting uke out of position entirely."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Attempting Daki-Age in IJF competition","fix":"This technique is prohibited in IJF and most major federation competitions since 2010. Attempting it results in disqualification and risks serious injury to your partner."},{"mistake":"Failing to get hips below uke's center before lifting","fix":"Bend the knees deeply before lifting. If your hips are at the same level as uke's, you will strain your lower back rather than drive through the legs."},{"mistake":"Loose arm grip — uke escapes or twists","fix":"Clasp your hands as tightly as possible and keep your elbows pulled in. A loose bear hug allows uke to rotate their torso and break free."},{"mistake":"Dropping uke vertically on their head","fix":"Always direct the fall backward or to the side. A vertical drop is dangerous and constitutes a competition foul (hansoku-make) even in situations where the lift is permitted."}],"when":"In traditional and kata contexts, Daki-Age is studied as a technique against an opponent who is extremely defensively crouched or has secured an unbreakable grip. It represents the ultimate removal of an opponent from the ground. In modern competition it is not applicable due to the ban. In randori, it may be explored cautiously with size-matched partners under direct instructor supervision.","setups":[],"followUps":[],"counters":["tani-otoshi"],"related":["ura-nage","sukui-nage","kata-guruma"],"source":"https://judolearn.com/techniques/daki-age/"},{"id":"daki-wakare","name":"Daki-Wakare","kanji":"抱分","translation":"High-Lift Separation","category":"Nage-Waza","subcategory":"Yoko-Sutemi-Waza","difficulty":3,"grade":"1st kyu","grades":["1st kyu","1st dan"],"videos":[{"yt":"Hr0cOMGBDYo","title":"抱分 / Daki-wakare","channel":"KODOKAN"},{"yt":"0UpzmPxhhvc","title":"DAKI WAKARE The Judo Version of a Gut Wrench","channel":"welcomematstevescott"},{"yt":"qgSR6walYbc","title":"Yoko guruma & Daki wakare","channel":"Millennium Judo Club Odessa"},{"yt":"kk5d4MJcaUQ","title":"Daki wakare compilation","channel":"Ochiru"}],"overview":"Daki-Wakare is an advanced side sacrifice throw where tori wraps around uke from behind, lifts them, and falls to the side — separating uke and depositing them on the mat with a high-lift motion. The \"daki\" (embracing) refers to the rear bear-hug wrap, and \"wakare\" (separation) refers to the separation of the bodies as uke is thrown. It requires considerable strength and timing.","phases":[{"label":"Kuzushi · Off-balance","text":"Tori approaches uke from behind or works around to the rear, wrapping both arms around uke's torso. The lift itself provides the kuzushi — uke is picked up and their feet leave the ground, completely removing their base."},{"label":"Tsukuri · Entry","text":"Wrap both arms around uke's torso from behind — ideally below the arms and around the midsection. The grip may clasp at the front of uke's torso, or grip the belt. Position your hips behind and below uke's hips to provide lift leverage."},{"label":"Kake · Execution","text":"Lift uke off the ground with a powerful drive from the legs and hips. Once lifted, fall to the side — the sacrifice. As you fall sideways with uke in your arms, the momentum separates uke and carries them over to land on the mat."}],"steps":[{"title":"Get behind uke or maneuver to the rear","text":"Position yourself behind uke — typically from a gripping exchange or after a failed throw attempt by uke."},{"title":"Wrap both arms around the torso","text":"Secure a deep two-arm rear wrap around uke's midsection. The grip must be secure — hands clasped or belt grip."},{"title":"Position for the lift","text":"Bend your knees and position your hips behind and below uke's hips for the upward lift drive."},{"title":"Lift uke high","text":"Drive upward with the legs, lifting uke clear of the ground. The higher the lift, the more effective the subsequent fall."},{"title":"Fall to the side and separate","text":"Fall decisively to the side while pulling uke over. As you land, the bodies separate (wakare) and uke is deposited on the mat."}],"principles":["Getting behind uke is the prerequisite — Daki-Wakare is impossible from the front or side without a complete repositioning.","The lift must be high and driven from the legs, not just the arms. Leg drive provides the power for the lift.","The side fall must be committed and complete. Hesitating creates a muddled position.","Daki-Wakare requires physical strength and is considered one of the more athletic techniques in judo."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Wrapping around the chest (too high)","fix":"The wrap should be around the midsection or below the arms. A chest-level wrap reduces lift power and may injure uke's ribs."},{"mistake":"Falling backward instead of to the side","fix":"Daki-Wakare is a yoko-sutemi (side sacrifice). Fall to the side, not straight backward — that would be a different technique."},{"mistake":"Insufficient lift before the fall","fix":"Lift uke before falling. Falling while uke is still grounded results in a tangled mess, not a throw."},{"mistake":"Losing the grip during the fall","fix":"Maintain the rear wrap until the momentum has carried uke away. Early release may drop uke on top of you."}],"when":"Daki-Wakare is used when tori manages to get behind uke — in a grip fight, after a failed throw, or in newaza transitions. It is also used as a counter when uke's forward attack exposes their back. As an advanced technique it requires extensive practice before competition use.","setups":[],"followUps":["utsuri-goshi","kesa-gatame"],"counters":[],"related":["tawara-gaeshi","yoko-guruma","yoko-wakare"],"source":"https://judolearn.com/techniques/daki-wakare/"},{"id":"de-ashi-barai","name":"De-Ashi-Barai","kanji":"出足払","translation":"Advancing Foot Sweep","category":"Nage-Waza","subcategory":"Ashi-Waza","difficulty":1,"grade":"6th kyu","grades":["6th kyu","5th kyu"],"videos":[{"yt":"4BUUvqxi_Kk","title":"出足払 / De-ashi-harai","channel":"KODOKAN"},{"yt":"zkx5UY1p1fI","title":"How to do a Judo foot sweep- Deashi barai","channel":"Shintaro Higashi"},{"yt":"XVPHtl6l58c","title":"De-Ashi-Harai Tips | Riki Judo Dojo","channel":"Riki Judo Dojo"},{"yt":"Dd6GuATqWrI","title":"De ashi barai compilation","channel":"Ochiru"}],"overview":"De-Ashi-Barai is a timing-based foot sweep that intercepts uke's advancing foot as it steps forward and bears weight. Unlike power throws, De-Ashi-Barai relies entirely on precise timing — the sweep catches uke's foot at the exact moment weight transfers onto it, making resistance impossible. When timed perfectly, uke is swept off their feet with minimal effort.","phases":[{"label":"Kuzushi · Off-balance","text":"Pull uke forward with your sleeve hand as their advancing foot lands, amplifying the moment of full weight transfer. The timing of this pull — coinciding with the sweep — is the primary kuzushi mechanism rather than prior static balance-breaking."},{"label":"Tsukuri · Entry","text":"Position yourself to the left side of uke's advancing right foot. Your left foot is your base. Your sweeping right foot is positioned and ready to intercept."},{"label":"Kake · Execution","text":"As uke's right foot lands and weight fully transfers onto it, sweep the outside of their ankle/foot with the sole of your right foot in a lateral sweeping motion. Simultaneously pull uke's sleeve arm in the direction of the sweep (forward and slightly down). The timing is everything — sweep at peak weight transfer."}],"steps":[{"title":"Create movement and read uke's stepping pattern","text":"Pull-push uke into a walking pattern using your grips. Watch for the moment they step forward with their right foot. De-Ashi-Barai lives and dies by timing."},{"title":"Position your base foot","text":"Your left foot is your base. Step so you are positioned to the left side of uke's advancing foot, able to sweep across your body without overreaching."},{"title":"Wait for peak weight transfer","text":"The attack window is the instant uke's foot lands and full body weight transfers onto it. Too early and their foot is still mobile. Too late and they are planted and stable."},{"title":"Sweep the ankle with your right foot","text":"Sweep the outside of uke's ankle in a flat, lateral motion using the sole of your foot. The sweep direction is across uke's direction of travel."},{"title":"Pull with your sleeve grip to amplify","text":"As you sweep, pull uke's sleeve arm forward and slightly downward in the same direction as the sweep. This rotational pull amplifies the sweeping action."}],"principles":["Timing is the entire technique. De-Ashi-Barai cannot be forced — it must be timed to intercept the advancing foot.","The sweep is flat and lateral — sweeping across the ground, not upward. A low sweep is more effective than a high one.","Create the stepping pattern you want to exploit. Use your grips to initiate movement in uke that produces the advancing step.","The attack window is the instant the heel contacts the mat and weight fully commits. Sweep immediately at heel-contact — sweeping during flight misses; waiting until they are planted loses the window."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Sweeping too early — foot is still moving","fix":"Wait until the foot has landed. Sweeping a moving foot just pushes it aside without unbalancing uke."},{"mistake":"Sweeping too late — uke is fully planted","fix":"You must catch the foot at peak weight transfer. If uke is stable, the window has closed."},{"mistake":"Sweeping upward rather than laterally","fix":"The sweep is a flat, horizontal motion across the ground. Think of sweeping a ball, not kicking it upward."},{"mistake":"Not creating movement — waiting passively","fix":"Generate uke's movement with your grips. Push-pull them into a stepping rhythm so you control when they step."}],"when":"De-Ashi-Barai is most effective when uke is moving — walking forward, stepping in to attack, or returning from a missed throw. Against a static uke it is very difficult. Excellent surprise technique when uke expects a grip fight — the foot sweep arrives from an unexpected direction.","setups":["seoi-nage","osoto-gari"],"followUps":[],"counters":["tsubame-gaeshi","ko-uchi-gari"],"related":["ko-soto-gari","ko-uchi-gari","uchi-mata"],"source":"https://judolearn.com/techniques/de-ashi-barai/"},{"id":"do-jime","name":"Do-Jime","kanji":"胴絞","translation":"Trunk Scissors","category":"Katame-Waza","subcategory":"Shime-Waza","difficulty":2,"grade":"3rd kyu","grades":["3rd kyu"],"videos":[{"yt":"D_0fFcoIbvY","title":"胴絞 /  Do-jime","channel":"KODOKAN"},{"yt":"cGjcdGzz55w","title":"DOJIME Body Squeeze or Scissors","channel":"welcomematstevescott"},{"yt":"2iUwIXhZi04","title":"DOJIME CONSTRICTING AND SQUEEZING OPPONENT'S BODY","channel":"welcomematstevescott"}],"overview":"Do-Jime is a scissors-hold in which tori wraps both legs around uke's torso (trunk/waist area) and squeezes with the thighs to compress the rib cage and diaphragm. IMPORTANT: Do-Jime is permanently banned in IJF sanctioned competition due to the risk of severe spinal injury — the compressive and twisting force of leg scissors around the torso can cause lumbar vertebra damage or internal organ injury. Despite the competition ban, Do-Jime remains part of the official Kodokan shime-waza classification and appears in traditional kata (particularly Katame no Kata) and formal curricula study. It is listed here for historical and technical completeness.","phases":[{"label":"Kuzushi · Off-balance","text":"Do-Jime is a ne-waza technique; the traditional kuzushi–tsukuri–kake framework for standing throws does not directly apply. Positional control is established by securing body contact around uke's trunk — typically from guard position or while controlling uke's upper body — before the legs are wrapped and locked around the torso."},{"label":"Tsukuri · Entry","text":"From a supine or guard position, wrap both legs around uke's torso at the waist or lower rib cage. The ankles may cross behind uke's back or hook together. Your legs are positioned above uke's hips to compress the trunk, not the pelvis. Body contact is maintained throughout."},{"label":"Kake · Execution","text":"Squeeze both thighs inward to compress uke's rib cage and restrict diaphragm movement. The compression force is directed inward from both sides simultaneously. In the traditional form, sustained squeezing forces uke to submit from respiratory restriction or discomfort."}],"steps":[{"title":"Establish guard position around uke's torso","text":"From your back with uke inside your legs, position your legs around uke's torso at the lower rib cage level — above the hips and below the armpits."},{"title":"Cross or hook ankles behind uke's back","text":"Cross your ankles together or hook them to lock the leg wrap in position. The locked leg position is what generates and sustains the squeeze pressure."},{"title":"Squeeze the thighs together to compress the trunk","text":"Drive both thighs inward, compressing uke's rib cage from both sides simultaneously. The squeeze should be steady and increasing — not a single burst of force."},{"title":"Maintain upper body control","text":"Control uke's posture with your arms throughout — gripping the collar, sleeves, or neck area to prevent uke from posturing up and breaking the leg wrap."}],"principles":["SAFETY WARNING: Do-Jime is banned in IJF competition (permanently). It is studied only in kata and traditional contexts. Never apply Do-Jime in randori or competition settings.","The technique compresses the rib cage and restricts breathing — it operates by respiratory restriction rather than carotid artery compression.","Leg positioning is above the hips (at the lower ribs), not at the pelvis. Pelvis-level scissors (do-jime applied at the hip joint) carries a higher spinal injury risk.","The IJF ban reflects the spinal injury risk when a powerful leg scissors applies rotational and compressive force to the lumbar spine simultaneously.","In Katame no Kata, Do-Jime is studied formally as part of the shime-waza section, demonstrating its technical legitimacy within classical judo."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Applying Do-Jime in IJF competition or standard randori","fix":"Do-Jime is banned in competition and not appropriate for normal randori. Study it only in kata contexts under direct supervision."},{"mistake":"Leg position at the pelvis level rather than the lower ribs","fix":"The legs should encircle the torso above the hips, at the lower rib cage. Pelvis-level scissors creates a dangerous twisting force on the lumbar spine."},{"mistake":"Applying explosive bursts of force rather than steady compression","fix":"In kata practice, apply Do-Jime with slow, controlled increasing pressure. Explosive force is particularly dangerous to the ribs and spine."}],"when":"Do-Jime is studied exclusively in traditional kata contexts (Katame no Kata) and classical curriculum. It has no application in modern competition. Understanding it provides completeness in the Kodokan shime-waza classification and context for why the IJF introduced the prohibition.","setups":[],"followUps":[],"counters":[],"related":["sankaku-jime","sankaku-garami"],"source":"https://judolearn.com/techniques/do-jime/"},{"id":"gyaku-juji-jime","name":"Gyaku-Juji-Jime","kanji":"逆十字絞","translation":"Reverse Cross Choke","category":"Katame-Waza","subcategory":"Shime-Waza","difficulty":2,"grade":"3rd kyu","grades":["3rd kyu","2nd kyu","1st kyu"],"videos":[{"yt":"t3tQriIPdlI","title":"逆十字絞 /  Gyaku-juji-jime","channel":"KODOKAN"},{"yt":"Urrf15zcZrQ","title":"Gyaku Juji Jime","channel":"Judoclub Meeuwen-Gruitrode"},{"yt":"wphA_2ITYOI","title":"Easy Juji Gatame from Gyaku Juji Jime","channel":"Sampson Judo"},{"yt":"37St5lkwE4M","title":"GYAKU JUJI JIME ROLLOVER TO PIN","channel":"welcomematstevescott"}],"overview":"Gyaku-Juji-Jime is a cross-collar choke in which both of tori's thumbs are inserted inside uke's collar — thumbs-in rather than the fingers-in grip of Nami-Juji-Jime. The thumbs-in grip pulls the collar edges outward and into the sides of uke's neck, compressing the carotid arteries. Gyaku (\"reverse\") refers to the reversed grip orientation compared to Nami-Juji-Jime. This choke is applied from a top position (mount, side control, or guard) and is one of the most reliable submission techniques in judo ground work.","phases":[{"label":"Kuzushi · Off-balance","text":"From a mounted or top position, control uke's upper body by keeping chest pressure and body weight down. Before gripping, ensure uke's collar is accessible — widen the lapel with one hand if needed. Uke's defensive movements (bridging, pushing) are neutralised by maintaining close body contact."},{"label":"Tsukuri · Entry","text":"Insert your right thumb into uke's left collar (your right, their left) as deep as possible toward the back of the neck. Then insert your left thumb into uke's right collar, crossing over your right arm. Both thumbs point toward uke's spine at the back of the collar. The crossed arms form an X shape (juji = cross/X)."},{"label":"Kake · Execution","text":"With both thumbs deep in the collar, rotate your wrists outward (thumbs rotating away from each other) while pulling the collar edges forward and outward. This action drives the collar edges into the sides of uke's neck, compressing the carotid arteries bilaterally. Simultaneously drop your chest onto uke to add body weight pressure."}],"steps":[{"title":"Establish top control position","text":"Secure a dominant top position — mount, modified mount, or kesa-gatame. Press your body weight onto uke to limit their movement."},{"title":"Open uke's collar","text":"If the collar is tight, use one hand to widen the lapel opening near the back of the neck to allow deep thumb insertion."},{"title":"Insert the first thumb (right hand)","text":"Insert your right thumb inside uke's left collar, sliding as deep as possible toward the back of the neck. Thumb points toward spine."},{"title":"Cross and insert the second thumb (left hand)","text":"Cross your left arm over your right and insert your left thumb inside uke's right collar. Left arm is on top of the cross."},{"title":"Apply the choke","text":"Rotate both wrists outward while pulling the collar edges forward and apart. Drop your chest onto uke. Maintain pressure until submission."}],"principles":["The thumbs must be inserted as deep as possible — a shallow grip means the collar edge cannot reach the carotid arteries.","The wrist rotation (outward) is the choking mechanism — pulling without rotating produces a weaker choke.","Body weight augments the arm pressure — leaning your chest onto uke multiplies the effectiveness.","Crossed arms (X pattern) is essential — parallel arms mean both collar edges pull in the same direction, reducing bilateral carotid compression."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Shallow thumb insertion (fingers on collar, thumb barely inside)","fix":"Push the thumb as far inside the collar as possible, ideally to the second knuckle at minimum. Shallow insertion prevents the collar edge from reaching the carotid."},{"mistake":"Pulling collar forward without rotating wrists","fix":"The wrist rotation (thumbs rotating outward) is what drives the collar edge into the neck. A pure pull compresses the front of the neck (trachea) rather than the sides (carotids)."},{"mistake":"Arms not crossed (parallel instead of X)","fix":"The arms must cross. If both thumbs pull in the same direction, only one side of the neck is compressed."},{"mistake":"Top body too far from uke's face","fix":"Drive your chest toward uke's face to add weight to the choke. Sitting upright relies entirely on arm strength."}],"when":"Gyaku-Juji-Jime is effective from mount (Tate-Shiho-Gatame) and from a modified top position after a failed throw transition. It works especially well when uke attempts to push up with both hands — their extended arms give tori access to both collars. It is an excellent response to uke's upward arm push from the bottom position.","setups":[],"followUps":["tate-shiho-gatame","juji-gatame"],"counters":[],"related":["nami-juji-jime","kata-juji-jime","okuri-eri-jime"],"source":"https://judolearn.com/techniques/gyaku-juji-jime/"},{"id":"hadaka-jime","name":"Hadaka-Jime","kanji":"裸絞","translation":"Naked Choke (Rear Naked Choke)","category":"Katame-Waza","subcategory":"Shime-Waza","difficulty":2,"grade":"3rd kyu","grades":["3rd kyu","2nd kyu"],"videos":[{"yt":"9f0n8jez7iA","title":"裸絞 /  Hadaka-jime","channel":"KODOKAN"},{"yt":"XJ86f7xkE6s","title":"Hadaka-Jime Tips | Riki Judo Dojo","channel":"Riki Judo Dojo"},{"yt":"1cCd52pgArA","title":"HADAKA JIME.     JUDO Rear Naked Choke by G Spinks 7dan","channel":"Judo Life"}],"overview":"Hadaka-Jime is applied without using the judogi — tori's forearm wraps directly around uke's neck. Known widely as the \"rear naked choke\" in MMA and submission grappling, it is highly effective because it does not depend on gripping the jacket. Applied from behind, the forearm compresses the carotid arteries. It is the most widely recognized submission hold in all combat sports.","phases":[],"steps":[{"title":"Establish back control","text":"Get behind uke with your chest against their back. Hook your legs around uke's hips to maintain the position."},{"title":"Slide your right arm across uke's throat","text":"Wrap your right arm around uke's neck so the crook of your elbow aligns with the center of their throat. Your forearm slides across to their right carotid."},{"title":"Place your left hand behind your right bicep","text":"Reach your left hand over uke's left shoulder and grip your own right bicep to form the figure-four: your right arm is across the throat, your left hand squeezes the right bicep, and your right hand rests on your own left shoulder or the back of uke's head."},{"title":"Place your left hand behind uke's head","text":"Push uke's head forward with your left hand. This tightens the choke by removing their ability to pull their chin down."},{"title":"Squeeze both arms and arch back","text":"Squeeze your arms together to compress both carotids. Arch your back to intensify the pressure. Both elements together create rapid carotid compression."}],"principles":["The crook of the elbow should be at the center of the throat (adam's apple area), not the forearm across the throat.","The figure-four grip (hand on bicep) amplifies pressure significantly over a simple wrist grip.","Pushing the head forward with the rear hand removes uke's ability to tuck their chin, which is the primary defense.","Arching away from uke stretches the neck and tightens the grip simultaneously."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Forearm across the throat (windpipe) instead of carotid","fix":"The crook of the elbow — not the forearm — sits at the throat. The forearm and upper arm compress the carotids on both sides."},{"mistake":"Not using the figure-four grip","fix":"Grip your bicep, not your wrist. The figure-four creates far more mechanical pressure."},{"mistake":"Allowing uke to tuck their chin","fix":"Push their head forward with your rear hand to prevent chin tuck. Once uke tucks the chin, the choke is partially blocked."}],"when":"Hadaka-Jime is the primary option whenever you have back control. If uke's collar is accessible, Okuri-Eri-Jime is an equally strong choice — both should be drilled as co-primary options from the back position. Extremely effective when uke is turtled and you have hooks in, or when no gi collar is available.","setups":[],"followUps":["okuri-eri-jime"],"counters":[],"related":["okuri-eri-jime","nami-juji-jime","juji-gatame"],"source":"https://judolearn.com/techniques/hadaka-jime/"},{"id":"hane-goshi","name":"Hane-Goshi","kanji":"跳腰","translation":"Spring Hip Throw","category":"Nage-Waza","subcategory":"Koshi-Waza","difficulty":2,"grade":"4th kyu","grades":["4th kyu","3rd kyu"],"videos":[{"yt":"M9_7De6A1kk","title":"跳腰 / Hane-goshi","channel":"KODOKAN"},{"yt":"UpfmXDrD05U","title":"How to do Hane Goshi","channel":"Sampson Judo"},{"yt":"7t2_f2dog_g","title":"Hane-Goshi Tips | Riki Judo Dojo","channel":"Riki Judo Dojo"},{"yt":"c-5NkbT4ddc","title":"Harai-Goshi Compilation","channel":"Ochiru"}],"overview":"Hane-Goshi is a dynamic hip throw that adds a spring or bounce action to the hip entry. Unlike Harai-Goshi where the leg sweeps, in Hane-Goshi tori bends the knee and \"springs\" (hane) it into uke's thigh in an upward, springing motion while simultaneously loading uke over the hip. The spring action creates a powerful launching effect, projecting uke high and far.","phases":[{"label":"Kuzushi · Off-balance","text":"Pull uke forward and upward onto their right front corner with the sleeve grip. The lapel arm simultaneously pulls uke's chest forward. Uke must be on their toes with weight fully forward. The higher and more forward the kuzushi, the more effective the spring action of the knee will be."},{"label":"Tsukuri · Entry","text":"Step the right foot in front of uke and pivot so your back faces uke's chest, similar to a standard hip throw entry. Bend both knees as you enter. Your right knee bends deeply — preparing to spring. Keep your back tight to uke's chest. The sleeve and lapel grips maintain upward tension."},{"label":"Kake · Execution","text":"Extend both legs explosively — the right bent knee drives upward and springs into uke's inner thigh or the back of their right thigh. This springing knee action lifts uke off the ground and combines with the rotation of your upper body and arm pulls to project uke in a high, powerful arc over your right hip."}],"steps":[{"title":"Create forward-upward kuzushi","text":"Pull sleeve arm upward and forward. Lapel arm pulls chest forward. Uke rises to their toes with weight loading forward."},{"title":"Step in and pivot","text":"Step right foot in front of uke and pivot back-to-chest. Bend both knees deeply on entry."},{"title":"Load the springing knee","text":"Bend the right knee deeply, bringing the heel up toward the back of the thigh. This coils the spring for the kake."},{"title":"Drive the knee upward into uke's thigh","text":"Spring the right knee upward into uke's inner thigh. The knee drives upward and forward — not a sweep, but a spring or bounce."},{"title":"Complete the throw with rotation and arm pull","text":"Simultaneously rotate forward at the hip, pull both arms forward and downward. Uke is launched over the hip in a high arc."}],"principles":["The spring (hane) action is the distinctive feature — the knee bends and then explodes upward. This is different from the sweeping action of Harai-Goshi.","The springing knee creates vertical lift. Uke is launched upward and over, not just swept forward.","Both knees must bend on entry. The standing leg bends to lower the hip; the springing leg coils for the drive.","Timing the spring to uke's loaded forward position is essential. The spring amplifies the force already created by kuzushi."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Sweeping the leg instead of springing","fix":"Hane-Goshi uses a bent-knee spring, not a sweeping extension. The knee bends deeply and then drives upward — think spring, not sweep."},{"mistake":"Insufficient knee bend before the spring","fix":"The knee must coil deeply before the spring. A shallow bend produces a weak spring with minimal lifting force."},{"mistake":"Springing without forward rotation","fix":"The spring amplifies rotation — it does not replace it. Rotate your upper body forward simultaneously with the spring for maximum throw power."},{"mistake":"Hip too high — not below uke's hip","fix":"Bend the knees on entry to lower your hip below uke's. A high hip blocks the loading and reduces the effectiveness of the spring."}],"when":"Hane-Goshi is effective against opponents who defend standard hip throws by spreading their legs or lowering their hips. The spring action bypasses low defenses by adding vertical lift. It is also effective against opponents with good forward kuzushi sensitivity — the explosive spring provides force before they can react.","setups":["ko-uchi-gari","o-uchi-gari"],"followUps":["hane-makikomi"],"counters":["tani-otoshi","ura-nage"],"related":["harai-goshi","uchi-mata","tsurikomi-goshi","hane-makikomi"],"source":"https://judolearn.com/techniques/hane-goshi/"},{"id":"hane-makikomi","name":"Hane-Makikomi","kanji":"跳巻込","translation":"Spring Wraparound","category":"Nage-Waza","subcategory":"Yoko-Sutemi-Waza","difficulty":2,"grade":"3rd kyu","grades":["3rd kyu","2nd kyu"],"videos":[{"yt":"6CRBGLGz9j8","title":"跳巻込 / Hane-makikomi","channel":"KODOKAN"},{"yt":"t1DFCzazA08","title":"Hane Makikomi || Spring Wraparound Throw 2.0","channel":"The Judo Way of Life"},{"yt":"iyRnLnxwGLw","title":"Harai-makikomi compilation","channel":"Ochiru"}],"overview":"Hane-Makikomi is the makikomi variant of Hane-Goshi — combining the springing knee action with an arm wrap and side fall sacrifice. Tori enters for Hane-Goshi but wraps uke's arm and falls to the side, creating a more committed throw. The spring of the knee combined with the sacrifice makes this a particularly powerful technique that is difficult to defend once the arm is wrapped.","phases":[{"label":"Kuzushi · Off-balance","text":"Pull uke forward and upward — identical to Hane-Goshi kuzushi. The sleeve arm elevates and pulls uke forward. Uke's weight loads to their front. The arm wrap begins during the entry to set up the makikomi element."},{"label":"Tsukuri · Entry","text":"Step in as for Hane-Goshi — right foot in front of uke, pivot to back-facing. Simultaneously wrap the entry arm around uke's arm. Bend both knees on entry, coiling the spring leg for the kake."},{"label":"Kake · Execution","text":"Spring the right knee upward into uke's thigh while simultaneously maintaining the arm wrap and falling to the side. The three elements — spring, wrap, fall — converge simultaneously. Uke is thrown in a powerful arc with the spring launching them and the sacrifice ensuring follow-through."}],"steps":[{"title":"Create forward kuzushi","text":"Pull uke forward and upward onto their front corner."},{"title":"Step in and pivot","text":"Step in, pivot back-to-chest, begin arm wrap."},{"title":"Wrap the arm","text":"Trap uke's arm against your chest as you complete the pivot."},{"title":"Coil the spring leg","text":"Bend the right knee deeply — coiling for the spring action."},{"title":"Spring, wrap, and fall","text":"Simultaneously: spring the knee up into uke's thigh, maintain the arm wrap, and fall to the side. Uke is thrown powerfully."}],"principles":["The spring must be genuine — bend the knee deeply and drive upward with force. The spring amplifies the sacrifice throw significantly.","All three elements must occur simultaneously: spring, arm wrap, side fall.","Hane-Makikomi is a high-commitment throw — once entered, follow through completely.","Ensure uke has good ukemi before drilling at speed, as the arm wrap limits their defensive breakfall."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Insufficient knee bend before the spring","fix":"Coil the knee deeply on entry. A shallow bend produces a weak spring with minimal lift effect."},{"mistake":"Spring then fall — sequential rather than simultaneous","fix":"All three elements converge at the same moment. Practice the coordination in slow drills before adding speed."},{"mistake":"Loose arm wrap","fix":"Pull uke's arm firmly to your chest. Loose wrap lets uke post and defend."},{"mistake":"Falling to the wrong side","fix":"Fall to the side corresponding to the spring leg. Falling to the wrong side negates the spring direction."}],"when":"Hane-Makikomi is used when Hane-Goshi defenses are anticipated, or as a committed entry when uke's arm position can be wrapped. Practitioners who use Hane-Goshi as their main throw often develop this variant to counter the common defenses.","setups":["ko-uchi-gari","o-uchi-gari"],"followUps":[],"counters":[],"related":["hane-goshi","harai-makikomi","uchi-mata-makikomi","soto-makikomi"],"source":"https://judolearn.com/techniques/hane-makikomi/"},{"id":"hara-gatame","name":"Hara-Gatame","kanji":"腹固","translation":"Stomach Armlock","category":"Katame-Waza","subcategory":"Kansetsu-Waza","difficulty":3,"grade":"1st kyu","grades":["1st kyu","1st dan"],"videos":[{"yt":"ZzEycg8R_9M","title":"腕挫腹固 /  Ude-hishigi-hara-gatame","channel":"KODOKAN"},{"yt":"jPiYUC3D5tw","title":"Hara-Gatame","channel":"Ki-Totejutsu"},{"yt":"_9xZYaGLaxc","title":"Ude Hishigi Hara Gatame in Competition","channel":"JudoData"}],"overview":"Hara-Gatame is an armlock in which tori traps uke's arm across tori's own abdomen or hip and uses their body weight to hyperextend uke's elbow joint — with tori's stomach/hip acting as the fulcrum rather than the hip as in Juji-Gatame. Applied from north-south position (tori straddles uke's torso or sits on uke's chest facing uke's feet), tori captures one of uke's arms across their own midsection, controls the wrist, and leans or sinks their body weight to drive the elbow into extension. It is part of the official Kodokan kansetsu-waza (joint technique) classification.","phases":[],"steps":[{"title":"Establish north-south or straddle position on uke's chest","text":"Position yourself facing uke's feet while sitting on or straddling their chest area. This north-south orientation gives you access to both of uke's arms from above."},{"title":"Capture uke's target arm and extend it","text":"Trap one of uke's arms — pulling it upward and straightening it. The arm should be extended, with the elbow straight. Grip near uke's wrist with both hands or one hand and your arm."},{"title":"Position uke's elbow against your abdomen","text":"Pull uke's extended arm across your stomach and hip area so that the back of uke's elbow rests against your lower abdomen or hip. Your stomach is now the fulcrum for the extension."},{"title":"Secure the wrist and control the arm direction","text":"Hold uke's wrist firmly with both hands and control the direction of the arm — the wrist should be directed downward (toward the mat) while the elbow presses against your abdomen."},{"title":"Apply body weight to hyperextend the elbow","text":"Lean forward or sink your body weight downward through your abdomen into uke's elbow, while simultaneously pulling the wrist downward. The opposing forces — weight down on elbow, wrist pulled down past the fulcrum — hyperextend the joint. Apply progressively, releasing immediately when uke taps."}],"principles":["The fulcrum is your own abdomen or hip — this distinguishes Hara-Gatame from Juji-Gatame (hip/thigh fulcrum) and Ude-Gatame (wrist fulcrum).","The arm must be fully extended before pressure is applied. Any bend at the elbow significantly reduces effectiveness and can cause the lock to slip.","Body weight is the primary force — leaning into the elbow via the stomach, not pulling with arms alone. Tori's mass drives the hyperextension.","The wrist must be directed downward (toward the mat surface) opposite to the upward pressure at the elbow — this creates the opposing force couple that breaks the joint.","North-south position is the most stable application point, giving tori full body-weight access while uke has limited bridging or rolling escape options.","Apply Hara-Gatame with progressive pressure only — the elbow joint under this angle reaches the hyperextension limit quickly."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Elbow not against the abdomen — fulcrum is missing","fix":"Pull uke's arm tight across your midsection until the elbow is in contact with your lower abdomen. Without the fulcrum contact, the extension force has no pivot point."},{"mistake":"Arm still bent at the elbow when applying pressure","fix":"Straighten uke's arm completely before applying body weight. A bent arm absorbs the pressure without reaching hyperextension until it is straightened."},{"mistake":"Using only arm strength to pull the wrist down without body weight","fix":"Lean your body forward or sink downward through the abdomen. The shoulder and body weight greatly exceed the force available from arms alone."},{"mistake":"Applying too rapidly — risk of elbow injury","fix":"Hara-Gatame reaches the hyperextension limit quickly. Apply slowly and with progressive force, giving uke sufficient time to tap. Rapid application is a significant injury risk."},{"mistake":"Unstable north-south position — uke bridges and escapes","fix":"Sit firmly on uke's chest and maintain base with your legs on both sides. An unstable position allows uke to bridge and roll, breaking the arm contact."}],"when":"Hara-Gatame is most applicable when tori is in north-south position and uke has an arm trapped at chest level. It can also be applied as a transition when Juji-Gatame entry is blocked — if uke fights the standard perpendicular positioning, shifting to north-south and applying Hara-Gatame can catch uke off guard. In competition, it is a useful secondary armlock when the primary Juji-Gatame position is not available. Also applicable when uke reaches up from bottom position and over-extends an arm toward tori.","setups":[],"followUps":["kami-shiho-gatame","juji-gatame"],"counters":[],"related":["juji-gatame","ude-gatame","waki-gatame"],"source":"https://judolearn.com/techniques/hara-gatame/"},{"id":"harai-goshi","name":"Harai-Goshi","kanji":"払腰","translation":"Sweeping Hip Throw","category":"Nage-Waza","subcategory":"Koshi-Waza","difficulty":2,"grade":"4th kyu","grades":["4th kyu","3rd kyu"],"videos":[{"yt":"qTo8HlAAkOo","title":"払腰 / Harai-goshi","channel":"KODOKAN"},{"yt":"_gcIWtS-9Ms","title":"Harai-goshi - Basic principles","channel":"Efficient Judo"},{"yt":"hUO6LPELX_8","title":"Harai-goshi - Competitive variations","channel":"Efficient Judo"},{"yt":"c-5NkbT4ddc","title":"Harai-Goshi Compilation","channel":"Ochiru"}],"overview":"Harai-Goshi is a powerful hip throw that adds a sweeping leg action to the classic hip entry. After turning in to place the hip, tori sweeps their right leg backward into uke's legs, amplifying the throwing force with leg sweep mechanics. It is one of the most effective throws in competition judo.","phases":[{"label":"Kuzushi · Off-balance","text":"Pull uke forward and upward onto their right front corner. Your sleeve hand elevates uke's arm while your lapel hand pulls them forward. The goal is full forward kuzushi — uke leaning forward with weight on the balls of their feet."},{"label":"Tsukuri · Entry","text":"Step your right foot in front of uke's right foot and pivot so your back faces uke's chest. Your lapel arm stays high, pulling uke onto your hip. Bend your knees to lower your hips below uke's hip level."},{"label":"Kake · Execution","text":"Straighten your right leg and sweep it backward and then upward into uke's right thigh, driving the back of your thigh through uke's leg. Simultaneously pull with both hands and rotate forward at the hip. The upward component of the sweep elevates uke's legs off the ground, sending them over in a powerful arc."}],"steps":[{"title":"Drive uke forward onto their toes (kuzushi)","text":"Pull the sleeve arm upward and forward. Your lapel hand pulls forward at chest height. Uke must be loaded forward before entry."},{"title":"Step in with your right foot","text":"Step your right foot in front of uke's right foot, toes pointed forward. This is your anchor point for the pivot."},{"title":"Pivot to back-facing position","text":"Rotate on your right foot, sweeping your left foot around until your back faces uke's chest. Bend your knees to lower your hips. Your right hip presses against uke's lower abdomen — positioned slightly above their hip line to act as the throwing rail, not just a fulcrum."},{"title":"Initiate the leg sweep","text":"Swing your right leg backward and into uke's right thigh or the back of their leg. The sweep begins as you start rotating forward."},{"title":"Complete the throw with rotation and sweep","text":"Pull both hands downward and forward. Straighten your standing leg. The combination of rotation, arm pull, and leg sweep projects uke powerfully over your right hip."}],"principles":["The leg sweep amplifies hip throw mechanics — the sweep is not a trip, it sweeps uke's legs away while you project them forward.","Contact the back of uke's thigh with the back of your thigh — mid-thigh contact maximizes lever effect.","Strong kuzushi is essential. Harai-Goshi against an upright uke often results in a blocked entry.","Keep the lapel arm high and pulling forward throughout. Dropping this arm kills the throw."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Sweeping too early (before the hip is loaded)","fix":"Establish hip contact first, then sweep. Sweeping prematurely misses uke's leg and you lose your base."},{"mistake":"Sweeping at the ankle instead of the thigh","fix":"Contact should be thigh-to-thigh. Sweeping at the ankle loses leverage and is easily defended."},{"mistake":"Dropping the lapel arm during kake","fix":"The lapel arm must pull forward throughout. If it drops, uke's upper body stays upright and the throw dies."},{"mistake":"Standing too upright — hips not below uke","fix":"Bend your knees on entry. If your hip is the same height as uke's, you cannot lever them over."}],"when":"Harai-Goshi is particularly effective when uke has a defensive posture that blocks hip entry — the sweeping leg bypasses their defensive leg placement. Works well after combinations from O-Uchi-Gari or Ko-Uchi-Gari. Most effective against same-height or slightly shorter opponents, or when uke is bent forward enough that hip entry is accessible regardless of height.","setups":["o-uchi-gari","ko-uchi-gari"],"followUps":["uchi-mata"],"counters":["harai-goshi-gaeshi","ura-nage","tani-otoshi","uchi-mata-gaeshi"],"related":["uchi-mata","o-goshi","hane-goshi","de-ashi-barai"],"source":"https://judolearn.com/techniques/harai-goshi/"},{"id":"harai-goshi-gaeshi","name":"Harai-Goshi-Gaeshi","kanji":"払腰返","translation":"Harai-Goshi Counter","category":"Nage-Waza","subcategory":"Koshi-Waza","difficulty":3,"grade":"2nd kyu","grades":["2nd kyu","1st kyu"],"videos":[{"yt":"4U3It-7PPsc","title":"払腰返 / Harai-goshi-gaeshi","channel":"KODOKAN"},{"yt":"t2hgLa7BZbE","title":"Harai-goshi-gaeshi - Demo","channel":"Efficient Judo"},{"yt":"JBp_bgu6iHA","title":"HARAI GOSHI GAESHI   COUNTER TO HARAI GOSHI","channel":"Judo Advantage"},{"yt":"br5HU1vqJzE","title":"HARAI GOSHI GAESHI CUT AGAINST GRAIN OF THROW","channel":"JBBA Judo Education Channel"}],"overview":"Harai-Goshi-Gaeshi is the counter-throw to Harai-Goshi. As uke enters for the hip sweep — rotating across tori's front with the sweeping leg extended — tori steps to uke's outside (the side opposite uke's sweeping leg), wraps the waist or hip, and throws with a backward hip arch that uses uke's own rotational momentum. The counter is classified as koshi-waza (hip technique) because the throwing mechanism involves hip engagement against uke's hip. Part of the Kodokan Shinmeisho no Waza.","phases":[{"label":"Kuzushi · Off-balance","text":"Kuzushi is supplied by uke's own forward rotational drive into the Harai-Goshi entry. As uke turns across tori's front, their body weight and momentum are directed forward and rotationally — tori exploits this by wrapping and redirecting that momentum backward into the counter-throw. The force uke generates for the original throw becomes the force that topples them."},{"label":"Tsukuri · Entry","text":"As uke rotates into the Harai-Goshi hip entry, step your outside foot (right foot if uke enters right) around to uke's outside-rear, bypassing uke's entry. Simultaneously wrap your arm around uke's waist or grip uke's hip. Your chest comes against uke's back or side. Your hips press into uke's lower back as you position for the backward arch."},{"label":"Kake · Execution","text":"Arch backward sharply, driving your hips into uke's lower back while pulling uke's upper body forward and over. Uke's rotational momentum carries them over your hip in a backward arc. Direct the fall so uke lands on their back to tori's rear."}],"steps":[{"title":"Read uke's Harai-Goshi entry","text":"Recognize the grip pull and hip rotation that signal a Harai-Goshi attempt. The moment uke begins to turn their back and extend the sweeping leg, the counter window opens."},{"title":"Step to uke's outside to bypass the hip entry","text":"Step your foot to the outside of uke's entry — to the side not being swept — so you move around uke's attack rather than into it. This positions you behind or to the side of uke's center."},{"title":"Wrap the waist and press your hip into uke's back","text":"As you step around, wrap your arm around uke's waist and pull your hips into uke's lower back or hip. Your body now loads against uke's rear-center."},{"title":"Arch backward to load the counter throw","text":"Drive your hips backward into uke's lower back while arching your upper body away. This creates a hip fulcrum that lifts uke's feet off the mat and redirects their forward rotation backward."},{"title":"Pull uke's upper body forward and over","text":"Pull uke's arm and torso forward (in the direction they were already rotating) as you arch backward. Uke's body folds over your hip and falls to the mat behind you."}],"principles":["The counter leverages uke's rotational commitment — the more aggressively uke enters Harai-Goshi, the stronger the counter.","Stepping to the outside rather than resisting in front is the key positional move. Trying to block Harai-Goshi from in front is far less effective.","The hip arch backward is the throwing mechanism — press the hips into uke's lower back and arch away to create the backward lift over the hip.","Uke's forward rotational direction during the Harai-Goshi entry is preserved in the counter — they continue rotating, but now backward over tori's hip.","Timing must be at the entry phase. If uke has already completed the sweep and established position, the counter is more difficult."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Trying to resist the Harai-Goshi frontally instead of stepping outside","fix":"Move to uke's outside rather than blocking. Frontal resistance allows uke to adjust their hip position or add more force."},{"mistake":"Stepping inside uke's attack instead of outside","fix":"Ensure you step to the outside (away from the sweeping leg). Stepping to the inside places you in the path of the original throw."},{"mistake":"No hip contact — counter throw becomes an arm throw only","fix":"Your hip must press into uke's lower back to create the fulcrum. Without hip contact, the backward arch has no lever."},{"mistake":"Counter throw too late — uke has completed Harai-Goshi and released","fix":"The counter must be initiated during uke's entry. After uke has swept through and released the throw, the moment is past."}],"when":"Harai-Goshi-Gaeshi is most effective against opponents who drive aggressively and repeatedly with Harai-Goshi. The heavier uke's hip rotation, the more power is available for the counter. It can also apply against Hane-Goshi and similar hip-sweep entries where uke rotates across tori's front. A player who establishes this counter forces opponents to slow or modify their Harai-Goshi attack.","setups":["harai-goshi"],"followUps":["utsuri-goshi"],"counters":[],"related":["harai-goshi","utsuri-goshi"],"source":"https://judolearn.com/techniques/harai-goshi-gaeshi/"},{"id":"harai-makikomi","name":"Harai-Makikomi","kanji":"払巻込","translation":"Sweeping Wraparound","category":"Nage-Waza","subcategory":"Yoko-Sutemi-Waza","difficulty":2,"grade":"3rd kyu","grades":["3rd kyu","2nd kyu"],"videos":[{"yt":"VBaHzKaCXss","title":"払巻込 /  Harai-makikomi","channel":"KODOKAN"},{"yt":"v0S1R0P5LzU","title":"HARAI MAKIKOMI Starting With Shoulder Control","channel":"welcomematstevescott"},{"yt":"mGRtrzMr2pQ","title":"How To Do a Harai Makikomi in Judo | Ronda's Dojo #51","channel":"Ronda Rousey"},{"yt":"iyRnLnxwGLw","title":"Harai-makikomi compilation","channel":"Ochiru"}],"overview":"Harai-Makikomi combines the sweeping leg action of Harai-Goshi with the arm wrap and side fall of makikomi throws. Tori enters for a Harai-Goshi-style sweep but wraps uke's arm and falls to the side rather than completing the throw standing. The wrapped arm and sacrifice fall make the throw more powerful and harder to counter than standard Harai-Goshi.","phases":[{"label":"Kuzushi · Off-balance","text":"Pull uke forward and upward onto their right front corner — identical kuzushi to Harai-Goshi. The sleeve grip elevates and pulls forward; the lapel side wraps and controls. Uke must be loaded forward onto their toes before the sweep and fall."},{"label":"Tsukuri · Entry","text":"Step in as for Harai-Goshi — right foot in front of uke's right foot, pivot to back-facing position. Simultaneously wrap your entry arm around uke's arm (makikomi), pulling it tight against your chest. The sweep leg is loaded back for the sweeping action."},{"label":"Kake · Execution","text":"Execute the Harai-Goshi sweep — sweeping the right leg into uke's thigh — while simultaneously wrapping the arm tight and falling to the side. The three elements (sweep, arm wrap, side fall) all converge at the same moment, creating a very powerful combination throw."}],"steps":[{"title":"Create forward kuzushi","text":"Pull uke forward onto their right front corner. Sleeve arm up and forward, lapel arm forward."},{"title":"Step in and pivot","text":"Step right foot in front of uke, pivot to back-facing position. Begin wrapping uke's arm with your entry arm."},{"title":"Wrap the arm (makikomi)","text":"Wrap your entry arm around uke's right arm, pulling it tightly against your chest. The arm is now controlled."},{"title":"Load the sweep","text":"Bring your sweeping leg back, coiling for the Harai-style sweep into uke's thigh."},{"title":"Sweep, wrap, and fall simultaneously","text":"Execute all three at once: sweep the leg into uke's thigh, maintain the arm wrap, and fall to the side. Uke is thrown powerfully to the mat."}],"principles":["The three elements — sweep, arm wrap, side fall — must converge simultaneously. Sequencing them loses the combined power.","The arm wrap traps uke's arm and prevents them from posting out or defending the sweep with that arm.","Harai-Makikomi is particularly effective when Harai-Goshi is being countered — the addition of the sacrifice overcomes the counter.","As a makikomi technique, ensure uke has good ukemi before drilling at full speed."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Executing as standing Harai-Goshi then adding the fall","fix":"The fall must be part of the execution, not an afterthought. The commitment to fall must begin with the sweep."},{"mistake":"Loose arm wrap","fix":"Wrap uke's arm tightly against your chest. A loose wrap allows uke to pull their arm free and defend."},{"mistake":"Falling backward instead of to the side","fix":"Harai-Makikomi falls to the side (yoko-sutemi). Falling backward changes the throw mechanics entirely."},{"mistake":"Sweep without sufficient forward kuzushi","fix":"The sweep requires uke to be loaded forward. Without kuzushi, the sweep meets resistance and the fall lacks projection force."}],"when":"Harai-Makikomi is used when standard Harai-Goshi is being countered. Adding the wrap and fall commits to the throw in a way that is harder to escape. It is also used by practitioners who favor a flowing, falling throwing style.","setups":["o-uchi-gari","ko-uchi-gari"],"followUps":[],"counters":[],"related":["harai-goshi","uchi-mata-makikomi","hane-makikomi","o-soto-makikomi"],"source":"https://judolearn.com/techniques/harai-makikomi/"},{"id":"harai-tsurikomi-ashi","name":"Harai-Tsurikomi-Ashi","kanji":"払釣込足","translation":"Lift-Pull Foot Sweep","category":"Nage-Waza","subcategory":"Ashi-Waza","difficulty":2,"grade":"4th kyu","grades":["4th kyu","3rd kyu","2nd kyu"],"videos":[{"yt":"gGPXvWL8VbE","title":"払釣込足 / Harai-tsurikomi-ashi","channel":"KODOKAN"},{"yt":"W-9mLRdyRos","title":"Harai-tsurikomi-ashi Tips | Riki Judo Dojo","channel":"Riki Judo Dojo"},{"yt":"4yyo2AVuytY","title":"Harai Tsurikomi Ashi || Lift-Pull Foot Sweep 2.0","channel":"The Judo Way of Life"},{"yt":"HE5lEukhhrU","title":"Travis Stevens Judo Breakdown: Harai-Tsurikomi-Ashi from A Russian 100kg Judoka","channel":"Travis Stevens"}],"overview":"Harai-Tsurikomi-Ashi is a lifting foot sweep in which tori steps across uke's front and sweeps the near leg while simultaneously lifting uke upward with both arms. The name describes the action: \"harai\" (sweep/reap), \"tsuri\" (lift), \"komi\" (pull), \"ashi\" (foot/leg). The combined lifting pull and sweeping action removes uke's support and sends them forward to the mat. It differs from simpler foot sweeps by incorporating the distinctive upward arm lift that raises uke onto their toes before the sweep.","phases":[{"label":"Kuzushi · Off-balance","text":"Step diagonally forward and to the right, pulling uke's sleeve arm forward and upward while the lapel arm also lifts upward. The lifting action should bring uke onto their toes and forward, breaking their balance to their front. The arms act in a \"tsuri\" (fishing/lifting) motion — pulling upward as if lifting a fish from water."},{"label":"Tsukuri · Entry","text":"Step your left foot across and in front of uke, positioning yourself diagonally. Your weight transfers to the left leg as you set up the sweep position. Your sweeping foot (right) prepares to arc across toward uke's right ankle or lower shin. Maintain the lifting tension on both arms."},{"label":"Kake · Execution","text":"As uke's right foot begins to shift forward under the lifting pressure, sweep your right foot in a wide, flat arc against uke's right ankle or lower shin, pushing it across and away. Simultaneously continue the upward pull with both arms, completing the lift-and-sweep combination that rotates uke forward to the mat."}],"steps":[{"title":"Apply the lifting kuzushi","text":"Pull uke's sleeve arm upward and forward while the lapel arm lifts as well. Draw uke forward onto their toes in a lifting \"fishing\" motion."},{"title":"Step diagonally across uke's front","text":"Move your left foot diagonally forward and across uke's centre line, transferring your weight to the left leg and positioning your body to the side."},{"title":"Align the sweeping foot","text":"Position your right foot to sweep from outside to inside across uke's ankle. The foot should be at ankle height, toes pointing slightly downward for a flat sweep."},{"title":"Sweep and lift simultaneously","text":"Drive the sweeping foot across uke's ankle while maintaining or increasing the upward arm lift. The sweep and lift happen together, not in sequence."},{"title":"Direct uke's fall forward","text":"Continue pulling the sleeve arm forward and down while the lapel arm guides uke's fall. Uke is projected forward and slightly to the side to the mat."}],"principles":["The lifting arm action (tsuri-komi) is what distinguishes this from a standard foot sweep — both arms actively lift before and during the sweep.","The sweep must be flat and horizontal at ankle level — a rising sweep deflects off the shin and misses.","Uke must be on their toes (kuzushi established) before the sweep is initiated; sweeping a flat-footed uke is ineffective.","Stepping diagonally positions your body to sweep across uke's foot rather than into it."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Pulling arms forward instead of upward","fix":"The \"tsuri\" (lift) component means the arms pull upward. A purely forward pull creates a different kuzushi that reduces the effectiveness of the sweep."},{"mistake":"Sweeping before kuzushi is established","fix":"Uke must be lifted onto their toes before the sweep begins. An early sweep against a flat-footed uke creates resistance."},{"mistake":"Sweeping into uke's ankle instead of across it","fix":"The sweep travels across the front of uke's ankle from outside to inside. Sweeping directly into the ankle stubs the foot rather than removing the leg."},{"mistake":"Stepping too close to uke before the sweep","fix":"You need room for the sweeping leg to arc. Too close and the sweep has no space to generate force."}],"when":"Harai-Tsurikomi-Ashi works particularly well when uke is taller than tori, as the lifting arm action makes use of the height differential. It is effective when uke steps forward predictably, and is often set up by drawing uke forward with a retreat. The technique is a strong option against opponents with good balance who resist simple foot sweeps.","setups":[],"followUps":["hiza-guruma","o-goshi"],"counters":["osoto-gari","o-uchi-gari"],"related":["de-ashi-barai","hiza-guruma","okuri-ashi-barai"],"source":"https://judolearn.com/techniques/harai-tsurikomi-ashi/"},{"id":"hikikomi-gaeshi","name":"Hikikomi-Gaeshi","kanji":"引込返","translation":"Pulling-Down Reversal","category":"Nage-Waza","subcategory":"Ma-Sutemi-Waza","difficulty":3,"grade":"2nd kyu","grades":["2nd kyu","1st kyu","1st dan"],"videos":[{"yt":"92zUYWBp5N8","title":"引込返 / Hikikomi-gaeshi","channel":"KODOKAN"},{"yt":"FWIvqzmd6vM","title":"HIKIKOMI GAESHI SUMI GAESHI OBI TORI GAESHI DIFFERENCES","channel":"welcomematstevescott"},{"yt":"CrRGfeKQQ4k","title":"Hikikomi-Gaeshi Compilation","channel":"Ochiru"}],"overview":"Hikikomi-Gaeshi is an advanced sacrifice throw where tori pulls uke down on top of themselves and rolls, reversing their relative positions so that uke ends up on the bottom. It is classified as ma-sutemi-waza because tori falls straight backward. The technique requires a strong pulling action and good timing — it is often used as a counter or when uke has a very strong defensive grip pulling backward.","phases":[{"label":"Kuzushi · Off-balance","text":"Tori pulls uke strongly forward and downward, loading uke's weight onto tori. Gripping the sleeve and lapel, tori pulls uke down in front of them, loading uke's weight onto tori's chest. The kuzushi draws uke's center of mass forward and over tori."},{"label":"Tsukuri · Entry","text":"Step or sit in directly in front of uke, placing one foot or both feet against uke's body (typically at the hip or thigh level) while gripping uke tightly. The position is immediately in front of uke with both parties in close contact and tori beginning to fall backward."},{"label":"Kake · Execution","text":"Fall straight backward (the sacrifice) while pulling uke forward with the grips. As tori falls back, the foot(s) placed at uke's hip or thigh area push upward, combining with the backward roll to flip uke over tori. Tori rolls through to end up on top or in a pin position."}],"steps":[{"title":"Pull uke strongly forward and down","text":"With strong grips on sleeve and lapel, pull uke forward and downward, loading their weight onto you."},{"title":"Drop in front of uke","text":"Drop to the ground directly in front of uke, falling backward. Maintain the pulling grips throughout."},{"title":"Place foot at uke's hip or thigh","text":"As you drop, place one or both feet against uke's hip or thigh area. These feet will act as a lever in the throw."},{"title":"Pull and push simultaneously — sacrifice","text":"Pull uke forward with the grips while pushing upward with the feet at uke's hips. Tori rolls backward; uke is thrown forward and over."},{"title":"Roll through to control position","text":"Continue rolling through the movement to come out on top of uke in a control or hold-down position."}],"principles":["The sacrifice is total — tori falls backward completely and relies on the mechanics of the roll to reverse positions.","Timing the foot placement at uke's hips with the backward fall is the critical coordination challenge.","Hikikomi-Gaeshi must be committed fully. A half-hearted fall leaves tori exposed on the ground without completing the throw.","Follow-through to a control position is essential — the throw is incomplete if tori does not come out in a dominant position."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Not falling far enough backward — stopping halfway","fix":"The sacrifice must be complete. Fall fully backward. Stopping halfway strands you under uke without completing the throw."},{"mistake":"Losing grip during the fall","fix":"Maintain both grips throughout the fall and roll. The grips control the direction of uke's throw."},{"mistake":"Foot placement too high or too low on uke","fix":"Feet should be at hip or thigh level — the fulcrum point for flipping uke. Too high or too low reduces the lever effect."},{"mistake":"Not rolling through after the throw","fix":"Roll through completely to reach a dominant position. A stalled roll leaves both parties in an unclear position."}],"when":"Hikikomi-Gaeshi is used when uke has a strong backward grip pulling tori toward them, or as a counter when uke is resisting with a strong forward posture. It is also used when an exchange of throwing attempts leads to a clinch and tori chooses to sacrifice. Advanced practitioners use it when both competitors are pulling strongly in the same direction.","setups":["o-uchi-gari"],"followUps":["kesa-gatame"],"counters":[],"related":["sumi-gaeshi","tawara-gaeshi","tomoe-nage"],"source":"https://judolearn.com/techniques/hikikomi-gaeshi/"},{"id":"hiza-gatame","name":"Hiza-Gatame","kanji":"膝固","translation":"Knee Armlock","category":"Katame-Waza","subcategory":"Kansetsu-Waza","difficulty":2,"grade":"3rd kyu","grades":["3rd kyu","2nd kyu","1st kyu"],"videos":[{"yt":"H2HtAJdiJcE","title":"腕挫膝固 /  Ude-hishigi-hiza-gatame","channel":"KODOKAN"},{"yt":"R9aHRdTIRfU","title":"Hiza Gatame Takedown for Judo/BJJ","channel":"Sampson Judo"},{"yt":"kCkwAegEOK4","title":"Judo Básico | Hiza Gatame | Basic Demonstration","channel":"Sensei Jose Eudes Monteiro"}],"overview":"Hiza-Gatame is a standing or kneeling armlock in which tori uses their knee to apply hyperextension pressure to uke's elbow joint. The technique is applied while uke is on the ground — tori controls uke's wrist and places their knee against uke's elbow, then straightens or levers the arm over the knee joint. It is unique among armlocks for its application while tori remains partially standing or kneeling, making it a natural transition from throwing positions.","phases":[{"label":"Kuzushi · Off-balance","text":"Control uke's wrist firmly with both hands, pulling their arm straight and away from their body. Uke must not be able to bend their elbow or rotate their wrist. The arm must be extended in a straight line with the elbow accessible."},{"label":"Tsukuri · Entry","text":"From a standing or kneeling position over uke, place your knee on the ground directly against the back of uke's elbow joint. Your knee acts as the fulcrum. Maintain wrist control with your hands, keeping uke's arm straight and the palm facing upward (supine position) to expose the elbow to hyperextension."},{"label":"Kake · Execution","text":"With the knee positioned behind uke's elbow, apply downward pressure through the knee while lifting uke's wrist upward and toward your body. This levers the elbow over the knee fulcrum, applying hyperextension pressure to the joint. Uke taps when the pressure is felt."}],"steps":[{"title":"Secure uke's wrist","text":"Grip uke's wrist firmly with both hands — one on the wrist, one on the forearm. Pull the arm straight and away from uke's body."},{"title":"Position uke's arm palm-up","text":"Rotate uke's arm so the palm faces upward (supine position). This orients the elbow for hyperextension rather than a natural bend."},{"title":"Place the knee at the elbow","text":"Lower your knee to the ground directly against the back of uke's elbow joint. Your knee is the fulcrum of the lever."},{"title":"Confirm the arm is straight","text":"Ensure uke's arm is fully extended with the elbow resting against your knee. A bent elbow has range of motion that delays the lock."},{"title":"Apply lever pressure","text":"Press your knee downward against uke's elbow while lifting uke's wrist upward toward your body. The lever action hyperextends the elbow. Uke taps or the lock is maintained."}],"principles":["The knee must be precisely at the elbow joint — not above it (upper arm) or below it (forearm).","Palm must face upward (arm supinated) to expose the elbow to hyperextension in the natural anatomical direction.","Wrist control must be tight and with two hands — a loose single-hand grip allows uke to rotate their arm to escape.","Apply pressure gradually and with control — this is a joint lock and excessive speed can cause injury before uke can tap."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Knee positioned on the forearm or upper arm instead of the elbow","fix":"The fulcrum must be precisely at the elbow joint. Misplacement means the lever misses the joint and the lock fails."},{"mistake":"Arm pronated (palm down) instead of supinated","fix":"Palm down orients the elbow's natural bend direction toward the lock, which reduces the hyperextension angle and allows uke to flex against you. Rotate the arm palm-up."},{"mistake":"Single-hand wrist grip","fix":"Use two hands on uke's wrist and forearm to prevent rotation. A single-hand grip allows uke to twist their arm out of the lock."},{"mistake":"Applying pressure too quickly","fix":"Joint locks must be applied with control. Fast application prevents uke from tapping in time and risks injury."}],"when":"Hiza-Gatame is most commonly applied in the transition from tachi-waza (standing) to ne-waza (ground work) — when uke lands and their arm is exposed. It is effective when uke tries to post on an outstretched arm while being thrown, or when uke pushes up with a straight arm from the bottom. The standing/kneeling position makes it a fast application technique.","setups":[],"followUps":["juji-gatame","kesa-gatame"],"counters":[],"related":["juji-gatame","ude-gatame","ude-garami"],"source":"https://judolearn.com/techniques/hiza-gatame/"},{"id":"hiza-guruma","name":"Hiza-Guruma","kanji":"膝車","translation":"Knee Wheel","category":"Nage-Waza","subcategory":"Ashi-Waza","difficulty":1,"grade":"6th kyu","grades":["6th kyu","5th kyu","4th kyu"],"videos":[{"yt":"JPJx9-oAVns","title":"膝車 / Hiza-guruma","channel":"KODOKAN"},{"yt":"tur645Tp3pA","title":"hiza guruma","channel":"Shintaro Higashi"},{"yt":"JzsBZ6mrEzk","title":"Hiza-Guruma Tips | Riki Judo Dojo","channel":"Riki Judo Dojo"},{"yt":"hK5pek8M4lo","title":"Hiza-Guruma alternative to Sasae","channel":"Shintaro Higashi"},{"yt":"TfWBWqGp7cY","title":"Hiza-Guruma compilation","channel":"Ochiru"}],"overview":"Hiza-Guruma is one of the first foot and leg techniques taught in judo. Tori places the sole of their foot against uke's knee and uses a coordinated arm pull to wheel uke over that point. The knee acts as the fulcrum of a wheel — uke's body rotates around it and falls to the mat. It is a foundational ashi-waza technique that teaches the essential principle of blocking a moving limb and redirecting uke's momentum.","phases":[{"label":"Kuzushi · Off-balance","text":"Step back on your left foot to draw uke forward into motion. Pull uke's sleeve arm sharply forward and downward, while the lapel arm draws upward. Uke's weight shifts to their right front corner as they step forward to maintain balance."},{"label":"Tsukuri · Entry","text":"As uke steps forward with their right foot, place the sole of your left foot against the outside of uke's right knee — the contact point is the sole of the foot on the knee joint, not the shin or ankle. Your foot should be placed as uke's foot is lifting, not when it is planted. Maintain your balance on the right leg."},{"label":"Kake · Execution","text":"As uke's right foot is in the air, press your left foot firmly against their knee to block it from advancing. Simultaneously pull uke's sleeve arm in a large circular arc downward and to your left, while the lapel arm pushes upward and to your right. The combined arm rotation and knee block wheels uke forward and to the mat."}],"steps":[{"title":"Initiate movement — step back","text":"Step backward with your left foot to create motion, drawing uke forward. Your arms pull to encourage uke's stepping momentum."},{"title":"Identify and time uke's step","text":"Watch for uke's right foot to lift off the mat as they step forward. The moment the foot lifts is your entry window."},{"title":"Place the sole of the foot on the knee","text":"Lift your left leg and place the sole (not the toe, not the shin) against the outside of uke's right knee. Your leg is slightly bent, not locked straight."},{"title":"Coordinate arm action with foot placement","text":"As your foot contacts uke's knee, pull the sleeve arm in a large downward arc to your left while the lapel arm lifts and rotates to your right."},{"title":"Complete the wheel","text":"Maintain foot pressure on the knee as uke's body rotates over it. Follow through with the arm action, staying balanced on your right leg, until uke lands on the mat."}],"principles":["Timing is everything — the foot must contact the knee when it is in the air, not when uke's weight is on it.","Use the sole of the foot, not the shin or ankle, for safe and effective knee contact.","The arm action (sleeve pull, lapel push) creates the rotation; the foot merely blocks the wheel's axle.","Step backward first to generate uke's forward motion — a static uke cannot be wheeled easily."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Placing the foot when uke's weight is on the knee","fix":"Contact must happen when uke's foot is off the ground. Pressing against a loaded knee is ineffective and potentially injurious."},{"mistake":"Using the toe or shin instead of the sole","fix":"The sole provides a broad, safe contact surface. Toes can buckle; the shin can slide off the knee."},{"mistake":"Arm action is too small or too slow","fix":"The sleeve arm should pull in a large, sweeping arc. Small pulls don't generate enough rotational force to wheel uke over."},{"mistake":"Losing balance on the standing leg","fix":"Keep your right knee slightly bent and your centre of gravity over your right foot. Leaning too far forward causes you to fall as well."}],"when":"Hiza-Guruma is most effective when uke is moving forward and is taller than tori, allowing easy access to the knee. It works well when uke steps in with a deliberate stride. In randori, it can be initiated by stepping back to invite uke's advance. It is also effective as a counter to uke's stepping entry for a throw.","setups":[],"followUps":["o-goshi","osoto-gari"],"counters":["ko-uchi-gari","o-uchi-gari"],"related":["de-ashi-barai","okuri-ashi-barai","harai-tsurikomi-ashi"],"source":"https://judolearn.com/techniques/hiza-guruma/"},{"id":"juji-gatame","name":"Juji-Gatame","kanji":"十字固","translation":"Cross Armlock","category":"Katame-Waza","subcategory":"Kansetsu-Waza","difficulty":2,"grade":"3rd kyu","grades":["3rd kyu","2nd kyu"],"videos":[{"yt":"OWgSOlCuMXw","title":"腕挫十字固 /  Ude-hishigi-juji-gatame","channel":"KODOKAN"},{"yt":"_kIBFNdAydU","title":"5 juji gatame attacks you should be drilling by Matt D’Aquino","channel":"BeyondGrappling"},{"yt":"6HYXcqEv410","title":"Top 10 Juji-gatame Compilation Highlights (arm lock/armbar) | 腕挫十字固","channel":"Judo Spirit"}],"overview":"Juji-Gatame is the most well-known joint lock in judo and the foundational armlock of both judo and Brazilian Jiu-Jitsu. Tori hyperextends uke's elbow by placing uke's straightened arm across their own hips while pulling the wrist toward the mat. It can be applied from multiple positions — mount, side control, north-south. In IJF competition judo, guard-based applications are restricted; the most common entries are from side control, mount, and ura-kesa positions.","phases":[],"steps":[{"title":"Isolate and control uke's arm","text":"Isolate uke's right arm so that it is straight and aligned perpendicular to their body. Control it with both your hands gripping near the wrist."},{"title":"Sit perpendicular to uke at their right shoulder","text":"Position yourself perpendicular to uke, with their arm between your thighs. Your right thigh is across their neck/shoulder; your left thigh is across their torso. Your right thigh pressing across uke's neck/shoulder prevents them from rolling into you to escape."},{"title":"Clamp uke's arm between your thighs","text":"Squeeze your thighs firmly around uke's arm to control it. Their elbow should be between your thighs pointing upward. The thumb of their controlled arm should point upward."},{"title":"Hold uke's wrist against your chest","text":"Pull uke's wrist to your chest with both hands. Their arm should be straight — not bent at the elbow."},{"title":"Apply pressure by thrusting your hips upward","text":"Drive your hips upward (bridge) while pulling the wrist downward toward the mat. This creates a hyperextension force on uke's elbow joint. Apply slowly and release immediately when uke taps."}],"principles":["The elbow must point upward (thumb up on the trapped hand) — rotating the arm 90 degrees before applying pressure eliminates the elbow's natural range.","Squeeze the thighs together firmly — any gap allows uke to pull their arm free.","The hip thrust (bridge) is the primary force — pulling arms alone is less effective.","Control the shoulder by placing your right thigh across it — this prevents uke from rolling into you.","Apply force incrementally — Juji-Gatame is an immediate tap-or-break situation and must be controlled."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Arm rotated incorrectly — thumb pointing down","fix":"Rotate uke's arm so their thumb points upward before extending. Thumb-down presents the bent elbow face, not the hyperextension face."},{"mistake":"Gap between thighs — arm escapes","fix":"Squeeze your thighs together. Practice \"pinching\" with your knees throughout the lock."},{"mistake":"Pulling the arm with hands only, no hip thrust","fix":"Bridge your hips upward while pulling the wrist down. The hip power is 10x the arm power."},{"mistake":"Applying too quickly — risk of injury","fix":"Juji-Gatame must be applied with progressive pressure, giving uke time to tap. A fast, sudden application is a joint injury risk."}],"when":"Juji-Gatame can be applied from mount, side control, north-south, or when uke leaves an arm exposed during ground transitions. In competition, the classic path is: secure side control or mount → isolate the near arm → roll into Juji-Gatame position → apply. Also common as a counter when uke reaches for a grip and overextends their arm.","setups":[],"followUps":["yoko-shiho-gatame","kami-shiho-gatame","okuri-eri-jime"],"counters":[],"related":["ude-garami","yoko-shiho-gatame","kami-shiho-gatame","okuri-eri-jime"],"source":"https://judolearn.com/techniques/juji-gatame/"},{"id":"kami-shiho-gatame","name":"Kami-Shiho-Gatame","kanji":"上四方固","translation":"Upper Four-Corner Hold","category":"Katame-Waza","subcategory":"Osaekomi-Waza","difficulty":2,"grade":"4th kyu","grades":["4th kyu","3rd kyu"],"videos":[{"yt":"HFuMjOv0WN8","title":"上四方固 /  Kami-shiho-gatame","channel":"KODOKAN"},{"yt":"seGsXy9I4G8","title":"上四方固の逃れ方 /  Kami-siho-gatame Escapes","channel":"KODOKAN"},{"yt":"pvu4nhEHqkM","title":"Kami-Shiho-Gatame Tips | Riki Judo Dojo","channel":"Riki Judo Dojo"}],"overview":"Kami-Shiho-Gatame controls uke from above their head. Tori lies chest-to-chest with uke in the opposite direction, reaching both arms down to grip uke's belt at the hips. The position pins both of uke's shoulders simultaneously with body weight from directly above, making it one of the most powerful hold-downs against a strong uke.","phases":[],"steps":[{"title":"Approach uke from above their head","text":"Position yourself directly above uke's head, with your own head pointing toward uke's feet and your chest facing downward toward uke's chest."},{"title":"Reach both arms under uke's shoulders","text":"Slide both arms under uke's shoulders, reaching through to grip their belt at the hip area. Alternatively, grip the mat alongside uke's body."},{"title":"Drive your chest and abdomen onto uke's chest","text":"Lower your chest and abdomen onto uke's chest. Your sternum presses on uke's upper sternum; your abdomen covers uke's face. Both contact points add pressure and reduce uke's vision and breathing space."},{"title":"Extend your legs for a wide, stable base","text":"Extend both legs wide behind you, toes on the mat. The wider your base, the harder to roll. Avoid having your knees up."},{"title":"Squeeze your elbows inward","text":"As you hold, squeeze your elbows in against uke's sides to prevent them from creating space. Keep your body heavy and connected."}],"principles":["The upper approach pins both shoulders simultaneously — this is what distinguishes it from side holds.","Weight distribution from above is particularly powerful against uke's bridge attempts.","Arm reach should be to belt level — too short (just under the shoulder blades) reduces control of uke's lower body.","Wide leg base: the further your feet are apart, the more stable and heavy you are."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Body position too upright — not enough chest contact","fix":"Drive your full chest weight onto uke. A lifted chest greatly reduces control."},{"mistake":"Arms reaching too shallow (not to belt)","fix":"Reach through deeply. Shallow arm position doesn't control uke's lower body and they can bridge."},{"mistake":"Legs too close together — high center of gravity","fix":"Spread legs wide and extend them straight. Bent knees behind you raise your hips and make you easy to roll."}],"when":"Kami-Shiho-Gatame is the natural progression when you are positioned above uke's head — often after transitioning from a north-south position, or when uke rolls onto their back from a standing throw. It is particularly powerful when uke has a strong bridge defense against side holds.","setups":[],"followUps":["juji-gatame","ude-garami"],"counters":[],"related":["yoko-shiho-gatame","kesa-gatame","juji-gatame"],"source":"https://judolearn.com/techniques/kami-shiho-gatame/"},{"id":"kata-gatame","name":"Kata-Gatame","kanji":"肩固","translation":"Shoulder Hold","category":"Katame-Waza","subcategory":"Osaekomi-Waza","difficulty":2,"grade":"4th kyu","grades":["4th kyu","3rd kyu","2nd kyu"],"videos":[{"yt":"zQR3IOXxO_Q","title":"肩固 /  Kata-gatame","channel":"KODOKAN"},{"yt":"jEBDlaua3ms","title":"How to do Kata-Gatame | Shoulder Hold | 肩固め | Judo and BJJ","channel":"Sampson Judo"},{"yt":"5v7fI9FcToQ","title":"Kata-Gatame Tips | Riki Judo Dojo","channel":"Riki Judo Dojo"},{"yt":"7hP1-W2yovk","title":"肩固の逃れ方 /  Kata-gatame Escapes","channel":"KODOKAN"},{"yt":"XnDCVXZXPCA","title":"judo kata-gatame turnover compilation","channel":"Ochiru"}],"overview":"Kata-Gatame is a pin in which tori traps uke's arm and head simultaneously — uke's own arm is used against them as a compression element in the hold. Tori positions themselves beside uke and traps uke's near arm between their own head and chest, while wrapping the other arm around uke's head. The trapped arm creates pressure on uke's neck, making Kata-Gatame function simultaneously as a hold-down and a potential arm-against-neck choke (if the arm presses into the carotid region).","phases":[{"label":"Kuzushi · Off-balance","text":"Control uke's near arm by pulling it across their own body so that it lies between your head and their neck. The arm must be pulled high — across the throat level. Prevent uke from withdrawing the arm by clamping it with your head and shoulder."},{"label":"Tsukuri · Entry","text":"Lie on your side beside uke, pressing your body against theirs. Grip uke's head with your arm wrapped around it from the other side. Simultaneously press uke's near arm across their own neck with your head and shoulder — clamp your head against uke's forearm/wrist, trapping the arm."},{"label":"Kake · Execution","text":"Clasp your hands together or grip deeply to lock the hold. Press your head down onto uke's trapped arm, pushing the arm into uke's neck. Your body weight presses against uke's upper body. Uke is held flat by the combined pressure of trapped arm and head control."}],"steps":[{"title":"Gain side control beside uke","text":"Position yourself on uke's right side, pressing your body against their upper chest and shoulder region."},{"title":"Pull uke's right arm across their body","text":"Grip uke's right wrist or sleeve and pull their arm across their own body so the forearm lies across their throat region."},{"title":"Trap the arm with your head","text":"Press your head down onto uke's trapped forearm. Your chin or temple pins uke's arm against their own neck."},{"title":"Wrap the other arm around uke's head","text":"Reach your right arm under uke's head and around to meet your left arm. Clasp your hands together to lock the hold."},{"title":"Apply pressure and maintain","text":"Drive your head down, pressing uke's arm into their neck. Keep your body weight low and pressed against uke. Hold for the required time."}],"principles":["Uke's own arm does a significant portion of the work — the higher it is pulled across their neck, the more choking pressure it creates.","Your head must be pressed firmly down onto uke's arm — lifting your head releases the arm trap.","Clasping hands creates a rigid frame that locks uke's head and arm together.","Keep body weight low and pressed against uke's shoulder — a high body position reduces the hold's effectiveness."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Arm trapped too low (across the chest, not the neck)","fix":"Pull uke's arm high — the forearm should be across the throat/neck area. Too low creates a chest hold rather than a neck-pressure hold."},{"mistake":"Head not pressing on the trapped arm","fix":"Your head must actively press down on uke's arm. Lift your head slightly and the arm escape is easy."},{"mistake":"Body positioned too far from uke","fix":"Press your chest and shoulder firmly against uke's shoulder. Space between bodies allows uke to create leverage to escape."},{"mistake":"Hands not clasped or gripped","fix":"Without clasped hands, the hold lacks rigidity. Clasp your hands or grip uke's collar/body on the far side to close the frame."}],"when":"Kata-Gatame occurs naturally when uke places an arm up to push you away or block — the arm is captured and the hold applied immediately. It transitions well from Kesa-Gatame when uke tries to extract their arm. It is also a strong finishing position after a rolling armlock attempt is defended by uke pulling their arm back.","setups":[],"followUps":["kuzure-kesa-gatame","gyaku-juji-jime"],"counters":[],"related":["kesa-gatame","kuzure-kesa-gatame","tate-shiho-gatame"],"source":"https://judolearn.com/techniques/kata-gatame/"},{"id":"kata-guruma","name":"Kata-Guruma","kanji":"肩車","translation":"Shoulder Wheel","category":"Nage-Waza","subcategory":"Te-Waza","difficulty":3,"grade":"2nd kyu","grades":["2nd kyu","1st kyu","1st dan"],"videos":[{"yt":"cnHRhSy8yi4","title":"肩車 / Kata-guruma","channel":"KODOKAN"},{"yt":"3GpTJr-LEJY","title":"Kata-guruma - Technical Breakdown","channel":"Efficient Judo"},{"yt":"FTLyTJLHbWs","title":"Crazy Kata Guruma Variation to Add To Your Game","channel":"Shintaro Higashi"},{"yt":"OFncAxoTCnY","title":"Kata Guruma Judo Highlights 肩車　柔道ハイライト","channel":"Judo Highlights"}],"overview":"Kata-Guruma is a spectacular shoulder throw in which tori squats deeply, places uke across both shoulders, and then stands and rotates to wheel uke off to one side. Often called the \"fireman's carry\" of judo, it requires tori to drop very low under uke's centre of gravity, loading their entire body weight across the shoulders. It is a high-difficulty technique due to the deep squat entry and the vulnerability it creates during execution. Modern competition versions often use a modified grip that avoids the full shoulder-load position.","phases":[{"label":"Kuzushi · Off-balance","text":"Pull uke's sleeve arm strongly forward and downward while driving your body low and forward under uke's centre of gravity. The kuzushi is downward and forward — you are not pulling uke to a corner but drawing their weight forward and down onto you as you drop beneath them."},{"label":"Tsukuri · Entry","text":"Drop into a deep squat directly in front of uke, driving your right shoulder into uke's lower abdomen or hip region. Simultaneously reach your right arm to grip the back of uke's right thigh (classical form: between the legs; IJF competition form: from the outside of the leg). Your left arm continues holding uke's sleeve, pulling it downward. Your shoulder becomes the loading platform."},{"label":"Kake · Execution","text":"With uke loaded across your shoulders — right shoulder supporting their abdomen, right arm gripping their thigh, left arm controlling their sleeve — stand up and simultaneously rotate your body. As you rise, the rotation and the standing motion wheel uke over your shoulder and to the mat beside you."}],"steps":[{"title":"Break balance downward and forward","text":"Pull uke's sleeve strongly forward and downward. Begin to drop your own level rapidly, drawing uke's weight over and forward."},{"title":"Drop into a deep squat under uke","text":"Squat deeply, lowering your hips until your right shoulder is at uke's hip level. Drive your right shoulder into uke's lower abdomen."},{"title":"Establish the loading grip","text":"In the classical form, reach your right arm between uke's legs to grip the back of their right thigh (note: this between-the-legs grip is prohibited in IJF competition — see competition variation below). Your left arm maintains the sleeve. Uke's weight begins to load across your shoulders."},{"title":"Complete the load","text":"Ensure uke's torso lies across your upper back and both shoulders. Their hips are above your right shoulder, legs above your left."},{"title":"Stand and wheel","text":"Drive upward with your legs while rotating your upper body. Uke wheels over your right shoulder and is projected to the mat."}],"principles":["The squat must be deep enough to get your shoulder under uke's centre of gravity — a shallow squat fails to load uke.","The thigh grip with the right arm is essential for controlling uke's lower body during the wheel.","The throwing power comes from the legs standing up — use your strongest muscles (quads, glutes) to generate the throw.","Speed of entry is critical; a slow drop gives uke time to sprawl and defend."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Insufficient depth of squat","fix":"You must get low enough that your shoulder is at uke's hip level. If you only partially squat, uke's weight is not loaded and the throw produces only a forward lean."},{"mistake":"Losing the thigh grip during the throw","fix":"The right arm must maintain contact with uke's thigh throughout. Without it, uke's lower body drops and the wheel motion is incomplete."},{"mistake":"Attempting the throw without committing to the drop","fix":"Kata-Guruma requires full commitment to the deep squat. A hesitant entry leaves you in a vulnerable crouched position that uke can exploit."},{"mistake":"Rotating without standing first","fix":"Stand up first to generate the lifting force, then rotate. Pure rotation without the leg drive produces a weak throw."}],"when":"Kata-Guruma is most effective against taller opponents — tori must drop deeply under uke's centre of gravity, which is more achievable when uke is taller. It is used as a surprise attack when uke commits hard to an upright defensive posture with their hips forward. In modern competition, it is often used via a modified grip when uke attempts a forward throw or drops to the ground.","setups":["ko-uchi-gari","seoi-nage"],"followUps":[],"counters":["tani-otoshi","ura-nage"],"related":["seoi-nage","tai-otoshi","sumi-otoshi"],"source":"https://judolearn.com/techniques/kata-guruma/"},{"id":"kata-juji-jime","name":"Kata-Juji-Jime","kanji":"片十字絞","translation":"Half Cross Choke","category":"Katame-Waza","subcategory":"Shime-Waza","difficulty":2,"grade":"3rd kyu","grades":["3rd kyu","2nd kyu","1st kyu"],"videos":[{"yt":"3VZVUAmiMD8","title":"片十字絞 /  Kata-juji-jime","channel":"KODOKAN"},{"yt":"Fhbd7qiGy_E","title":"Kata Juji Jime || Half Cross Lock","channel":"The Judo Way of Life"},{"yt":"dlXL9ShKJa4","title":"Juji Jime Against Turtled Opponent by Satoshi Ishii","channel":"JudoFanatics"},{"yt":"r0sFWrIANmM","title":"KATA JUJI JIME LOOP VARIATION","channel":"welcomematstevescott"},{"yt":"wphA_2ITYOI","title":"Easy Juji Gatame from Gyaku Juji Jime","channel":"Sampson Judo"}],"overview":"Kata-Juji-Jime is a cross-collar choke that uses a mixed grip — one hand with the thumb inside the collar and one hand with the fingers inside the collar. \"Kata\" means \"half\" or \"one-sided,\" referring to this hybrid grip orientation. One arm pulls the collar edge into the carotid on one side while the other pushes the collar edge from the other side, creating a scissors-like compression. Kata-Juji-Jime is considered a middle-ground technique between Nami-Juji-Jime and Gyaku-Juji-Jime, combining elements of both.","phases":[{"label":"Kuzushi · Off-balance","text":"From a top or mounted position, maintain chest contact with uke and keep their upper body controlled. Open the collar with the thumb-grip hand first to establish the deeper grip. Uke's head should be relatively flat on the mat to allow clear collar access."},{"label":"Tsukuri · Entry","text":"Insert your right thumb inside uke's left collar, pulling it toward the side of uke's neck (as in Gyaku-Juji-Jime). Then insert your left hand with fingers inside uke's right collar, thumb outside (as in Nami-Juji-Jime). Your arms cross at the wrists — the thumb-in arm goes deeper, the fingers-in arm crosses over."},{"label":"Kake · Execution","text":"With the mixed grip established, apply the choke by pulling the thumb-in hand backward (driving collar edge into carotid) while pushing the fingers-in hand forward (scissors action). The dual action compresses both sides of the neck. Drive your chest forward to add body weight to the compression."}],"steps":[{"title":"Secure top position and access collar","text":"Establish a top position (mount or modified side control) and widen uke's collar if necessary."},{"title":"Insert thumb-in grip (deep hand)","text":"Insert your right thumb inside uke's left collar as deep as possible, directing it toward the back of the neck. This is the thumb-in grip."},{"title":"Insert fingers-in grip (near hand)","text":"Cross your left arm over your right and insert fingers inside uke's right collar. This is the fingers-in grip — fingers inside, thumb outside the collar."},{"title":"Apply scissors compression","text":"Pull the thumb-in arm backward while pushing the fingers-in arm forward. The scissors action drives both collar edges into uke's carotid arteries."},{"title":"Add chest pressure","text":"Lower your chest onto uke and maintain the pulling-pushing scissors action. Sustain until submission."}],"principles":["The mixed grip (one thumb-in, one fingers-in) creates a scissors action — one hand pulls while the other pushes.","The thumb-in hand goes deeper and should be placed first for maximum collar depth.","The scissors mechanism compresses both carotids simultaneously, making this an efficient bilateral choke.","Chest contact amplifies the choking force — a purely arm-based choke is weaker."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Both hands using the same grip orientation","fix":"Kata-Juji-Jime requires one thumb-in and one fingers-in grip. If both are the same, it becomes Gyaku or Nami, losing the scissors advantage."},{"mistake":"Shallow collar grips on either hand","fix":"Both grips must penetrate deep into the collar. Shallow grips rest on the front of the neck (trachea) rather than the sides (carotids)."},{"mistake":"Pulling both hands in the same direction","fix":"The scissors action requires one hand to pull and one to push in opposite directions. Same-direction pulling reduces bilateral compression."},{"mistake":"No body weight applied","fix":"Body weight is a multiplier. Drive your chest toward uke to add body pressure to the choke mechanics."}],"when":"Kata-Juji-Jime is particularly useful when uke's collar positioning makes a full Gyaku-Juji-Jime difficult — the hybrid grip provides flexibility. It works from mount, side control, and as a transition from other collar chokes when uke shifts their defence. It can also be applied when moving from guard to top position as uke tries to establish grips.","setups":[],"followUps":["gyaku-juji-jime","juji-gatame"],"counters":[],"related":["nami-juji-jime","gyaku-juji-jime","okuri-eri-jime"],"source":"https://judolearn.com/techniques/kata-juji-jime/"},{"id":"kata-te-jime","name":"Kata-Te-Jime","kanji":"片手絞","translation":"One-Hand Strangle","category":"Katame-Waza","subcategory":"Shime-Waza","difficulty":2,"grade":"3rd kyu","grades":["3rd kyu","2nd kyu"],"videos":[{"yt":"cHeIs-fSqwE","title":"片手絞 /  Katate-jime","channel":"KODOKAN"},{"yt":"qXzPnMYecyU","title":"Katate-Jime Tips & Variations | Riki Judo Dojo","channel":"Riki Judo Dojo"},{"yt":"-vLJdh4TJwU","title":"KATATE JIME SINGLE HAND STRANGLE","channel":"welcomematstevescott"}],"overview":"Kata-Te-Jime is a one-handed collar choke in which a single hand reaches across uke's collar — gripping deep on the opposite lapel — to compress the carotid artery on one side of the neck. The free hand is used to control uke's posture by gripping the arm, head, or same-side lapel, and may also contribute pressure from the opposite side. Part of the official Kodokan shime-waza (strangulation technique) classification, Kata-Te-Jime is applied from positions of upper-body control such as kesa-gatame, yoko-shiho position, or the mount.","phases":[{"label":"Kuzushi · Off-balance","text":"In ne-waza shime-waza, the traditional kuzushi concept does not directly apply. Control is established by securing a dominant top position — typically a hold-down variant — before applying the strangle. Body weight and positional pressure replace kuzushi: tori's mass on top of uke prevents escape and forces the neck into the choke."},{"label":"Tsukuri · Entry","text":"From a controlling top position (kesa-gatame, side control, or mount), reach the choking hand across uke's throat to grip the far collar as deeply as possible, with the palm facing downward. The thumb may enter from the outside or inside of the collar depending on the grip variation used. Your body weight presses down onto uke throughout the grip establishment."},{"label":"Kake · Execution","text":"Once the cross-collar grip is secured, pull inward and rotate your wrist so the blade edge of the forearm or wrist compresses the near carotid artery against the side of uke's neck. Simultaneously use the free hand — gripping uke's arm, head, or same-side lapel — to prevent uke from bridging and to maintain chest-to-chest (or chest-to-back) body weight pressure. The strangle effect is carotid compression on the gripped side."}],"steps":[{"title":"Establish a dominant top position","text":"Secure a controlling ground position — kesa-gatame, yoko-shiho, or mount — before attempting the choke. An insecure position allows uke to escape before the choke develops."},{"title":"Reach the choking hand across uke's throat","text":"Reach your choking hand across uke's neck and grip the far collar as deeply as possible — four fingers inside the collar, palm down. The deeper the grip, the more effective the compression."},{"title":"Position the wrist against the near carotid","text":"Rotate the choking wrist so the blade of the forearm or wrist presses against the near side of uke's neck, loading the carotid artery."},{"title":"Use the free hand to control and add pressure","text":"Grip uke's arm, same-side collar, or place the free hand on uke's head to control posture and prevent bridging. Some variations use the free hand to compress the opposite carotid from the other side."},{"title":"Apply compression with inward pull and weight","text":"Pull the choking hand inward toward your own chest while pressing your body weight down. The combined inward pull, wrist rotation, and top-position weight create the carotid compression."}],"principles":["Depth of the collar grip is critical — a shallow grip compresses the windpipe (painful but slow); a deep grip compresses the carotid arteries (effective and rapid).","The choke works on the near-side carotid. The cross-collar grip pulls the collar fabric across the neck while the forearm or wrist edge presses from the near side.","Top-position body weight is a multiplier. The compression from one hand is amplified by tori's full weight pressing down.","Control the free hand actively — it prevents uke from turning into or bridging out of the choke while it is being set.","Do not rely on windpipe pressure. Kata-Te-Jime, like all Kodokan shime-waza, is effective through carotid artery compression."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Shallow collar grip compressing only the windpipe","fix":"Drive the gripping hand deeper into the collar until the knuckles contact the back of uke's neck. Shallow grips are painful but not efficient strangles."},{"mistake":"Losing top-position control while gripping","fix":"Establish and maintain the top hold before reaching for the collar. Attempting the grip from an unstable position allows uke to turn and escape."},{"mistake":"Free hand idle — uke bridges out of the choke","fix":"Use the free hand actively to control uke's arm or apply additional pressure. An idle free hand removes half the choke's security."},{"mistake":"Pulling the collar outward instead of inward","fix":"Pull the choking hand inward toward your chest — this drives the collar fabric deeper across the neck and increases carotid pressure."}],"when":"Kata-Te-Jime is most applicable when you control uke's upper body from a top position and uke's collar is accessible but a two-handed choke grip (such as Okuri-Eri-Jime) is not immediately available. It is also a useful transitional choke when uke is defending one arm from a choke attempt — the single-hand cross-collar grip can be established with one arm while the other continues to control.","setups":[],"followUps":["kesa-gatame","yoko-shiho-gatame"],"counters":[],"related":["hadaka-jime","okuri-eri-jime","kataha-jime"],"source":"https://judolearn.com/techniques/kata-te-jime/"},{"id":"kataha-jime","name":"Kataha-Jime","kanji":"片羽絞","translation":"Single Wing Strangle","category":"Katame-Waza","subcategory":"Shime-Waza","difficulty":2,"grade":"3rd kyu","grades":["3rd kyu","2nd kyu"],"videos":[{"yt":"yaTGgRjnwB8","title":"片羽絞 /  Kataha-jime","channel":"KODOKAN"},{"yt":"vKt19egNiWk","title":"How to do Kata Ha jime","channel":"Sampson Judo"},{"yt":"DAQKFfthkUU","title":"Rolling Kataha-jime vs Turtle Position","channel":"Takedowns For Jiu-jitsu"},{"yt":"MIV5RnhqCcQ","title":"Kataha-Jime Tips | Riki Judo Dojo","channel":"Riki Judo Dojo"}],"overview":"Kataha-Jime is a powerful strangle applied from behind uke that combines a choking arm around the neck with an arm that traps one of uke's arms up behind their back. The \"single wing\" (kataha) refers to the one arm of uke that is trapped — bent up behind their head like a bird's folded wing. This dual control of the neck and an arm makes it both a choke and an arm control technique.","phases":[],"steps":[{"title":"Establish rear control","text":"Get behind uke — on their back or in a rear control position with uke sitting or lying."},{"title":"Apply the choking arm","text":"Slide one arm under uke's chin and around their neck. The forearm or upper arm contacts the carotid arteries at the sides of the neck."},{"title":"Trap uke's near arm","text":"With your free hand, reach over uke's shoulder and grab their near arm (the arm on the same side as your choking arm). Bend their arm up behind their neck."},{"title":"The \"wing\" position","text":"Uke's arm is bent with their elbow pointing up behind their head — like a bird's folded wing. This position traps the arm and prevents them from defending the choke."},{"title":"Apply the strangle","text":"Squeeze the choking arm into the neck while maintaining the arm trap. The combination of the choke and the trapped arm prevents most escape attempts."}],"principles":["The arm trap is what distinguishes Kataha-Jime from other rear chokes — it removes one defense arm while applying the strangle.","The choking arm contacts the sides of the neck (carotid arteries), not the throat. Tracheal pressure is both ineffective and dangerous.","Once the wing position is achieved, uke has very limited options. The trapped arm prevents any reach-back defense.","Body positioning behind uke must be secure. If uke can create space, they can roll to escape."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Choking at the throat instead of the sides of the neck","fix":"Aim for the carotid compression — sides of the neck. Trachea chokes are illegal and ineffective in competition."},{"mistake":"Not completing the arm trap — wing not secured","fix":"Ensure uke's arm is fully bent up behind their head. A partial trap allows uke to free the arm and defend."},{"mistake":"Losing rear control — uke rolls to face you","fix":"Maintain rear body control with your legs or by hooking uke's body. If uke faces you, the strangle position is lost."},{"mistake":"Applying too much arm muscle — not using technique","fix":"The choke works by compressing the carotids, not by brute arm strength. Proper alignment is more important than pure force."}],"when":"Kataha-Jime is used from the rear control position — after a takedown or throw that ends with tori behind uke, or after uke rolls in newaza and exposes their back. It is particularly effective because it simultaneously attacks the neck and immobilizes one arm.","setups":[],"followUps":["ura-gatame","okuri-eri-jime"],"counters":[],"related":["hadaka-jime","okuri-eri-jime","sankaku-jime"],"source":"https://judolearn.com/techniques/kataha-jime/"},{"id":"kesa-gatame","name":"Kesa-Gatame","kanji":"袈裟固","translation":"Scarf Hold","category":"Katame-Waza","subcategory":"Osaekomi-Waza","difficulty":1,"grade":"6th kyu","grades":["6th kyu","5th kyu"],"videos":[{"yt":"NDaQuJOFBYk","title":"袈裟固 /  Kesa-gatame","channel":"KODOKAN"},{"yt":"sEmKk3m8nwE","title":"HOW TO ESCAPE KESA GATAME - Travis Stevens Basic Judo Techniques","channel":"Travis Stevens"},{"yt":"wUfi4GJ_8kM","title":"Kesa Gatame, Judo's Heaviest Pin for BJJ","channel":"Stephan Kesting"}],"overview":"Kesa-Gatame is the foundational hold-down of judo and typically the first osaekomi-waza taught to beginners. Tori sits beside uke, trapping uke's arm under their armpit and wrapping the other arm around uke's neck, controlling them with body weight and leg positioning. It teaches the core principles of ground control: posture, weight distribution, and responding to uke's escapes.","phases":[],"steps":[{"title":"Sit beside uke at their right shoulder, facing the same direction as their head","text":"Position yourself at uke's upper right side. Your right hip is adjacent to uke's right shoulder. Face toward uke's feet."},{"title":"Trap uke's right arm under your left armpit","text":"Wrap your left arm around and under uke's right arm, trapping it tightly in your armpit. Hold their arm close to your body — do not allow any space for them to pull it free."},{"title":"Wrap your right arm around uke's neck","text":"Slide your right arm under uke's neck and grip uke's right collar near the base of their neck, or wrap around to grip behind their head. Your forearm rests against the side of their head."},{"title":"Drive your weight onto uke's chest","text":"Lower your center of gravity onto uke's chest by leaning into them. Distribute your body weight — the heavier and lower you are, the more effective the hold."},{"title":"Position your legs for stability and countering escapes","text":"Spread your legs wide for a low, stable base. Your right leg extends away from uke for balance. As uke attempts to escape, adjust your leg position to maintain control."}],"principles":["Body weight is the primary holding mechanism — your weight presses uke's shoulder and chest to the mat.","The trapped arm must be held tight in your armpit with no space — any gap and uke can work it free.","Low center of gravity: spread your legs and lower your hips. A high, kneeling position is easily rolled.","Stay connected to uke at all times. If they move, you move with them, not against them."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Sitting too upright — high center of gravity","fix":"Lean your body weight forward and down onto uke. The lower and heavier you feel to uke, the harder to escape."},{"mistake":"Allowing space under the trapped arm","fix":"Clamp uke's arm in your armpit with your entire arm and shoulder. Any gap gives them leverage to escape."},{"mistake":"Legs too close together — unstable base","fix":"Spread your legs wide. Wide legs lower your center of gravity and resist uke's rolling escapes."},{"mistake":"Pulling up on the arm trap (arms too tense)","fix":"Relax your arms and let body weight do the holding. Pulling up with arms raises your center of gravity."}],"when":"Kesa-Gatame is the natural hold-down transition from throws that project uke to your side — Osoto-Gari, Ko-Soto-Gari, or any throw where tori lands beside uke. After Seoi-Nage, Yoko-Shiho-Gatame or mount is typically more accessible. It is the beginner's introduction to ne-waza control and teaches essential body weight mechanics.","setups":[],"followUps":["seoi-nage","osoto-gari"],"counters":[],"related":["yoko-shiho-gatame","kami-shiho-gatame","kuzure-kesa-gatame"],"source":"https://judolearn.com/techniques/kesa-gatame/"},{"id":"kibisu-gaeshi","name":"Kibisu-Gaeshi","kanji":"踵返","translation":"Heel Reversal","category":"Nage-Waza","subcategory":"Te-Waza","difficulty":2,"grade":"3rd kyu","grades":["3rd kyu","2nd kyu"],"videos":[{"yt":"tJylJYfBliA","title":"踵返 / Kibisu-gaeshi","channel":"KODOKAN"},{"yt":"uyIvUpde_r4","title":"How to do Kibisu Gaeshi (Traditional version) | Ankle Pick | 踵返","channel":"Sampson Judo"},{"yt":"GnjpQIkZTQA","title":"KIBISU GAESHI BASIC SKILLS","channel":"JBBA Judo Education Channel"},{"yt":"kZl_A8WnGTc","title":"Kibisu-Gaeshi Tips | Riki Judo Dojo","channel":"Riki Judo Dojo"},{"yt":"gxTqi1NWPas","title":"The difference between kuchiki taoshi and kibisu gaeshi","channel":"BeyondGrappling"}],"overview":"Kibisu-Gaeshi is a heel pick throw where tori seizes uke's heel and lifts it sharply upward while simultaneously pushing uke's upper body backward, dumping them to the mat. It is often used as a counter-technique or in combination when uke retreats or moves backward, and it exemplifies the te-waza principle of using the hands alone — without hip or leg contact — to execute a throw.","phases":[{"label":"Kuzushi · Off-balance","text":"Uke's balance is broken to their rear. This is typically achieved by pushing uke's upper body backward with one hand on the chest or collar, while simultaneously seizing their heel. The backward push and the heel lift create opposing forces that collapse uke's base from underneath."},{"label":"Tsukuri · Entry","text":"Lower your body by bending at the knees and hips. Reach down with one hand to grip uke's heel from behind or the side — the grip wraps around the heel, fingers under the Achilles tendon. Your other hand simultaneously grips uke's collar or lapel at chest height for the push."},{"label":"Kake · Execution","text":"Lift the heel sharply upward while driving uke's upper body backward with the collar push. The simultaneous actions of lifting the foot and pushing the chest create an immediate and dramatic loss of balance. Uke falls backward onto the mat."}],"steps":[{"title":"Create or identify rearward movement","text":"Kibisu-Gaeshi works when uke steps backward or is being pushed backward. Create the opportunity by pushing uke's chest or by following uke's retreat."},{"title":"Lower your level","text":"Bend your knees and drop your hips to bring your hand to heel height. Stay close to uke as you lower down."},{"title":"Grip the heel","text":"Reach down and grip uke's near heel firmly — fingers curling around the back of the heel under the Achilles tendon. The grip must be secure."},{"title":"Grip the collar or chest with the free hand","text":"Simultaneously grip uke's lapel near the collar with your other hand for the backward push."},{"title":"Lift and push simultaneously","text":"Drive the heel upward sharply while pushing uke's upper body backward. These two forces destroy uke's base and send them straight onto their back."}],"principles":["Both actions — heel lift and upper-body push — must happen simultaneously. Sequential execution gives uke time to adjust.","Stay close to uke when gripping the heel. Reaching too far extends your back and reduces lifting power.","The heel grip should be firm and wrap under the Achilles tendon. A grip on the toe or ankle is weaker and easier for uke to pull free.","Kibisu-Gaeshi often arises from uke's retreating movement — learn to read when uke is stepping back and time the technique to coincide with their weight shift."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Pushing first, then lifting the heel","fix":"The push and lift must be simultaneous. If you push first, uke hops backward. If you lift first, uke leans forward. The simultaneous action is what traps them."},{"mistake":"Gripping the toe or lower ankle","fix":"Grip the heel — wrap your fingers under the Achilles tendon. A toe or ankle grip has poor leverage and uke can simply pull their foot free."},{"mistake":"Standing too far from uke when reaching for the heel","fix":"Move in close before dropping to heel height. Reaching from a distance rounds your back and removes power from the lift."},{"mistake":"Attempting Kibisu-Gaeshi against a stationary uke","fix":"The technique is most effective when uke is moving or off-balance. Against a planted, upright uke, the heel is hard to grip and easier to defend."}],"when":"Kibisu-Gaeshi is most effective as a counter when uke steps backward and briefly has weight on the back foot, making the heel accessible. It is also used in combination with forward attacks — when uke retreats to avoid a throw, tori switches to the heel pick. It is classified as te-waza because the throw is executed entirely with the hands.","setups":[],"followUps":["o-uchi-gari","harai-goshi"],"counters":["tai-otoshi"],"related":["sukui-nage","morote-gari","uki-otoshi"],"source":"https://judolearn.com/techniques/kibisu-gaeshi/"},{"id":"ko-soto-gake","name":"Ko-Soto-Gake","kanji":"小外掛","translation":"Small Outer Hook","category":"Nage-Waza","subcategory":"Ashi-Waza","difficulty":2,"grade":"4th kyu","grades":["4th kyu","3rd kyu"],"videos":[{"yt":"8b6kY4s4zH4","title":"小外掛 / Ko-soto-gake","channel":"KODOKAN"},{"yt":"j0eKrS2SQ8I","title":"Kosoto gake","channel":"Shintaro Higashi"},{"yt":"80KK0dVJlNs","title":"How to Execute a Devastating Kosoto-Gake","channel":"Shintaro Nakano"},{"yt":"N1oyPnkYfF0","title":"Ko-Soto-Gake Tips | Riki Judo Dojo","channel":"Riki Judo Dojo"},{"yt":"HfU9mAYpZ5k","title":"kosoto-gake Compilation","channel":"Ochiru"}],"overview":"Ko-Soto-Gake is a small outer hooking throw that targets the back of uke's lower leg or heel from the outside. Unlike Ko-Soto-Gari which reaps the foot, Ko-Soto-Gake hooks (gake) the calf or heel and holds it, pressing forward with the upper body simultaneously to tip uke backward. The \"hook and hold\" rather than \"reap and release\" distinguishes it from Ko-Soto-Gari.","phases":[{"label":"Kuzushi · Off-balance","text":"Push uke backward and onto their rear heel. The hands push uke's upper body backward — lapel and sleeve grips both push rather than pull. The goal is to get uke's weight loading onto the heel that will be hooked. A backward push or feint that draws uke's weight to their rear helps set up the technique."},{"label":"Tsukuri · Entry","text":"Step to the outside of uke's right foot with your left foot, positioning yourself at uke's right side. Bend your right knee and hook your right leg behind uke's right lower leg — the back of your knee hooks against uke's calf or heel. Your upper body is against uke's side or slightly in front, ready to push."},{"label":"Kake · Execution","text":"Hook and hold uke's lower leg with your right leg — keeping the hook in place rather than sweeping through. Simultaneously push uke's upper body backward and over the hooked leg. Uke's leg is caught (hooked) while their upper body tips backward, dumping them to the mat."}],"steps":[{"title":"Push uke backward","text":"Use both grips to push uke's upper body backward. The goal is to shift uke's weight to their rear heel."},{"title":"Step to the outside of uke's foot","text":"Step your left foot to the outside and slightly behind uke's right foot, positioning your body at uke's right side."},{"title":"Hook the back of the lower leg","text":"Bend your right knee and hook it behind uke's right calf or heel. Your knee hooks from the back — the back of your knee catches uke's leg."},{"title":"Hold the hook and push the upper body","text":"Maintain the hook rather than sweeping through. Simultaneously push uke's upper body backward over the hooked leg."},{"title":"Tip uke backward to the mat","text":"Uke's leg is trapped by the hook while their upper body tips backward. They fall onto their back."}],"principles":["The key distinction from Ko-Soto-Gari is \"hook and hold\" vs \"reap and release.\" Ko-Soto-Gake maintains the hook contact throughout.","The hook should be on the calf or behind the heel — not the ankle. The calf or heel hook allows the push force to tip uke backward effectively.","The push force is as important as the hook. Without a strong upper-body push, uke simply has a hooked leg and can maintain balance.","The backward kuzushi must be established before the hook. Hooking before uke's weight is backward simply catches their leg without tipping them."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Sweeping the leg away (Ko-Soto-Gari mechanics)","fix":"In Ko-Soto-Gake, keep the leg hooked and fixed. The throw works by tipping uke backward over the stationary hook, not by reaping the leg away."},{"mistake":"Hooking too low — at the ankle","fix":"The hook should be at the calf or heel. Ankle-level hooking is weaker and may hook the joint in a way that is dangerous."},{"mistake":"Insufficient backward push","fix":"The upper body push must be strong and continuous. A weak push allows uke to post with their hands and resist."},{"mistake":"Not establishing backward kuzushi first","fix":"The technique requires uke's weight to be backward before hooking. Push uke back before hooking — do not hook and then try to push."}],"when":"Ko-Soto-Gake is effective when uke is moving or retreating backward, putting weight on their rear foot. It works well as a counter when uke steps backward defensively. It can also be set up by feinting a forward throw to make uke retreat, then switching to Ko-Soto-Gake as they step back.","setups":[],"followUps":["osoto-gari","harai-goshi","kesa-gatame"],"counters":["tai-otoshi"],"related":["ko-soto-gari","osoto-gari","o-soto-guruma"],"source":"https://judolearn.com/techniques/ko-soto-gake/"},{"id":"ko-soto-gari","name":"Ko-Soto-Gari","kanji":"小外刈","translation":"Minor Outer Reap","category":"Nage-Waza","subcategory":"Ashi-Waza","difficulty":1,"grade":"5th kyu","grades":["5th kyu","4th kyu"],"videos":[{"yt":"jeQ541ScLB4","title":"小外刈 / Ko-soto-gari","channel":"KODOKAN"},{"yt":"qPezVdOUx0A","title":"Kosoto gari in depth","channel":"Shintaro Higashi"},{"yt":"5YdLnVgPoY4","title":"Ko-Soto-Gari Tips | Riki Judo Dojo","channel":"Riki Judo Dojo"},{"yt":"tiG3EShhemI","title":"Ko soto gari Compilation","channel":"Ochiru"}],"overview":"Ko-Soto-Gari is a minor outer reap that hooks the outside of uke's heel with a quick, compact scooping action. Smaller and more subtle than Osoto-Gari, it targets the heel rather than the full leg, making it useful as a feint, a transition, or a combination trigger. It is particularly effective when uke's weight is on their heels.","phases":[{"label":"Kuzushi · Off-balance","text":"Push uke backward and toward their right rear corner. Your lapel hand drives diagonally into uke's chest toward their right shoulder, loading weight specifically onto the right heel. The heel must be bearing weight for the reap to work."},{"label":"Tsukuri · Entry","text":"Step to the outside of uke's right foot with your left foot. Position yourself at uke's side. Your right foot is free to hook."},{"label":"Kake · Execution","text":"Hook behind uke's right heel with your right foot, making contact at the Achilles tendon/heel area from the outside. The scooping motion pulls the heel inward and upward. Simultaneously drive your upper body forward into uke. The combined heel scoop and push topples uke backward."}],"steps":[{"title":"Load uke's weight onto their right heel","text":"Push uke backward with your lapel hand. Their right heel must be bearing weight — this is the prerequisite for an effective reap."},{"title":"Step to the outside with your left foot","text":"Step your left foot to the outside of uke's right foot. You are now positioned slightly to uke's right side."},{"title":"Hook the outside of uke's heel","text":"Hook behind uke's right heel with your right foot, contacting the Achilles tendon/heel from the outside. Use a scooping motion to pull it inward and upward."},{"title":"Drive forward and down","text":"Push uke backward with your chest and grips as you scoop the heel. The push and scoop together topple uke backward."}],"principles":["This is a minor technique — small, quick, and precise. Do not try to force it with power.","The reap is at the heel level. Contact higher up the leg is blocked by uke bending their knee.","Ko-Soto-Gari pairs well with O-Uchi-Gari: attack inside-out repeatedly to disrupt uke's balance.","Works best on a static or heel-weighted uke — catches them flatfooted."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Trying to reap a foot that is not bearing weight","fix":"Create backward kuzushi first. A floating foot cannot be reaped effectively."},{"mistake":"Contact too high (at the ankle or calf)","fix":"Target the heel specifically. Higher contact can be blocked."},{"mistake":"Using too much force for a minor technique","fix":"Ko-Soto-Gari is subtle. It relies on precision and timing, not power."}],"when":"Ko-Soto-Gari is most useful as a transitional technique — attacking one side then the other — or when catching uke standing still with weight on their heels. Also effective after O-Uchi-Gari if uke defends by stepping back with their right leg: their weight shifts to the heel, creating a Ko-Soto-Gari opportunity.","setups":["o-uchi-gari","seoi-nage"],"followUps":[],"counters":["o-uchi-gari","uchi-mata"],"related":["osoto-gari","o-uchi-gari","de-ashi-barai"],"source":"https://judolearn.com/techniques/ko-soto-gari/"},{"id":"ko-uchi-gaeshi","name":"Ko-Uchi-Gaeshi","kanji":"小内返","translation":"Ko-Uchi Counter","category":"Nage-Waza","subcategory":"Ashi-Waza","difficulty":3,"grade":"2nd kyu","grades":["2nd kyu","1st kyu"],"videos":[{"yt":"_MWAdYi_LC4","title":"小内返 / Ko-uchi-gaeshi","channel":"KODOKAN"},{"yt":"aENEt9_VmuU","title":"Kouchi-gaeshi - Demo","channel":"Efficient Judo"},{"yt":"IaFgrUPRCDE","title":"Judo Básico: Ko Uchi Gaeshi em 4 Direções | Basic Ko Uchi Gaeshi Demonstration (4 Directions)","channel":"Sensei Jose Eudes Monteiro"}],"overview":"Ko-Uchi-Gaeshi is the counter-throw to Ko-Uchi-Gari. When uke hooks inward with Ko-Uchi-Gari against tori's rear heel or ankle, tori steps over or around uke's hooking leg and immediately sweeps uke's supporting (base) leg with O-Soto-Gari or De-Ashi-Barai. The escape step simultaneously defeats uke's hook and positions tori for a counter sweep. Part of the Kodokan Shinmeisho no Waza, it demonstrates how a small commitment (Ko-Uchi-Gari) can still create a full-throw counter opportunity.","phases":[{"label":"Kuzushi · Off-balance","text":"Ko-Uchi-Gari is a subtle attack, but when committed, uke's hooking leg is committed inward and their weight shifts over the supporting leg. The counter exploits this moment of single-leg dependence. By stepping over or around the hook, tori removes uke's reaping contact while simultaneously threatening the loaded support leg."},{"label":"Tsukuri · Entry","text":"As uke's foot hooks inward against your heel or ankle, lift or step your threatened foot over uke's hooking leg. This escapes the hook and simultaneously moves you to the outside of uke's base. Your weight shifts to your free leg, which now positions to attack uke's support."},{"label":"Kake · Execution","text":"Apply De-Ashi-Barai (sweeping uke's supporting foot laterally as their weight is on it) or O-Soto-Gari (reaping the support leg from behind). The choice depends on position: if uke's support foot is lateral and weight-bearing, De-Ashi-Barai; if you are positioned behind uke's support leg, O-Soto-Gari. Coordinate the sweep with an arm pull."}],"steps":[{"title":"Detect uke's Ko-Uchi-Gari hook","text":"Feel uke's foot driving inward toward your heel or inner ankle. Ko-Uchi-Gari is a smaller, quicker attack than O-Uchi-Gari — the hook is tighter and lower."},{"title":"Lift or step the threatened foot over uke's hook","text":"Raise the targeted foot and step it over uke's hooking leg, escaping the hook before it can load. The step should carry you to the outside of uke's attacking position."},{"title":"Identify uke's supporting leg","text":"As you step over the hook, uke's weight is on their supporting leg. Identify which leg is bearing weight — this is your target."},{"title":"Apply De-Ashi-Barai or O-Soto-Gari to the support","text":"Sweep uke's supporting foot laterally (De-Ashi-Barai) or reap it from behind (O-Soto-Gari). The sweep timing coincides with uke's maximum weight commitment to that leg."},{"title":"Coordinate arm control with the sweep","text":"Pull uke's arms in the direction of the sweep — sleeve grip pulls toward the counter throw. The combined arm pull and leg sweep complete the throw."}],"principles":["The escape step over uke's hook is the foundation — it simultaneously defeats the Ko-Uchi-Gari and transitions into counter position.","Counter sweep mechanics are the same as the primary techniques (De-Ashi-Barai or O-Soto-Gari) — the difference is in the timing and context.","Ko-Uchi-Gari is a quick, subtle attack; the counter must match its pace. A slow response misses the committed phase.","Uke's supporting leg is always the target in ashi-waza counters — attack the leg bearing weight, not the attacking leg in the air.","The step over the hook does not need to be a large movement — a small, sharp lift and step-through is sufficient."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Stepping away from the hook rather than over it","fix":"Step over uke's hooking foot, not away from it. Stepping away simply retreats and removes the counter opportunity."},{"mistake":"Counter too late — uke has already recovered from the hook","fix":"The transition from step-over to counter sweep must be immediate. If uke's hook is already withdrawn, the counter timing has passed."},{"mistake":"Targeting uke's hooking leg instead of the support","fix":"After stepping over the hook, focus on the supporting leg. The hooking leg is returning to the mat — it is not in the ideal counter sweep position."},{"mistake":"No arm pull — counter sweep alone is insufficient","fix":"Pull uke's arms in the direction of the throw. The leg sweep and arm pull must combine to create the topple."}],"when":"Ko-Uchi-Gaeshi is most effective against opponents who use Ko-Uchi-Gari frequently and with commitment — particularly those who drive the hook deep and load their weight into the attack. It is also applicable when uke uses Ko-Uchi-Gari as a setup to create movement, as the counter can be applied during the commitment phase before they transition. In competition, establishing this counter discourages repeated inner-heel attacks.","setups":[],"followUps":["ko-uchi-gari","de-ashi-barai","osoto-gari"],"counters":[],"related":["ko-uchi-gari","de-ashi-barai","osoto-gari"],"source":"https://judolearn.com/techniques/ko-uchi-gaeshi/"},{"id":"ko-uchi-gake","name":"Ko-Uchi-Gake","kanji":"小内掛","translation":"Small Inner Hook","category":"Nage-Waza","subcategory":"Ashi-Waza","difficulty":2,"grade":"4th kyu","grades":["4th kyu","3rd kyu"],"videos":[{"yt":"v-bPmFsDU7A","title":"Kouchi-gake - Demo","channel":"Efficient Judo"},{"yt":"E4zE8LLdUF8","title":"How to ... Ko Uchi Gake","channel":"The Art of Balance Dojo"},{"yt":"KYDpqtadPqk","title":"Ko uchi gake - highlights","channel":"Technique of the week"}],"overview":"Ko-Uchi-Gake is a small inner hooking throw that targets the inside of uke's lower leg. Like Ko-Soto-Gake, it hooks and holds rather than reaping. Tori hooks the inside of uke's lower leg with their own leg, trapping it, while pushing or driving uke's upper body backward or to the side. It is related to Ko-Uchi-Gari but uses the gake (hook) principle rather than the gari (reap) principle.","phases":[{"label":"Kuzushi · Off-balance","text":"Push or drive uke backward onto their rear heel, or pull uke to one side to shift their weight onto one foot. The inner hook works best when uke's weight is on the leg being hooked. A combination of pushing the upper body and stepping to create a side-on position helps establish the kuzushi direction."},{"label":"Tsukuri · Entry","text":"Step in with one foot and position your hooking leg to attack the inside of uke's lower leg. The hook wraps around the inside of uke's calf or above the ankle from the inner side. Your body faces uke or is at a slight angle. The hooking leg contacts uke's leg from the inner side, not a sweep but a catch."},{"label":"Kake · Execution","text":"Maintain the inner hook while driving uke's upper body in the opposite direction — typically backward or to the outside. The hook traps the inner leg while the upper body is pushed over it. Uke's base is destroyed from the inside as their upper body goes over the trapped leg."}],"steps":[{"title":"Load uke's weight onto the target leg","text":"Through gripping and pushing, shift uke's weight onto the leg you will hook. The hook is most effective when that leg bears weight."},{"title":"Step in close","text":"Step your foot in beside uke's foot to position your hooking leg. You need to be close enough that your leg can contact the inside of uke's lower leg."},{"title":"Hook from the inside","text":"Hook your leg around the inside of uke's lower leg — the back of your knee hooks against uke's calf from the inner side."},{"title":"Hold the hook firmly","text":"Unlike a reap, maintain the hook contact. Your leg is holding uke's leg trapped, not sweeping it."},{"title":"Drive uke's upper body over the hook","text":"Push uke's upper body backward or to the outside simultaneously. Uke tips over the trapped inner leg and falls to the mat."}],"principles":["Ko-Uchi-Gake hooks and holds the inside of uke's lower leg — it does not sweep through like Ko-Uchi-Gari.","The direction of the upper body push matters — pushing backward tips uke backward; pushing to the outside tips them sideways. Match the push direction to the kuzushi.","Close body positioning is essential. The inner hook requires proximity — too far away and the leg reach is insufficient.","A trapped leg is only half the technique. Strong upper body control is the other half."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Sweeping through instead of hooking and holding","fix":"Ko-Uchi-Gake requires maintaining the hook contact. If the leg sweeps through and releases, it becomes Ko-Uchi-Gari mechanics."},{"mistake":"Hooking too high — above the knee","fix":"The hook targets the lower leg, below the knee. Hooking above the knee applies torque to the knee joint and may injure uke."},{"mistake":"No upper body control during the hook","fix":"The hook alone cannot throw uke. Drive the upper body simultaneously to complete the technique."},{"mistake":"Standing too far from uke","fix":"Move in close before hooking. The inner hook requires body proximity."}],"when":"Ko-Uchi-Gake works best when uke is in a square stance with weight evenly distributed or slightly backward. It complements Ko-Uchi-Gari — when uke has begun to defend the reap by pulling the foot back, shift to the hook. Use it in combination sequences when you need to attack the inside of uke's base.","setups":["harai-goshi"],"followUps":["ko-uchi-gari"],"counters":["tai-otoshi"],"related":["ko-uchi-gari","ko-soto-gake","o-uchi-gari"],"source":"https://judolearn.com/techniques/ko-uchi-gake/"},{"id":"ko-uchi-gari","name":"Ko-Uchi-Gari","kanji":"小内刈","translation":"Minor Inner Reap","category":"Nage-Waza","subcategory":"Ashi-Waza","difficulty":1,"grade":"5th kyu","grades":["5th kyu","4th kyu"],"videos":[{"yt":"3Jb3tZvr9Ng","title":"小内刈 / Ko-uchi-gari","channel":"KODOKAN"},{"yt":"dYcz1bc5_-s","title":"Basic Kouchi Gari TRICK","channel":"Shintaro Higashi"},{"yt":"1rgrk_yau4g","title":"Ko-Uchi-Gari Tips | Riki Judo Dojo","channel":"Riki Judo Dojo"},{"yt":"IWPC2ynWawY","title":"ko-uchi-gari compilation","channel":"Ochiru"}],"overview":"Ko-Uchi-Gari is a minor inner reap that hooks uke's heel from the inside, typically targeting a single foot as they step or shift weight. It is one of the most versatile setup techniques in judo — used either as a scoring throw or more commonly to disrupt balance and create openings for major techniques like Seoi-Nage, Tai-Otoshi, or Harai-Goshi.","phases":[{"label":"Kuzushi · Off-balance","text":"Ko-Uchi-Gari works both with backward kuzushi (push uke backward onto the targeted foot) and by timing the attack as uke steps forward, catching the heel as weight transfers onto it."},{"label":"Tsukuri · Entry","text":"Step your left foot to the inside-left of uke's stance, positioning yourself to attack uke's right heel from the inside. Keep your body compact — do not step between their feet."},{"label":"Kake · Execution","text":"Hook your right foot around the inside of uke's right heel in a scooping motion, pulling their heel upward and inward toward uke's centerline. Simultaneously use your grips to push uke backward over the destabilized foot. The action is a quick, sharp hook — not a long sweep."}],"steps":[{"title":"Time the attack or create backward kuzushi","text":"Either push uke backward so their weight is on their right heel, or time the attack as they step forward with their right foot (catching it mid-weight-transfer)."},{"title":"Step your left foot in","text":"Plant your left foot between uke's legs or beside their right foot. Keep your body compact — do not overreach."},{"title":"Hook uke's heel with your right foot","text":"Hook the inside of uke's right heel with your right foot in a scooping motion. Pull the heel upward and inward toward uke's centerline — the action is compact and sharp, not a big sweep."},{"title":"Push with your grips","text":"Drive uke backward over the destabilized foot using your lapel and sleeve grips. The leg hook and upper body push work together."},{"title":"Follow up or transition","text":"If Ko-Uchi-Gari scores, follow uke to the ground into ne-waza. If uke defends and you only disrupt their balance, immediately transition to Seoi-Nage, Tai-Otoshi, or another major technique."}],"principles":["Ko-Uchi-Gari is often more valuable as a disruption tool than a finishing throw — use it to break posture and create openings.","Timing the attack as uke steps forward is often more effective than fighting for kuzushi from a static position.","The reap targets the heel, not the calf. The hook is at the ankle/heel level — small and precise.","Combine freely: Ko-Uchi-Gari → Seoi-Nage is one of the most common combination sequences in competition judo."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Overreaching and losing your own balance","fix":"Keep the technique compact. Ko-Uchi-Gari is a small technique — do not lunge for it."},{"mistake":"Targeting the calf or mid-leg instead of the heel","fix":"The hook is at heel level. Contact higher up the leg is easily blocked by uke simply bending their knee."},{"mistake":"Using it as a standalone throw when uke is stable","fix":"Against a stable, braced uke, Ko-Uchi-Gari will not score. Use it when uke is stepping or already off-balance."},{"mistake":"Not following up after a failed attack","fix":"Even when Ko-Uchi-Gari fails, uke's defensive reaction shifts their weight in a predictable direction. Always have a combination ready."}],"when":"Ko-Uchi-Gari is most effective as part of a combination: the first attack sets up the second. Attack Ko-Uchi-Gari — if uke pushes their foot downward and into the ground to resist the lift, their weight shifts forward — immediately switch to Seoi-Nage. If they pull their foot away, the space opens for O-Uchi-Gari on the other leg or a Tai-Otoshi entry. It is also effective as a solo throw when catching uke mid-step.","setups":["seoi-nage","tai-otoshi","harai-goshi","osoto-gari"],"followUps":[],"counters":["ko-uchi-gaeshi","harai-goshi","seoi-nage"],"related":["o-uchi-gari","ko-soto-gari","de-ashi-barai","seoi-nage"],"source":"https://judolearn.com/techniques/ko-uchi-gari/"},{"id":"koshi-guruma","name":"Koshi-Guruma","kanji":"腰車","translation":"Hip Wheel","category":"Nage-Waza","subcategory":"Koshi-Waza","difficulty":2,"grade":"4th kyu","grades":["4th kyu","3rd kyu"],"videos":[{"yt":"SU7Id6uVJ44","title":"腰車 / Koshi-guruma","channel":"KODOKAN"},{"yt":"mSoj1aQirvM","title":"Koshi Guruma - Applying All the Concepts with Travis Stevens","channel":"JudoFanatics"},{"yt":"jAMnXzPrW8w","title":"Koshi Guruma 3 ways","channel":"Shintaro Higashi"},{"yt":"JgIaC4x9IgQ","title":"Top 10 Judo Koshi guruma Compilation Highlights | 腰車","channel":"Judo Spirit"}],"overview":"Koshi-Guruma is a hip throw in which tori wraps their arm around uke's neck and uses the hip as a wheel to rotate uke over and to the mat. Unlike O-Goshi where the arm encircles the waist, Koshi-Guruma places the arm around uke's head and neck, creating a higher fulcrum point that generates a powerful wheeling rotation. This technique is particularly effective against opponents who resist by dropping their hips.","phases":[{"label":"Kuzushi · Off-balance","text":"Pull uke forward and upward onto their right front corner using your sleeve grip. Simultaneously use the lapel arm to pull uke's upper body toward you, encouraging them to lean forward. Uke's weight should shift to their toes and their upper body tilt toward you."},{"label":"Tsukuri · Entry","text":"Step your right foot in deep, placing it between uke's feet with toes turned out. Pivot sharply on the right foot to bring your left foot across so your back is fully toward uke's front. As you turn, slide your right arm over uke's shoulder and around the back of their neck, locking their head against your right shoulder. Your hip must make solid contact with uke's hip region."},{"label":"Kake · Execution","text":"With the arm firmly controlling uke's head and neck, bend forward from the hips while pulling uke's sleeve arm down and across. The hip acts as the wheel's axle — uke's body rolls over your hip in a circular arc. Drive your hip through and straighten your legs to complete the projection, sending uke forward and down to the mat."}],"steps":[{"title":"Break uke's balance forward","text":"Pull uke's sleeve upward and toward their right front corner. Your lapel hand draws their upper body toward you until their weight is on their toes."},{"title":"Step in and begin the pivot","text":"Step your right foot inside and in front of uke's right foot, toes angled outward. Begin your 180-degree pivot on the right foot."},{"title":"Complete the turn and wrap uke's neck","text":"Swing your left foot around to complete the pivot. Slide your right arm up and around uke's neck, securing their head against your shoulder. Your back should be pressed against uke's chest."},{"title":"Load uke onto your hip","text":"With knees slightly bent, your hip should be at or just below uke's centre of gravity. Straighten your legs to lift uke's weight onto your hip."},{"title":"Execute the wheel — bend and throw","text":"Bend sharply forward from the waist, driving your right elbow down and pulling the sleeve hand across and down. Uke wheels over your hip and is projected forward to the mat."}],"principles":["The neck/head arm must lock uke's upper body firmly against your shoulder — any looseness allows uke to pull their head free.","Hip contact must be solid: your right hip presses into uke's right hip before the bend-and-throw phase.","The \"wheel\" motion requires a sharp forward bend — your torso drives down to initiate the rotation.","Keep uke close throughout the entry; distance between bodies kills the leverage."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Arm placed too loosely around uke's neck","fix":"Drive your arm deep around the neck and press uke's head firmly into your shoulder. A loose arm lets uke straighten up and escape."},{"mistake":"Failing to complete the full pivot","fix":"Your back must be fully facing uke's chest. A half-turn means your hip is to the side rather than in front, losing the wheel axis."},{"mistake":"Remaining upright during kake","fix":"The throw is completed by bending sharply forward. Staying upright reduces the throw to a lift rather than a wheel."},{"mistake":"Placing the neck arm too high on uke's head","fix":"The arm should wrap around the back of the neck at shoulder level, not around the skull. Too high puts strain on uke's neck and reduces control."}],"when":"Koshi-Guruma works well against an opponent who bends forward at the waist or who has a defensive, head-down posture. The neck wrap is difficult to establish against a very upright, stiff opponent. It pairs well as a follow-up to failed O-Goshi attempts — when uke resists the waist wrap, transitioning the arm to the neck completes the entry.","setups":["ko-uchi-gari","o-uchi-gari"],"followUps":[],"counters":["ura-nage","tani-otoshi"],"related":["o-goshi","tsuri-goshi","harai-goshi"],"source":"https://judolearn.com/techniques/koshi-guruma/"},{"id":"kuchiki-taoshi","name":"Kuchiki-Taoshi","kanji":"朽木倒","translation":"Single-Leg Takedown","category":"Nage-Waza","subcategory":"Te-Waza","difficulty":1,"grade":"5th kyu","grades":["5th kyu","4th kyu"],"videos":[{"yt":"ZNL47q1aJNY","title":"朽木倒 / Kuchiki-taoshi","channel":"KODOKAN"},{"yt":"qC0sal36tMM","title":"Powerful Old School Single Leg (Kuchiki Taoshi) to Ouchi Gari takedown for Judo / BJJ","channel":"Sampson Judo"},{"yt":"OeI7xOu0bRA","title":"Outside Kuchiki-taoshi - Demo","channel":"Efficient Judo"},{"yt":"gxTqi1NWPas","title":"The difference between kuchiki taoshi and kibisu gaeshi","channel":"BeyondGrappling"}],"overview":"Kuchiki-Taoshi is a single-leg takedown in which tori grabs uke's near leg from the outside and drives uke backward to the mat. The name literally means \"rotting tree topple,\" evoking the image of a tree losing its base and falling. It is one of the most instinctively recognizable throws in judo and translates directly into wrestling and MMA leg-attack techniques. Classified among the Shinmeisho no Waza (newly accepted techniques), it is accessible to beginners due to its mechanical simplicity. IMPORTANT: Under current IJF rules (since 2010), initiating Kuchiki-Taoshi as a direct standing leg grab from the standard grip position is prohibited in IJF competition. The technique remains fully valid in randori, club practice, and non-IJF competition formats.","phases":[{"label":"Kuzushi · Off-balance","text":"Push uke backward with your chest and both arms as you shoot in for the leg — the forward drive of your body weight into uke's torso creates the backward off-balance. The grip on the leg simultaneously removes uke's ability to step back and recover. The combined effect is a clean backward kuzushi."},{"label":"Tsukuri · Entry","text":"Shoot your lead foot forward between uke's legs or to the outside of their near leg. Bend your knees to lower your level as you penetrate. Your chest presses into uke's hip or lower abdomen. Your grabbing arm hooks the outside of uke's near knee or thigh, lifting or scooping the leg toward you."},{"label":"Kake · Execution","text":"Drive forward and upward through uke's centre while scooping the grabbed leg up and toward you. Lean your bodyweight into uke's chest, forcing them to arch backward. Guide uke down to the mat — onto their back — as the base leg is pulled forward and their upper body is driven backward simultaneously."}],"steps":[{"title":"Close the distance with a forward drive","text":"Step forward aggressively to close the space between you and uke. Lower your level by bending at the knees, not the waist, to bring your center of gravity below uke's hips."},{"title":"Reach and grip the outside of uke's near knee","text":"Reach down with your arm to grip the outside of uke's near leg at the knee or just above it. The grip is from the outside, curling under the knee to scoop upward."},{"title":"Press your chest into uke's hip or lower torso","text":"Drive your chest and shoulder into uke's hip or lower abdomen as you grip the leg. This creates the backward-loading pressure that prevents uke from posting or stepping away."},{"title":"Scoop the leg upward and toward you","text":"Pull the gripped leg up and toward your body, removing uke's ground contact on that side. Your arm scoops rather than pulls straight back — the upward component lifts uke off balance."},{"title":"Drive forward to topple uke backward","text":"Continue driving forward through uke's center while the gripped leg is held up. Uke's upper body falls backward as their base is eliminated. Guide them to the mat in a controlled backward fall."}],"principles":["Level change is critical — lower your hips by bending the knees before reaching for the leg. Reaching down with a straight back is easily sprawled on.","Drive forward through uke's center simultaneously with the leg grab — the chest pressure into the torso is what causes the topple, not arm strength alone.","Grip the outside of the knee, not the ankle — the knee provides more mechanical leverage and a more reliable scoop angle.","The technique works because you eliminate one base leg while pushing the upper body in the opposite direction — a classic lever-and-base takedown.","Speed of penetration matters more than raw strength. A fast, committed level change is harder to sprawl than a slow, tentative one."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Bending at the waist instead of the knees to reach the leg","fix":"Lower your hips by squatting, not folding forward. Bending at the waist raises your center of gravity and puts your head down in a vulnerable position."},{"mistake":"Grabbing the ankle instead of the knee","fix":"Grip at the knee or lower thigh for better leverage. Ankle grabs allow uke to step over or around your arm more easily."},{"mistake":"Pulling the leg without driving the chest into uke","fix":"The chest drive into uke's torso is as important as the leg grab. Without it, uke simply hops backward on the remaining leg."},{"mistake":"Failing to complete the finish — leaving uke standing on one leg","fix":"Continue driving forward until uke is on the mat. A partially complete Kuchiki-Taoshi leaves you in a compromised position reaching low while uke is mobile."}],"when":"Kuchiki-Taoshi is most effective when uke is upright and advancing, or when they shift weight to the near leg — for example, planting a foot to resist your pull. It can also be used as a low-level option when uke drops into a strong defensive crouch and presents their legs. In competition, it pairs well with grip-fighting sequences that draw uke's attention upward before a low leg attack.","setups":["osoto-gari","o-uchi-gari"],"followUps":[],"counters":["tani-otoshi"],"related":["morote-gari","kibisu-gaeshi","sukui-nage"],"source":"https://judolearn.com/techniques/kuchiki-taoshi/"},{"id":"kuzure-kami-shiho-gatame","name":"Kuzure-Kami-Shiho-Gatame","kanji":"崩上四方固","translation":"Modified Upper Four-Corner Hold","category":"Katame-Waza","subcategory":"Osaekomi-Waza","difficulty":1,"grade":"5th kyu","grades":["5th kyu","4th kyu"],"videos":[{"yt":"YUrogQWdwiY","title":"崩上四方固 /  Kuzure-kami-shiho-gatame","channel":"KODOKAN"},{"yt":"kmeqRY-uFQ8","title":"Kuzure-kami-shiho-gatame - Demo","channel":"Efficient Judo"},{"yt":"2YO5cNynxwc","title":"How to do Kuzure Kami Shiho Gatame","channel":"Sampson Judo"},{"yt":"PPe7E_7d7UI","title":"崩上四方固の逃れ方 /  Kuzure-kami-siho-gatame Escapes","channel":"KODOKAN"}],"overview":"Kuzure-Kami-Shiho-Gatame is a modified (kuzure) version of Kami-Shiho-Gatame where tori controls uke from above but changes one or both arm grips to create a broken or varied version of the standard upper four-corner hold. Typically tori releases one or both belt grips in favor of trapping one of uke's arms — this variation allows more control over uke's arm and improves the hold against certain escapes.","phases":[],"steps":[{"title":"Position above uke's head facing their feet","text":"Lie or position over uke's head, facing their feet — same as Kami-Shiho-Gatame."},{"title":"Trap one arm instead of gripping both sides of the belt","text":"Rather than gripping both sides of uke's belt, trap one of uke's arms by wrapping your arm around it. This is the \"kuzure\" modification."},{"title":"Use free arm to grip belt or collar","text":"With the free arm, grip uke's belt or collar on the other side. This maintains control on both sides but with different grip types."},{"title":"Apply chest pressure to uke's chest","text":"Drive your chest weight downward onto uke's chest or sternum. The weight on the chest is the primary holding force."},{"title":"Spread legs and flatten the body","text":"Spread both legs and flatten your body to lower your center of gravity. Wide legs resist bridging escapes."}],"principles":["The \"kuzure\" modification — typically an arm trap — provides better control of one of uke's arms compared to standard Kami-Shiho-Gatame.","Chest pressure on uke's chest is the holding force. Drive your weight downward.","The arm trap immobilizes one of uke's escape tools. The remaining arm is controlled by the other grip.","Kuzure variations allow adaptation when uke's arm position makes the standard belt grip difficult to maintain."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Chest too high — sitting up rather than pressing down","fix":"Drive your chest weight down onto uke. A high chest means your weight is in your arms, not pressing uke."},{"mistake":"Arm trap too loose","fix":"The trapped arm should be firmly secured — wrap it in or use your knee to pin it. A loose trap allows escape."},{"mistake":"Legs too close together — vulnerable to bridge","fix":"Spread your legs to both sides. A wide base resists uke's bridging attempts."},{"mistake":"Head position too high","fix":"Keep your head low and pressed to uke's body. A high head raises your center of gravity and gives uke leverage to roll you."}],"when":"Kuzure-Kami-Shiho-Gatame is the natural hold after a seoi-nage or other forward shoulder throw where tori ends up above uke's head. The arm trap modification makes it more effective when uke actively works to free their arms. Use when one of uke's arms is accessible for trapping.","setups":[],"followUps":["seoi-otoshi","tai-otoshi"],"counters":[],"related":["kami-shiho-gatame","yoko-shiho-gatame","tate-shiho-gatame"],"source":"https://judolearn.com/techniques/kuzure-kami-shiho-gatame/"},{"id":"kuzure-kesa-gatame","name":"Kuzure-Kesa-Gatame","kanji":"崩袈裟固","translation":"Broken Scarf Hold","category":"Katame-Waza","subcategory":"Osaekomi-Waza","difficulty":1,"grade":"6th kyu","grades":["6th kyu","5th kyu","4th kyu"],"videos":[{"yt":"Q2fb9jaoUFQ","title":"崩袈裟固 /  Kuzure-kesa-gatame","channel":"KODOKAN"},{"yt":"B8TfODeMBiw","title":"How to do Kuzure-Kesa-Gatame | Broken Scarf Hold | 崩袈裟固","channel":"Sampson Judo"},{"yt":"9LwgMBBFmR0","title":"Kuzure-Kesa-Gatame Tips | Riki Judo Dojo","channel":"Riki Judo Dojo"},{"yt":"-zFQ6h4yKT4","title":"崩袈裟固の逃れ方 /  Kuzure-kesa-gatame Escapes","channel":"KODOKAN"}],"overview":"Kuzure-Kesa-Gatame is a variation of Kesa-Gatame (Scarf Hold) in which the arm position is modified — instead of wrapping uke's arm under tori's armpit, tori places their arm around uke's head or neck. This \"broken\" (kuzure) variation is often more comfortable for tori and more difficult for uke to escape. The hold remains at uke's side, with tori's body weight low and to the side, but the changed arm position provides different control and transition options.","phases":[{"label":"Kuzushi · Off-balance","text":"Control uke's upper body by pressing your body weight toward their head-side. Use your arm around uke's neck/head to prevent them from turning toward you, and your grips to prevent them from bridging effectively."},{"label":"Tsukuri · Entry","text":"From uke's right side, sit low with your hips close to the ground. Place your right arm under uke's neck and around the back of their head (rather than trapping their arm as in standard Kesa-Gatame). Grip uke's collar or the mat beside their head. Your left arm controls uke's right arm — grip the sleeve or wrist, trapping it against your body."},{"label":"Kake · Execution","text":"Apply pressure with your body weight by leaning toward uke's upper body. Spread your legs wide for a stable base — your right leg extends away from uke and your left leg bends forward. Keep your hips low and your head down. The hold is maintained by weight distribution, not muscle strength alone."}],"steps":[{"title":"Approach and establish side position","text":"Move to uke's right side, positioning your hips close to their armpit region. Sit low with legs spread for a stable base."},{"title":"Wrap the arm around uke's neck","text":"Slide your right arm under uke's neck and around the back of their head. This is the \"broken\" variation — around the neck, not trapping the arm."},{"title":"Control uke's near arm","text":"Grip uke's right sleeve or wrist with your left hand, pulling their arm against your torso and controlling it."},{"title":"Establish the base","text":"Extend your right leg backward and bend your left leg forward, keeping your hips close to the ground. This wide base resists uke's bridge and roll."},{"title":"Apply and maintain the hold","text":"Lean your body weight toward uke's upper chest. Keep your head low and pressed toward uke. Hold for 10 seconds for waza-ari, 20 seconds for ippon."}],"principles":["Body weight does the pinning — lean into uke rather than trying to hold with arm strength.","The base (spread legs, low hips) is what resists uke's bridge. A high or narrow base can be rolled.","Keep the neck arm pressed down — lifting your head lifts your weight and loosens the hold.","The \"broken\" arm position (around neck instead of trapping arm) offers different transition options to other holds and submissions."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Sitting too high (hips raised)","fix":"Hips must be close to the mat. High hips create a lever point that uke can use to bridge and roll you."},{"mistake":"Holding with arms instead of body weight","fix":"Relax your arms and let body weight do the work. Muscle-holding fatigues quickly; weight distribution is sustainable."},{"mistake":"Narrow leg base","fix":"Widen your leg stance significantly. A narrow base is easily upset by uke's bridging attempts."},{"mistake":"Losing control of uke's near arm","fix":"The near arm (controlled by your left hand) is uke's primary escape tool. Maintain firm control of it against your body."}],"when":"Kuzure-Kesa-Gatame is ideal immediately after a throw when uke lands on their side — the natural follow-through from a side-falling throw. It is also a good transition from Kesa-Gatame when uke begins to extract their arm from under your armpit. The neck wrap allows easy transitions to choke attacks.","setups":[],"followUps":["kata-gatame","gyaku-juji-jime"],"counters":[],"related":["kesa-gatame","kata-gatame","tate-shiho-gatame"],"source":"https://judolearn.com/techniques/kuzure-kesa-gatame/"},{"id":"makura-kesa-gatame","name":"Makura-Kesa-Gatame","kanji":"枕袈裟固","translation":"Pillow Scarf Hold","category":"Katame-Waza","subcategory":"Osaekomi-Waza","difficulty":1,"grade":"6th kyu","grades":["6th kyu","5th kyu"],"videos":[{"yt":"e5HrhANfDcU","title":"Makura-kesa-gatame - Demo","channel":"Efficient Judo"},{"yt":"KNtgtVFh4Rk","title":"MAKURA KESA GATAME","channel":"Judo Olympique"},{"yt":"pJMcG5vpzUs","title":"Makura-Kesa-Gatame Tips | Riki Judo Dojo","channel":"Riki Judo Dojo"}],"overview":"Makura-Kesa-Gatame is a variation of Kesa-Gatame where instead of gripping uke's collar, tori uses their arm as a \"pillow\" (makura) under uke's neck — cradling uke's head with one arm while the other traps uke's arm. It is a comfortable, stable hold that is excellent for beginners and provides good control while being slightly less exposed to certain escapes than standard Kesa-Gatame.","phases":[],"steps":[{"title":"Position at uke's right shoulder","text":"Sit beside uke at their right shoulder, facing toward their feet — same positioning as Kesa-Gatame."},{"title":"Trap uke's right arm under your left armpit","text":"Wrap your left arm under and around uke's right arm, trapping it tightly in your armpit. This is identical to standard Kesa-Gatame."},{"title":"Slide your right arm under uke's neck","text":"Rather than gripping uke's collar, slide your right forearm under uke's neck so that uke's head rests on your arm like a pillow (makura). Your arm is the cradle."},{"title":"Cradle the head with your right arm","text":"Your right arm supports uke's head — the forearm acts as the pillow. This head cradle provides additional control over uke's head and neck movement."},{"title":"Spread legs and apply weight","text":"Spread your legs for a stable base. Drive your weight into uke's chest and shoulder. The head cradle prevents uke from bridging toward your head."}],"principles":["The \"pillow\" arm under uke's neck controls their head — where the head goes, the body follows. Head control prevents many escape attempts.","The trapped arm mechanics are identical to Kesa-Gatame. The distinction is only in the neck/head control arm.","Body weight remains the primary holding force — spread your legs and press your weight onto uke.","Makura-Kesa-Gatame is a natural first variation to teach after standard Kesa-Gatame, as the mechanics are almost identical."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Arm too far under uke's neck — pressure on throat","fix":"The arm should be under the neck, not pressing on the throat. Adjust to cradle the back of the neck, not the front."},{"mistake":"Losing the arm trap while focusing on the head cradle","fix":"Both elements must be maintained simultaneously. The arm trap and the pillow grip both need to be secure."},{"mistake":"Sitting upright — high center of gravity","fix":"Lean your weight forward and down onto uke's chest. A high position is easily escaped."},{"mistake":"Legs too close together","fix":"Spread legs wide for stability. Narrow leg position is vulnerable to rolling escapes."}],"when":"Makura-Kesa-Gatame is the natural hold-down from throws that project uke beside tori — Osoto-Gari, Ko-Soto-Gari, Ko-Soto-Gake. The pillow arm variation provides better head control than standard Kesa-Gatame, making it preferred when uke is trying to bridge toward tori's head.","setups":[],"followUps":["osoto-gari","ko-soto-gari"],"counters":[],"related":["kesa-gatame","kuzure-kesa-gatame","yoko-shiho-gatame"],"source":"https://judolearn.com/techniques/makura-kesa-gatame/"},{"id":"morote-gari","name":"Morote-Gari","kanji":"双手刈","translation":"Two-Hand Reap","category":"Nage-Waza","subcategory":"Te-Waza","difficulty":1,"grade":"5th kyu","grades":["5th kyu","4th kyu"],"videos":[{"yt":"BHLQS4K85bs","title":"双手刈 / Morote-gari","channel":"KODOKAN"},{"yt":"TlEKtlzyNIc","title":"How to do a pick-up Double Leg Takedown in Gi (Morote Gari)","channel":"Global Grappler"},{"yt":"lDQLWgLFjPw","title":"Morote-Gari Tips | Riki Judo Dojo","channel":"Riki Judo Dojo"}],"overview":"Morote-Gari is a double-leg takedown technique where tori shoots low and grasps both of uke's thighs, then drives forward and upward to dump uke to the mat. It is a recognized Kodokan judo technique but has been banned in IJF competition since 2010 due to safety concerns about head-down attacks. It remains valid in many non-IJF competitions, kata training, and dojo practice.","phases":[{"label":"Kuzushi · Off-balance","text":"Uke's balance is broken forward and upward initially to distract them, then tori shoots low and backward underneath uke's center of gravity. The takedown creates kuzushi by removing both legs as support simultaneously. A fake or attack to the upper body is often used to draw uke's attention high before the double-leg shot."},{"label":"Tsukuri · Entry","text":"From a standing position, drive off the rear foot and shoot forward and low — drop one knee to the mat and shoot the lead knee between uke's feet. Both arms wrap around uke's thighs, gripping firmly just above the back of the knees. Keep the head up and to the side of uke's body (not driving into uke's midsection with the head)."},{"label":"Kake · Execution","text":"Once the thighs are gripped, drive forward and upward with the legs and back while pulling uke's thighs toward you. Simultaneously step or drive forward with your lead leg. The combination of forward drive and thigh pull lifts and tips uke backward to the mat."}],"steps":[{"title":"Create an upper body distraction","text":"Feint an upper-body attack, push-pull with grips, or wait for uke's attention to be on their upper body before shooting."},{"title":"Shoot low","text":"Drop your level explosively and shoot forward — lead knee drops near uke's feet. Keep head up. Drive off the back foot with power."},{"title":"Wrap both thighs","text":"Wrap both arms around uke's thighs just above the back of the knees. Pull the thighs tightly toward your chest."},{"title":"Drive forward and up","text":"Use leg drive to stand up and drive forward simultaneously. Your legs and back drive the power, not just your arms."},{"title":"Dump uke to the mat","text":"As uke tips backward, step through with your lead leg and lower them to the mat under control. Transition to a hold-down."}],"principles":["Head position is critical for safety — keep the head up and to the side, never driving the crown of the head into uke's midsection.","The shot must be explosive and low. A slow, gradual entry gives uke time to sprawl or counter. Speed and penetration depth are key.","Grip the thighs above the back of the knees for maximum leverage. Gripping too low or at the ankles reduces lift power.","Note: Morote-Gari is banned in IJF competition. Know the rule set before applying in shiai."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Driving the head into uke's stomach","fix":"Keep the head up and to the side throughout. Head-down entries are dangerous and illegal in IJF competition."},{"mistake":"Gripping too low (ankles or calves)","fix":"Grip above the back of the knees — at the thighs. Low grips have limited leverage and uke can hop backward to escape."},{"mistake":"Shooting from too far away","fix":"Close the distance with a step before shooting. Shooting from too far leaves you extended and vulnerable to knee strikes or guillotines."},{"mistake":"Using only arm strength on the drive","fix":"Power comes from the legs standing up, not arm pulling. Stand up through the drive — use your full body to complete the takedown."}],"when":"Morote-Gari is effective in non-IJF competition contexts, kata practice, and self-defense. It is most effective when uke is upright and not anticipating a low attack. Note that Morote-Gari is banned in IJF international competition (since 2010) due to the head-down posture required and risk of neck injury. Practitioners should be aware of the specific competition rules governing the event they participate in.","setups":["o-uchi-gari"],"followUps":["kesa-gatame"],"counters":["tani-otoshi"],"related":["sukui-nage","kibisu-gaeshi","uki-otoshi"],"source":"https://judolearn.com/techniques/morote-gari/"},{"id":"nami-juji-jime","name":"Nami-Juji-Jime","kanji":"並十字絞","translation":"Normal Cross Choke","category":"Katame-Waza","subcategory":"Shime-Waza","difficulty":2,"grade":"3rd kyu","grades":["3rd kyu","2nd kyu"],"videos":[{"yt":"k2cHry9HByQ","title":"並十字絞 /  Nami-juji-jime","channel":"KODOKAN"},{"yt":"1KEQlJK9poI","title":"Nami-Juji-Jime, Kata-Juji-Jime, And Gyaku-Juji-Jime Tips | Riki Judo Dojo","channel":"Riki Judo Dojo"},{"yt":"258m1Wv8TL8","title":"Nami Juji Jime Judo - Strong turn over from all fours","channel":"The Judo Coach"}],"overview":"Nami-Juji-Jime is the standard cross-lapel choke applied from mount or a guard position. Both hands grip the opposite collars, and the forearms cross at uke's throat to compress the carotids. Together with Gyaku-Juji-Jime (reverse cross choke) and Kata-Juji-Jime (half cross choke), it forms the fundamental Juji-Jime family. It is heavily used in BJJ as \"cross collar choke.\"","phases":[],"steps":[{"title":"Establish mount or control position","text":"Nami-Juji-Jime is most commonly applied from mount (tori sitting on uke's chest) or from a guard-pass position."},{"title":"Insert your right hand deeply into uke's left collar","text":"Slide your right hand palm-up deep into uke's left collar. Your four fingers are inside the collar, thumb outside. This hand will become the primary choke arm."},{"title":"Insert your left hand into uke's right collar","text":"Cross your left arm over your right arm and insert your left hand into uke's right collar. This is the \"cross\" — both arms are now crossing in front of uke's throat."},{"title":"Close the choke by squeezing elbows together","text":"Pull your elbows down and together. This drives your forearms (and the crossing collar material) into both sides of uke's neck, compressing the carotids."},{"title":"Apply final pressure","text":"Lean your body weight forward to increase the choke pressure. The crossing forearms compress uke's carotid arteries on both sides simultaneously."}],"principles":["Palm-up grip on the first hand allows the forearm to contact the carotid more effectively.","The deeper the collar grip, the more leverage and carotid contact you have.","Both arms cross at the wrist/forearm — the crossing point should be at uke's throat.","Squeezing the elbows inward (not just the hands) creates the maximum choking pressure."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Shallow collar grip","fix":"Drive your hand as deep as possible into the collar. Shallow grip reduces the forearm contact on the carotid."},{"mistake":"Pulling outward instead of squeezing elbows inward","fix":"The pressure comes from driving the elbows down and together. Pulling the hands outward just stretches the collar without compressing the neck."},{"mistake":"Pressure on the windpipe, not the carotids","fix":"The forearms should contact the sides of the neck, not the front. Adjust your hand position so you feel carotid contact."}],"when":"Nami-Juji-Jime is most commonly applied from mount or after passing the guard. It is a first-choice choke when you have both collars available. Pairs with Gyaku-Juji-Jime — if uke defends one collar, switch grip to the other variant.","setups":[],"followUps":["juji-gatame","hadaka-jime"],"counters":[],"related":["okuri-eri-jime","hadaka-jime","juji-gatame"],"source":"https://judolearn.com/techniques/nami-juji-jime/"},{"id":"o-goshi","name":"O-Goshi","kanji":"大腰","translation":"Major Hip Throw","category":"Nage-Waza","subcategory":"Koshi-Waza","difficulty":1,"grade":"6th kyu","grades":["6th kyu","5th kyu"],"videos":[{"yt":"yhu1mfy2vJ4","title":"大腰 / O-goshi","channel":"KODOKAN"},{"yt":"VLYKx-Fwhxg","title":"O-goshi - Basic principles","channel":"Efficient Judo"},{"yt":"O1HFxNk7Tr8","title":"Quick Tips to Speed Up Your Ogoshi","channel":"Shintaro Higashi"},{"yt":"35qAGGn_mes","title":"Top 10 O-Goshi / Tsuri-Goshi Compilation Highlights | 大腰","channel":"Judo Spirit"}],"overview":"O-Goshi is the foundational hip throw of judo and one of the first techniques taught to beginners worldwide. Tori wraps their arm around uke's waist, pivots to place their hip in front of uke's hips, and uses the hip as a fulcrum to lever uke over and to the mat. It teaches the essential mechanics of koshi-waza: entry, hip placement, and leverage.","phases":[{"label":"Kuzushi · Off-balance","text":"Pull uke forward and slightly upward with your sleeve grip, breaking their balance to their right front corner. Raise their arm upward to tilt their body and get them rising onto their toes."},{"label":"Tsukuri · Entry","text":"Step your right foot in front of and just inside uke's right foot, then pivot on it to bring your left foot around so your back faces uke's front. As you enter, wrap your right arm firmly around uke's waist. Your right hip must be positioned in front of and against uke's right hip — the contact point is critical."},{"label":"Kake · Execution","text":"Bend forward at the waist, pulling uke's sleeve arm sharply downward while keeping the waist arm grip firm. Your hip acts as the fulcrum — uke's weight pivots over it. Straighten your legs simultaneously to assist loading uke's weight onto your hip. Uke is projected forward to the mat."}],"steps":[{"title":"Pull uke forward to break balance (kuzushi)","text":"Use your sleeve grip to pull uke's arm upward and toward their right front corner. Your lapel hand lifts. Uke should lean onto their toes."},{"title":"Step your right foot in with toes pointed forward","text":"Step your right foot in front of and just inside uke's right foot. Your toes point in the same direction as uke's."},{"title":"Pivot and wrap the waist","text":"Pivot on your right foot and swing your left foot around to complete the body turn. Simultaneously slide your right arm around uke's waist and grip firmly. Your back must be against uke's chest."},{"title":"Position your hip as the fulcrum","text":"Your right hip should press firmly into uke's lower abdomen/hip area. Bend your knees slightly so your hip level is lower than uke's hip, then straighten them to load uke's weight onto your hip."},{"title":"Project uke over the hip","text":"Bend forward at the waist while pulling uke's sleeve arm sharply downward. Uke's body pivots over your hip and they land on the mat in front of you."}],"principles":["The hip contact must be firm and direct — your hip against uke's hip is the lever. Any gap reduces effectiveness.","Waist arm grip: wrap deeply around uke's midsection, not just touching their back.","Bend forward during kake to complete the lever action — remaining upright blocks the rotation.","O-Goshi teaches the template for all koshi-waza: enter, place hip below uke's hip, load, project."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Hip placed too low (around uke's thighs)","fix":"Your hip should contact uke at their hip level. Too low and you cannot lever them; too high and they simply step over you."},{"mistake":"Not bending forward during kake","fix":"The throw requires you to bow forward. If you stay upright, uke simply gets lifted — not thrown."},{"mistake":"Waist arm grip too loose","fix":"Pull uke tightly into your back. A loose waist grip allows uke to create space and escape the throw."},{"mistake":"Feet too close together during entry","fix":"Your feet should be roughly shoulder-width apart after the pivot. Too narrow and you have no base; too wide and you cannot generate hip drive."}],"when":"O-Goshi is most effective against shorter or same-height opponents where hip-level leverage is accessible. It is a foundational learning throw — at competitive levels it is often replaced by Harai-Goshi or Uchi-Mata, which require less full-body contact. However, O-Goshi remains valid as a combination element and against ukes with an upright, rigid posture.","setups":[],"followUps":["ko-uchi-gari","de-ashi-barai"],"counters":["tani-otoshi","ura-nage"],"related":["harai-goshi","uchi-mata","koshi-guruma"],"source":"https://judolearn.com/techniques/o-goshi/"},{"id":"o-guruma","name":"O-Guruma","kanji":"大車","translation":"Large Wheel","category":"Nage-Waza","subcategory":"Ashi-Waza","difficulty":2,"grade":"3rd kyu","grades":["3rd kyu","2nd kyu"],"videos":[{"yt":"SnZciTAY9vc","title":"大車 / O-guruma","channel":"KODOKAN"},{"yt":"20Tiwr0eHEw","title":"How to do O Guruma | Major Wheel | 大車","channel":"Sampson Judo"},{"yt":"XEULdkGNsaA","title":"O Guruma Master Class","channel":"Shintaro Higashi"},{"yt":"vR-DQwOdPFQ","title":"O-Guruma Tips | Riki Judo Dojo","channel":"Riki Judo Dojo"}],"overview":"O-Guruma is a large wheel throw where tori sweeps their leg across both of uke's thighs simultaneously — creating a wheel-like fulcrum that throws uke in a large rotational arc. Unlike Harai-Goshi which sweeps one leg, O-Guruma places the leg across both of uke's thighs at hip level, using the double contact as a powerful lever.","phases":[{"label":"Kuzushi · Off-balance","text":"Pull uke strongly forward and upward onto their right front corner. The sleeve grip elevates and pulls uke's arm forward; the lapel grip pulls their chest forward. Strong forward kuzushi is essential because O-Guruma requires uke to be fully committed forward before the leg is placed as the wheel."},{"label":"Tsukuri · Entry","text":"Step your right foot in between or just outside uke's feet and pivot, similar to a hip throw entry. As you pivot, your back faces uke's chest. Bend the standing leg to lower your hips. Your right leg is then extended backward and placed across both of uke's upper thighs — the leg rests across uke like a horizontal bar."},{"label":"Kake · Execution","text":"Your extended right leg acts as the wheel axle across uke's thighs. Pull both arms forward and downward in a large arc. Simultaneously rotate your upper body forward. Uke's legs are blocked by your leg while their upper body continues forward — they rotate over the leg-wheel in a large arc and land on the mat."}],"steps":[{"title":"Create strong forward kuzushi","text":"Pull uke's sleeve arm up and forward. Lapel hand drives uke's chest forward. Uke must be fully loaded forward with weight on their toes."},{"title":"Step in and pivot","text":"Step your right foot in between uke's feet and pivot to face the same direction as uke. Keep your knees bent."},{"title":"Place the right leg as the wheel","text":"Extend your right leg backward and place it across both of uke's upper thighs. Your leg contacts uke across both legs simultaneously at hip height."},{"title":"Pull arms in a forward arc","text":"Pull both arms forward and downward in a large circular arc — sleeve arm pulling uke's arm forward and down, lapel arm pulling the chest."},{"title":"Complete the rotational throw","text":"Uke's legs are blocked by your extended leg while their upper body rotates forward over it. They land in a large arc on the mat in front of you."}],"principles":["The leg placement is the defining feature — it must contact both thighs simultaneously at a consistent height. Placement on only one thigh becomes Harai-Goshi.","The throw is rotational, not a forward dump. The arm pull should describe a large arc — uke wheels over the leg.","Strong forward kuzushi before the leg is placed is essential. The leg acts as a stationary wheel only if uke is already committed forward.","Keep the standing leg slightly bent during kake to maintain balance as uke's weight loads onto your extended leg."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Placing the leg on only one of uke's thighs","fix":"Both thighs must be contacted simultaneously. Single-thigh contact is Harai-Goshi mechanics, not O-Guruma. Ensure the leg sweeps fully across both legs."},{"mistake":"Placing the leg too low (across the knees)","fix":"The leg should contact across the upper thighs, not at knee height. Knee-level placement reduces leverage and can injure uke's knee joint."},{"mistake":"Insufficient kuzushi before placing the leg","fix":"If uke is not loaded forward when the leg is placed, they simply push backward against your leg. Establish full forward kuzushi first."},{"mistake":"Short, downward arm pull instead of a large arc","fix":"The arm pull must be a large forward arc to create the rotation. A short downward pull just bends uke without wheeling them over."}],"when":"O-Guruma is effective against opponents who have a wide stance or who advance with both feet simultaneously, making the double-thigh leg placement accessible. It is used when tori wants the leverage of a double-thigh wheel rather than a single-leg sweep. In competition it can be used against opponents who defend hip throws by spreading their legs.","setups":["o-uchi-gari","de-ashi-barai"],"followUps":[],"counters":["tani-otoshi","ura-nage"],"related":["harai-goshi","uchi-mata","seoi-otoshi"],"source":"https://judolearn.com/techniques/o-guruma/"},{"id":"o-soto-guruma","name":"O-Soto-Guruma","kanji":"大外車","translation":"Large Outer Wheel","category":"Nage-Waza","subcategory":"Ashi-Waza","difficulty":2,"grade":"3rd kyu","grades":["3rd kyu","2nd kyu"],"videos":[{"yt":"92KbCm6pQeI","title":"大外車 / O-soto-guruma","channel":"KODOKAN"},{"yt":"_RDpjItQ0hM","title":"How Osoto and Ashi Garuma can be used simultaneously, depending on uke’s reaction.","channel":"Nick Yonezuka - Cranford JKC"},{"yt":"pUxARErOz3o","title":"O-Soto-Guruma Tips | Riki Judo Dojo","channel":"Riki Judo Dojo"}],"overview":"O-Soto-Guruma is a large outer wheel throw that extends the principle of Osoto-Gari to sweep both of uke's legs simultaneously from behind. Where Osoto-Gari reaps one leg, O-Soto-Guruma's sweeping leg reaches across both of uke's legs — wheeling them both — creating a larger, more powerful rotational throw. It is effective against opponents who defend Osoto-Gari by shifting weight to the far leg.","phases":[{"label":"Kuzushi · Off-balance","text":"Drive uke's upper body backward and to their right rear corner. The lapel grip pushes uke's collar backward while the sleeve grip pulls upward and away. Uke must be tipped backward with weight distributing to both legs — this is the setup for the double-leg sweep."},{"label":"Tsukuri · Entry","text":"Step your left foot in close to the outside of uke's right foot, positioning yourself at uke's right side. Your chest is against uke's right shoulder and chest. The sweeping leg — your right leg — is loaded back and ready to sweep through both of uke's legs from behind and outside."},{"label":"Kake · Execution","text":"Drive your right leg in a large sweeping arc from behind and through both of uke's legs — sweeping both legs simultaneously in a wheel-like motion. Your upper body continues driving forward and downward to project uke backward. The combination of the double-leg sweep and the upper body push dumps uke straight onto their back."}],"steps":[{"title":"Create backward kuzushi","text":"Push uke's upper body backward with the collar grip. Sleeve grip assists. Uke's weight shifts backward across both legs."},{"title":"Step in close to the outside","text":"Step your left foot in beside uke's right foot, positioning your body at uke's right side. Keep your chest pressing against uke."},{"title":"Load the sweeping leg","text":"Bring your right leg back and coil it for the sweeping action. The leg must swing from far behind to sweep both of uke's legs."},{"title":"Sweep both legs simultaneously","text":"Drive your right leg in a large sweeping arc from behind, through both of uke's legs simultaneously. The sweep must contact both legs."},{"title":"Drive forward and project uke backward","text":"As the sweep occurs, press your upper body forward and downward. Uke falls straight backward to the mat."}],"principles":["The sweep must catch both of uke's legs simultaneously — catching only one is Osoto-Gari mechanics.","The sweeping arc is large — from far behind and sweeping fully through both legs. A short sweep cannot reach the far leg.","Upper body pressure must be continuous during the sweep. If you lean back, uke's forward defense blocks the throw.","O-Soto-Guruma counters the Osoto-Gari defense of shifting weight to the far leg — by sweeping both legs you cover both options."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Sweeping only one leg — becoming Osoto-Gari","fix":"The sweeping leg must reach far enough to catch both legs. Swing through a larger arc, starting the sweep from further behind."},{"mistake":"Insufficient backward kuzushi","fix":"Uke's weight must be backward before the sweep. If uke is upright, they resist the double sweep easily."},{"mistake":"Body position too far from uke","fix":"Stay close — chest to shoulder contact with uke. Distance reduces the effectiveness of both the sweep and the upper body drive."},{"mistake":"Sweeping with the shin instead of the thigh","fix":"The contact should be your thigh sweeping through uke's legs. Shin contact is weaker and less precise."}],"when":"O-Soto-Guruma is used when uke defends Osoto-Gari by shifting their weight to their far leg — because O-Soto-Guruma's sweep reaches both legs, it covers this defense. It is also effective against opponents with a wide stance. Use it in combination after a failed Osoto-Gari when uke has spread their base.","setups":["o-uchi-gari"],"followUps":["osoto-gari"],"counters":["tani-otoshi"],"related":["osoto-gari","o-soto-otoshi","ko-soto-gari","ko-soto-gake"],"source":"https://judolearn.com/techniques/o-soto-guruma/"},{"id":"o-soto-makikomi","name":"O-Soto-Makikomi","kanji":"大外巻込","translation":"Large Outer Wraparound","category":"Nage-Waza","subcategory":"Yoko-Sutemi-Waza","difficulty":2,"grade":"3rd kyu","grades":["3rd kyu","2nd kyu"],"videos":[{"yt":"DGDv2oMwmas","title":"大外巻込 / O-soto-makikomi","channel":"KODOKAN"},{"yt":"2Wvrcs7kkMU","title":"O SOTO MAKIKOMI Georgian Grip","channel":"welcomematstevescott"},{"yt":"-CZO8Mj5cA8","title":"Osoto-makikomi - Demo","channel":"Efficient Judo"}],"overview":"O-Soto-Makikomi is a variation of Osoto-Gari where tori wraps (maki) uke's arm and falls to the side during the outer reap, creating a wraparound sacrifice. The wrapping of uke's arm and the simultaneous side fall combine the reaping action of Osoto-Gari with the sacrifice mechanics of yoko-sutemi-waza, creating a powerful, difficult-to-escape throw.","phases":[{"label":"Kuzushi · Off-balance","text":"Create backward kuzushi — push uke onto their rear heel with the lapel grip while the sleeve is wrapped. Uke's weight shifts backward, setting up the outer reap. The wrapped arm adds additional control over uke's upper body during the throw."},{"label":"Tsukuri · Entry","text":"Step in to uke's right side as for Osoto-Gari. Simultaneously, trap uke's right arm by wrapping your left arm around it (makikomi — winding in). Your chest presses against uke's chest and you are close enough to initiate both the reap and the side fall."},{"label":"Kake · Execution","text":"Execute the outer reap while simultaneously falling to the side — the sacrifice element. The wrapped arm traps uke and prevents them from posting or blocking with that arm. The combination of the reap and the side fall drives uke powerfully to the mat."}],"steps":[{"title":"Create backward kuzushi","text":"Push uke backward onto their right rear heel. Weight loads backward."},{"title":"Step in and wrap the arm","text":"Step in to uke's right side. Simultaneously trap uke's right arm by wrapping your left arm around it — pulling it tight against your chest."},{"title":"Position for the reap","text":"Load your reaping leg back and position your body close to uke for the Osoto-style outer reap."},{"title":"Reap and fall simultaneously","text":"Execute the outer reap of the back of uke's right thigh while falling to the side — the sacrifice. Both actions happen together."},{"title":"Drive uke to the mat","text":"The combination of reap and side fall drives uke powerfully to the mat. The wrapped arm prevents uke from defending with that arm."}],"principles":["The arm wrap (makikomi) is what distinguishes this from standard Osoto-Gari. The wrap traps uke's arm and adds sacrifice mechanics.","The reap and the side fall must be simultaneous. Reaping first then falling, or falling first then reaping, reduces power.","The arm wrap must be tight — uke's arm trapped against tori's chest. A loose wrap can slip and uke regains arm control.","Makikomi throws are powerful and carry some risk to uke's shoulder — practice with care and ensure uke has good ukemi."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Wrapping loosely — arm slips free","fix":"Pull uke's arm firmly against your chest as you wrap. The arm should be fully controlled throughout."},{"mistake":"Reaping without falling — standard Osoto-Gari","fix":"The sacrifice element must be present. Fall to the side during the reap to activate the makikomi mechanics."},{"mistake":"Falling before the reap connects","fix":"The reap and fall are simultaneous. Falling without the reap creating contact means you land beside uke rather than taking them with you."},{"mistake":"Insufficient backward kuzushi","fix":"Like Osoto-Gari, O-Soto-Makikomi requires uke's weight to be backward before the reap. Without kuzushi, uke counters easily."}],"when":"O-Soto-Makikomi is used when Osoto-Gari has been partially entered but uke is resisting the reap. Adding the arm wrap and falling creates a more committed throw that is harder to counter. It is also used when tori wants to ensure uke cannot post with the arm being wrapped.","setups":[],"followUps":["osoto-gari","kesa-gatame"],"counters":["tani-otoshi"],"related":["osoto-gari","o-soto-guruma","harai-makikomi","uchi-mata-makikomi"],"source":"https://judolearn.com/techniques/o-soto-makikomi/"},{"id":"o-soto-otoshi","name":"O-Soto-Otoshi","kanji":"大外落","translation":"Major Outer Drop","category":"Nage-Waza","subcategory":"Te-Waza","difficulty":1,"grade":"6th kyu","grades":["6th kyu","5th kyu","4th kyu"],"videos":[{"yt":"2DsVvDw7b8g","title":"大外落 / O-soto-otoshi","channel":"KODOKAN"},{"yt":"CYH7zR_yqq4","title":"Osoto-otoshi - Demo","channel":"Efficient Judo"},{"yt":"Ltj1d_ooC_E","title":"O SOTO OTOSHI","channel":"Sport-Management- System"},{"yt":"cgkY-n1kACk","title":"O-Soto-Otoshi Tips | Riki Judo Dojo","channel":"Riki Judo Dojo"}],"overview":"O-Soto-Otoshi is a major outer dropping throw closely related to Osoto-Gari. Where Osoto-Gari reaps the leg with a pendulum swing, O-Soto-Otoshi drops uke directly backward by blocking the outside of their leg and using body weight to push them over and down. The \"otoshi\" (drop) component means tori drives their body weight directly downward onto uke rather than reaping. This makes O-Soto-Otoshi more accessible for beginners as it relies on body weight rather than sweeping leg power.","phases":[{"label":"Kuzushi · Off-balance","text":"Step to uke's right side and pull their sleeve arm forward and upward while pushing the lapel arm over and to uke's right rear corner. Uke's weight should shift onto their right heel as they are pulled and pushed backward and to their right."},{"label":"Tsukuri · Entry","text":"Step your right foot to the outside of uke's right foot, placing it behind and to the outer side. Move your chest close to uke's chest. Your right leg is now outside uke's right leg. Begin to lean your upper body forward and into uke's upper body, driving their weight backward onto their right heel."},{"label":"Kake · Execution","text":"Push uke's upper body backward and down while simultaneously pressing your right leg against the back of uke's right thigh, blocking their leg from stepping backward to recover. Drop your own body weight forward and onto uke, driving them straight down and backward to the mat. The dropping body weight does the primary work."}],"steps":[{"title":"Step to the outside","text":"Step your right foot to the outside of uke's right foot, positioning your body to uke's right side with your chest facing theirs."},{"title":"Break uke's balance backward","text":"Pull uke's sleeve arm upward and forward while driving the lapel arm across uke's body toward their right rear. Shift their weight onto their right heel."},{"title":"Press your body against uke","text":"Drive your chest into uke's chest, leaning forward. Maintain the backward push with your arms while physically pressing uke backward with your torso."},{"title":"Block uke's retreating leg","text":"Place your right leg against the back of uke's right thigh. This blocks uke from stepping backward to recover balance."},{"title":"Drop uke backward","text":"Drive your body weight forward and downward, pushing uke over the blocked leg. Uke drops straight backward to the mat."}],"principles":["The \"drop\" is driven by body weight — lean your chest into uke and use gravity, not just arm strength.","The leg block prevents uke stepping backward; without it, uke simply retreats and the throw fails.","Break uke's balance to their right rear before entering — a balanced uke can absorb the forward push.","Stay tight to uke throughout; any distance between chests reduces the pressure needed to drop them."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Trying to reap the leg like Osoto-Gari","fix":"O-Soto-Otoshi is a drop, not a reap. The leg blocks uke's thigh and the body weight drives downward — there is no pendulum reap."},{"mistake":"Standing too far from uke during the drop","fix":"Your chest should be pressed against uke's chest. Distance reduces the forward-dropping pressure on uke."},{"mistake":"Blocking too low (knee or ankle instead of thigh)","fix":"The block should be against uke's upper thigh. Blocking at the knee puts torque on the joint; at the ankle, uke can step over it."},{"mistake":"Not establishing backward kuzushi first","fix":"Uke must be pushed backward before the drop. A forward-balanced uke will simply push you back."}],"when":"O-Soto-Otoshi is effective as a direct attack when uke is backing away or has been pushed backward. It works well in combination with forward-attacking throws — if uke resists a forward throw and pushes back, transitioning immediately to O-Soto-Otoshi exploits their backward momentum. It is also effective as a counter to uke stepping in for a hip throw.","setups":[],"followUps":["o-goshi","tai-otoshi"],"counters":["o-uchi-gari","ko-uchi-gari"],"related":["osoto-gari","tani-otoshi","ko-soto-gari"],"source":"https://judolearn.com/techniques/o-soto-otoshi/"},{"id":"o-uchi-gaeshi","name":"O-Uchi-Gaeshi","kanji":"大内返","translation":"Large Inner Counter","category":"Nage-Waza","subcategory":"Ashi-Waza","difficulty":3,"grade":"2nd kyu","grades":["2nd kyu","1st kyu"],"videos":[{"yt":"dCyZTXyjIXE","title":"大内返 / O-uchi-gaeshi","channel":"KODOKAN"},{"yt":"cd1WwHmG2FU","title":"O Uchi Gaeshi - Judo - O-Uchi-Gaeshi","channel":"Passion Judo"},{"yt":"V5jQkLDww7g","title":"Ouchi-gaeshi - Demo","channel":"Efficient Judo"}],"overview":"O-Uchi-Gaeshi is the counter-throw to O-Uchi-Gari. When uke hooks inward with their leg to perform O-Uchi-Gari, tori pivots outward away from the hooking leg — turning uke's inside attack into an outside exposure — and applies Ko-Soto-Gari or O-Soto-Gari to uke's now-extended supporting leg. The counter converts uke's inward leg attack into an outward leg attack on the vulnerable support. Part of the Kodokan Shinmeisho no Waza.","phases":[{"label":"Kuzushi · Off-balance","text":"Uke's commitment to the O-Uchi-Gari reap locks their weight and attention into the inward hooking motion. As their reaping leg drives inward and their body leans into the attack, their supporting leg becomes the sole base — bearing all their weight and unguarded. The pivot and counter exploit this moment of single-leg dependence."},{"label":"Tsukuri · Entry","text":"As uke's reaping leg hooks inward between your legs (or against your inner thigh), pivot your body outward — rotating on your stable foot away from the hook direction. This rotation moves you to the outside of uke's attack and positions your reaping leg to attack uke's supporting leg from the outside."},{"label":"Kake · Execution","text":"Apply Ko-Soto-Gari or O-Soto-Gari to uke's supporting leg — sweep or reap it from the outside-rear. Simultaneously pull uke's upper body forward and downward with your grips. Uke's single supporting leg is swept away while their torso is directed downward."}],"steps":[{"title":"Detect uke's O-Uchi-Gari hook","text":"Feel uke's leg driving inward and their body weight shifting into the attack. The grip tension will increase on the side of the attacking leg as uke prepares to load the reap."},{"title":"Pivot outward on your stable foot","text":"As uke's leg hooks inward, pivot your body outward — rotating away from the hook. Your foot on the attacked side turns outward, moving your body to uke's outside."},{"title":"Position your leg behind uke's supporting leg","text":"The outward pivot brings you to uke's outside, where uke's supporting leg is exposed. Position your attacking leg to sweep or reap from behind or the outside."},{"title":"Apply Ko-Soto-Gari or O-Soto-Gari to uke's support","text":"Sweep uke's supporting leg outward (Ko-Soto-Gari) or reap it from behind (O-Soto-Gari). The mechanics follow the same principles as those primary techniques."},{"title":"Pull uke's upper body to complete the throw","text":"Simultaneously direct uke's torso forward or diagonally downward with your grips as you sweep. The combination of the leg sweep and upper body direction causes uke to fall."}],"principles":["The pivot outward is the defining action — it converts uke's inward attack into an opportunity for an outward counter.","Uke's supporting leg is the target, not the reaping leg. The reaping leg is mobile and bears no weight.","The counter applies the mechanics of Ko-Soto-Gari or O-Soto-Gari — techniques that should already be well-understood.","Speed of recognition matters: the window is the moment uke's reaping leg is committed inward and cannot quickly return.","The upper-body pull must coordinate with the leg sweep. A leg sweep without upper-body direction is far less effective."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Stepping inward (toward the hook) instead of pivoting outward","fix":"The counter requires moving to the outside. Moving inward into the hook allows O-Uchi-Gari to succeed."},{"mistake":"Targeting uke's reaping leg instead of the supporting leg","fix":"Focus on the supporting leg — it bears all weight and is the actual target. The reaping leg is in the air and has no base."},{"mistake":"Pivot too slow — uke withdraws the reap before the counter lands","fix":"The outward pivot must begin the instant uke's reap is detected. A slow pivot allows uke to abort the attack and reset."},{"mistake":"No arm control during the counter sweep","fix":"Pull uke's upper body in the direction of the sweep. Leg sweep without arm coordination is partial and less reliable."}],"when":"O-Uchi-Gaeshi is most effective against opponents who rely heavily on O-Uchi-Gari as a primary attack. The outward pivot pairs especially well if uke overextends their hooking leg or drives too far with their body weight. It can also be applied against Ko-Uchi-Gari attempts where the inward hook mechanics are similar. In competition, establishing this counter changes uke's risk calculus for repeated inner-leg attacks.","setups":["o-uchi-gari"],"followUps":["ko-soto-gari"],"counters":[],"related":["o-uchi-gari","ko-soto-gari"],"source":"https://judolearn.com/techniques/o-uchi-gaeshi/"},{"id":"o-uchi-gari","name":"O-Uchi-Gari","kanji":"大内刈","translation":"Major Inner Reap","category":"Nage-Waza","subcategory":"Ashi-Waza","difficulty":1,"grade":"6th kyu","grades":["6th kyu","5th kyu"],"videos":[{"yt":"0itJFhV9pDQ","title":"大内刈 / O-uchi-gari","channel":"KODOKAN"},{"yt":"I3BWf1ZoIuc","title":"Ouchi-gari - Basic principles","channel":"Efficient Judo"},{"yt":"tYP8JjuXClk","title":"ouchi gari in depth","channel":"Shintaro Higashi"},{"yt":"WOtrX2DCr34","title":"Top 10 Ouchi Gari Highlights Compilation  大内刈","channel":"Judo Spirit"}],"overview":"O-Uchi-Gari is a major inner leg reap — one of the foundational judo throws and a fixture in high-level competition. Tori drives uke backward and off-balance, then sweeps the inside of uke's leg with a hooking reap. It pairs naturally with Osoto-Gari to form one of judo's most effective combination attacks.","phases":[{"label":"Kuzushi · Off-balance","text":"Drive uke backward and to their right rear corner. Your lapel hand pushes uke's chest upward and backward. Your sleeve hand pulls upward. Uke's weight must be on their right heel before you attack."},{"label":"Tsukuri · Entry","text":"Step your right foot inside uke's stance, roughly level with their right foot, positioning yourself to attack from an inside angle."},{"label":"Kake · Execution","text":"Swing your left leg in a hooking arc, driving the inner surface of your left calf into the back of uke's right calf/ankle area. Simultaneously, lean your upper body forward over uke and drive your lapel hand backward into their chest. The inner reap sweeps uke's right foot forward while your body pushes them backward."}],"steps":[{"title":"Push uke backward to their right rear corner (kuzushi)","text":"Drive your lapel hand into uke's chest and push them backward. Your sleeve arm pulls upward. Uke's weight shifts to their right heel."},{"title":"Step in with your right foot","text":"Plant your right foot to the inside-left of uke's right foot. You are now positioned between or beside uke's legs."},{"title":"Swing your left leg into the reap","text":"Hook your left leg behind uke's right leg, making contact with the inner surface of your left calf against the back of uke's calf/ankle. The action is a hooking reap, scooping uke's foot upward and forward."},{"title":"Drive your upper body forward over uke","text":"Lean your chest forward over uke as you reap. Your body weight drives them backward while the reap sweeps their leg forward — a scissoring action that is difficult to resist."},{"title":"Follow through and drive to the mat","text":"Carry through the reap and body drive until uke is fully on the mat. Maintain grip control throughout for potential transition to ne-waza."}],"principles":["The inner reap scoops uke's foot forward while the upper body drives them backward — this scissoring action is the core of the technique.","Backward kuzushi is mandatory. Inner reap against an upright uke is easily blocked — they just pick up the attacked leg.","The reaping leg hooks around the ankle/calf area, not the thigh. Contact too high allows uke to step over.","O-Uchi-Gari pairs naturally with Osoto-Gari: if uke defends one side (inner), switch to the other side (outer), and vice versa."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Attacking an upright uke with no backward kuzushi","fix":"Push them backward first. Without prior kuzushi, uke simply picks their leg up and you are off-balance."},{"mistake":"Not driving the upper body over uke during kake","fix":"Lean forward over uke while reaping. The scissoring action requires the body drive; the leg alone is not enough."},{"mistake":"Too narrow an entry — stepping between uke's feet and losing base","fix":"Step your base foot to a slight angle — your entry foot should be beside uke's right foot, not crossing past it. Drill the entry position until your weight naturally stays on the correct side."},{"mistake":"Reaping at the thigh (too high)","fix":"Target the calf or ankle. High contact is easily blocked — uke simply straightens their leg over your reap."}],"when":"O-Uchi-Gari is devastating as part of a combination with Osoto-Gari. Attack Osoto-Gari from the outside — if uke defends by shifting weight inward, immediately switch to O-Uchi-Gari on the opposite leg. Also effective when uke is stepping forward with their right leg (catching it mid-step when weight is transferring onto it).","setups":["seoi-nage","harai-goshi"],"followUps":["osoto-gari"],"counters":["o-uchi-gaeshi","seoi-nage","osoto-gari"],"related":["osoto-gari","ko-uchi-gari","ko-soto-gari","harai-goshi"],"source":"https://judolearn.com/techniques/o-uchi-gari/"},{"id":"obi-otoshi","name":"Obi-Otoshi","kanji":"帯落","translation":"Belt Drop","category":"Nage-Waza","subcategory":"Te-Waza","difficulty":3,"grade":"1st kyu","grades":["1st kyu","1st dan"],"videos":[{"yt":"ff8U2TVZIYI","title":"帯落 / Obi-otoshi","channel":"KODOKAN"},{"yt":"3dGTN6sGbmk","title":"Obi-otoshi - Demo","channel":"Efficient Judo"},{"yt":"RAYZmXiprv0","title":"OBI OTOSHI The Belt Drop Throw","channel":"welcomematstevescott"}],"overview":"Obi-Otoshi is an advanced te-waza where tori grips uke's belt and uses that grip — combined with a body drop or leg action — to project uke to the mat. The belt grip gives exceptional control over uke's hips and center of mass, but establishing the grip requires overcoming uke's defensive reactions. It is a rare technique seen primarily in kata and advanced randori.","phases":[{"label":"Kuzushi · Off-balance","text":"Kuzushi is created through the belt grip itself — pulling uke's hips sharply forward and upward breaks their balance to the front. Combined with a simultaneous pulling action on the sleeve or collar, uke is driven onto their toes and forward. The belt grip positions tori's pulling force directly at uke's center of mass."},{"label":"Tsukuri · Entry","text":"Secure the belt grip — typically with the dominant hand reaching under and around to grip the back of uke's belt, or gripping the belt at the side. The other hand maintains collar or sleeve control. Entry involves lowering the body and positioning the hips or leg to serve as a fulcrum or blocking point."},{"label":"Kake · Execution","text":"Drive the belt grip upward and forward, simultaneously pulling the collar grip to create rotation. If using a leg or hip as a fulcrum, the combination of belt drive and blocking lever projects uke over. The throw can be completed as a hip-type throw using the belt grip as the key control, or as a pure hand technique driving uke forward."}],"steps":[{"title":"Establish belt grip","text":"Reach around uke to grip the back or side of uke's belt firmly. The grip should be deep — as close to the knot as possible for maximum control."},{"title":"Create forward kuzushi","text":"Pull the belt grip upward and forward, simultaneously using your other grip to destabilize uke's upper body. Uke's hips tip forward."},{"title":"Position the throwing fulcrum","text":"Step in or position your hip or thigh as a blocking point against uke's body. The belt-grip side of your body provides the lever."},{"title":"Drive the belt and pull simultaneously","text":"Pull the belt sharply upward and forward while driving your body rotation. The free arm controls uke's upper body direction."},{"title":"Project to the mat","text":"Uke's hips are pulled over the fulcrum point and they land on the mat in front of or beside tori."}],"principles":["The belt grip is only as effective as its depth — a shallow grip on the edge of the belt can slip. Grip deeply as close to the knot as possible.","The belt grip bypasses uke's sleeve and lapel defenses, acting directly on the center of mass. This is its primary advantage.","Obi-Otoshi requires excellent timing and proximity — establishing the belt grip takes time, so it is often applied after a prior technique creates the opening.","As an advanced technique, it benefits from mastery of hip throw mechanics, as the belt-grip entry shares principles with koshi-waza."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Gripping the edge of the belt — grip slips","fix":"Reach deeply around uke and grip as close to the belt knot as possible. A secure, deep grip is the foundation of the technique."},{"mistake":"No secondary grip control","fix":"Always use the free hand for collar or sleeve control. The belt grip alone without secondary control allows uke to post out and escape."},{"mistake":"Attempting Obi-Otoshi without sufficient proximity","fix":"You must be very close to uke to establish the belt grip. Use gripping exchanges or combination attacks to close the distance first."},{"mistake":"Pulling the belt sideways instead of upward and forward","fix":"The belt pull must be upward and toward uke's front. A sideways pull simply rotates them without lifting off their base."}],"when":"Obi-Otoshi is most practical when uke's grip defenses focus on the lapel and sleeve, leaving the belt accessible. It is used by experienced practitioners in randori as a surprise element or as a kata demonstration technique. It is rarely seen at competition level due to the time required to establish the belt grip, but it is a valuable element of advanced judo curriculum.","setups":[],"followUps":["o-goshi","harai-goshi"],"counters":["tani-otoshi"],"related":["uki-otoshi","seoi-otoshi","tsurikomi-goshi"],"source":"https://judolearn.com/techniques/obi-otoshi/"},{"id":"okuri-ashi-barai","name":"Okuri-Ashi-Barai","kanji":"送足払","translation":"Following Foot Sweep","category":"Nage-Waza","subcategory":"Ashi-Waza","difficulty":2,"grade":"4th kyu","grades":["4th kyu","3rd kyu","2nd kyu"],"videos":[{"yt":"nw1ZdRjrdRI","title":"送足払 / Okuri-ashi-harai","channel":"KODOKAN"},{"yt":"Q2SU_CZUNFg","title":"How to do a Classic Okuri-Ashi-Barai","channel":"Shintaro Higashi"},{"yt":"43MBu_umkfc","title":"Korean Judo | Okuri ashi barai","channel":"Fighting Films"},{"yt":"fxJ_maZHZZQ","title":"Ouchi-gari into okuri-ashi-barai","channel":"Shintaro Higashi"}],"overview":"Okuri-Ashi-Barai is a sweeping foot technique executed against both of uke's feet simultaneously as they move laterally. Tori times the sweep to catch uke mid-step when both feet are momentarily close together — the \"following foot\" (the trailing foot moving to catch up) is swept to collide with or sweep the lead foot, taking both feet out and dropping uke laterally. It is considered one of the most elegant and timing-sensitive techniques in judo.","phases":[{"label":"Kuzushi · Off-balance","text":"Move uke laterally by pushing and pulling them to one side. As uke takes a lateral step, their lead foot lands and their trailing foot follows — at the moment the trailing foot is closing the gap, uke's weight is briefly distributed between both feet. This is the kuzushi moment: uke is neither fully on one foot nor the other."},{"label":"Tsukuri · Entry","text":"Move with uke to create the stepping pattern. As uke steps to their right, their right foot plants and their left foot begins to follow. Time your sweep to coincide with the moment the left foot is closing toward the right. Position your sweeping right foot with a flat, horizontal arc ready to contact the back of uke's ankle region."},{"label":"Kake · Execution","text":"Sweep your right foot in a flat, horizontal arc (like sweeping a floor) to contact the back of uke's left ankle as it moves toward the right foot. The sweep pushes uke's left foot into their right foot, sweeping both from under them. Simultaneously pull the sleeve arm downward and push the lapel arm upward to topple uke to their right side."}],"steps":[{"title":"Establish lateral motion with uke","text":"Move uke side-to-side using arm pressure and body movement. You need a rhythmic, predictable stepping pattern to time the sweep."},{"title":"Identify the \"following foot\" moment","text":"Watch for uke's trailing foot closing toward the lead foot. This brief window — when both feet are close together — is the target."},{"title":"Swing the sweeping foot into position","text":"As uke's trailing foot nears the lead foot, raise your sweeping foot and bring it in a wide, flat horizontal arc toward uke's ankle level."},{"title":"Execute the ankle-level sweep","text":"Contact the back of uke's trailing ankle with a brushing, sweeping motion — flat and parallel to the ground. Push the foot toward uke's other foot."},{"title":"Complete with coordinated arm action","text":"As the sweep makes contact, pull the sleeve arm downward and toward you while lifting and pushing the lapel arm upward. Uke drops laterally to the mat."}],"principles":["Timing is the entire technique — sweeping a planted, loaded foot is ineffective. Contact must happen when the foot is moving.","The sweep is flat and horizontal — like brushing a floor, not lifting a leg. Lifting results in a blocked, upward-deflecting foot.","Lateral motion must be pre-established; you cannot create the following-foot moment from a stationary position.","Arm action (sleeve pull, lapel push) directs uke's fall and must coordinate precisely with the sweep."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Sweeping too early — catching the planted foot","fix":"Wait for the following foot to be in motion before sweeping. Sweeping a planted foot meets resistance and fails."},{"mistake":"Sweeping with a lifting motion instead of a flat arc","fix":"The sweep should be parallel to the ground, like brushing a floor. Lifting the foot causes it to glance off uke's ankle without removing it."},{"mistake":"No coordinated arm action","fix":"The sweep alone will not drop uke — the sleeve pull and lapel push must direct uke's fall. Arm and leg must act together."},{"mistake":"Moving too slowly or hesitating","fix":"Okuri-Ashi-Barai requires commitment. Half-speed sweeps alert uke and give them time to adjust their footing."}],"when":"Okuri-Ashi-Barai is most effective when uke is moving laterally in a predictable pattern — common during circular movement (tai-sabaki) or when moving around the mat edge. It is excellent against opponents who take large lateral steps and is a staple in high-level competition as a set-up technique from circular movement.","setups":["harai-goshi","uchi-mata"],"followUps":[],"counters":["o-uchi-gari","ko-uchi-gari"],"related":["de-ashi-barai","harai-tsurikomi-ashi","hiza-guruma"],"source":"https://judolearn.com/techniques/okuri-ashi-barai/"},{"id":"okuri-eri-jime","name":"Okuri-Eri-Jime","kanji":"送襟絞","translation":"Sliding Lapel Choke","category":"Katame-Waza","subcategory":"Shime-Waza","difficulty":2,"grade":"3rd kyu","grades":["3rd kyu","2nd kyu"],"videos":[{"yt":"EiqyoVcIAi8","title":"送襟絞 /  Okuri-eri-jime","channel":"KODOKAN"},{"yt":"BFkasnJBSeg","title":"Okuri eri jime roll","channel":"Shintaro Higashi"},{"yt":"YMC83QFb3PU","title":"Okuri-Eri-Jime Tips | Riki Judo Dojo","channel":"Riki Judo Dojo"},{"yt":"ecgaAx9-3b8","title":"Okuri-eri-jime Compilation.","channel":"Ochiru"}],"overview":"Okuri-Eri-Jime is the most frequently applied choke in elite judo competition. Applied from behind uke, tori slides one forearm across uke's throat while the other arm drives the near collar into the side of uke's neck, creating a blood choke on the carotid arteries. It requires back control, which makes it highly efficient — the position itself is dominant.","phases":[],"steps":[{"title":"Establish back control","text":"Get behind uke with your chest against their back. Hook your legs around uke's hips (body triangle or hooks in)."},{"title":"Right arm over uke's right shoulder, grip deep into uke's left collar","text":"Reach your right arm over uke's right shoulder. Slide your right hand palm-up deep into uke's left collar. Your four fingers inside the collar, thumb outside. This is the primary choking arm."},{"title":"Left hand grips uke's right (near) collar","text":"Your left hand grips uke's right collar near the neck. This arm will drive the collar into uke's left carotid."},{"title":"Draw the right arm across the throat","text":"Pull your right arm across uke's throat so the wrist/radial bone area presses against uke's right carotid. The right arm is now the cross-throat arm."},{"title":"Squeeze both arms and arch back","text":"Pull your right arm toward you while driving your left hand away, pressing uke's right collar into their left carotid. Lean back to arch and tighten. Both carotids are compressed simultaneously."}],"principles":["Carotid compression on both sides simultaneously is the mechanism — this is a blood choke, not an air choke.","Back control is the prerequisite. You cannot apply this from in front of uke.","The left forearm (the crossing arm) should contact with the wrist/radial bone edge for maximum pressure on the carotid.","Extending your back (arching away from uke) increases the tightening force significantly."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Applying pressure to the throat (windpipe) instead of the carotids","fix":"This is a blood choke, not an air choke. Target the sides of the neck. Pressing on the windpipe is ineffective and unnecessarily uncomfortable."},{"mistake":"Not getting deep enough with the first hand into the collar","fix":"The deeper the first hand is in the collar, the more of the forearm contacts the neck."},{"mistake":"Not leaning back to tighten","fix":"Arching away from uke stretches the collar tight and increases pressure dramatically."}],"when":"Okuri-Eri-Jime is the primary weapon from back control in judo ne-waza. Any time you can establish back control — after a throw, during scrambles, or when uke is turtled — Okuri-Eri-Jime is the first-choice submission.","setups":[],"followUps":["juji-gatame"],"counters":[],"related":["hadaka-jime","nami-juji-jime","juji-gatame"],"source":"https://judolearn.com/techniques/okuri-eri-jime/"},{"id":"osoto-gaeshi","name":"Osoto-Gaeshi","kanji":"大外返","translation":"Large Outer Counter","category":"Nage-Waza","subcategory":"Ashi-Waza","difficulty":3,"grade":"2nd kyu","grades":["2nd kyu","1st kyu"],"videos":[{"yt":"8ZjM3X_EANo","title":"大外返 / O-soto-gaeshi","channel":"KODOKAN"},{"yt":"Vlsr5pfEurk","title":"O SOTO GAESHI","channel":"Judo Advantage"},{"yt":"d2zxyt2d_aw","title":"O SOTO GAESHI COUNTER TO O SOTO GARI","channel":"welcomematstevescott"},{"yt":"ULbgwvZhEE0","title":"O SOTO GAESHI","channel":"JBBA Judo Education Channel"}],"overview":"Osoto-Gaeshi is the counter-throw to O-Soto-Gari. When uke attempts O-Soto-Gari by driving their leg behind tori's and reaping, tori shifts their body weight onto the threatened (reaping-side) leg, steps around uke's committed attack, and applies their own O-Soto-Gari to uke's now-extended and weight-committed supporting leg. The principle is to absorb and redirect the attack force rather than resist it. Part of the Kodokan Shinmeisho no Waza, it exemplifies the judo concept of ju (yielding) applied to ashi-waza.","phases":[{"label":"Kuzushi · Off-balance","text":"Kuzushi comes from uke's own commitment to the O-Soto-Gari attack. As uke drives their leg behind tori and leans into the reap, their body weight shifts heavily to the supporting leg — which becomes unguarded. The moment of maximum attack commitment is simultaneously the moment of maximum counter-throw opportunity."},{"label":"Tsukuri · Entry","text":"As uke's reaping leg sweeps behind and uke leans forward into the throw, shift your weight onto the leg being targeted (rather than away from it). Step or pivot your body around and slightly behind uke, positioning your reaping leg to attack uke's committed supporting leg. Maintain your upright posture — do not collapse away from the attack."},{"label":"Kake · Execution","text":"Sweep or reap uke's supporting leg from the outside with your own leg (using O-Soto-Gari mechanics) while pulling uke's upper body forward or downward with your grips. Uke's weight is already committed to the supporting leg — your reap removes it. Direct uke's fall backward or to the side."}],"steps":[{"title":"Read and absorb uke's O-Soto-Gari attack","text":"As uke drives into the O-Soto-Gari, resist the instinct to step away from their reaping leg. Instead, remain upright and shift weight toward the uke's attacking side. Stepping away allows the reap to complete; absorbing it sets up the counter."},{"title":"Shift weight onto the targeted leg","text":"Transfer your body weight onto the leg uke is attempting to reap. This requires accepting that uke's leg is sweeping behind you while you remain stable on that leg."},{"title":"Step or pivot around to uke's outside","text":"Pivot or step your free leg around and behind uke to bring yourself to the outside of uke's supporting leg. Your body now positions for the counter-reap."},{"title":"Drive your reaping leg into uke's supporting leg","text":"Apply O-Soto-Gari mechanics to uke's now-exposed supporting leg — sweep from the outside and behind, with your reaping leg swinging through in a large arc."},{"title":"Pull uke's upper body to complete the throw","text":"Use your grips to pull uke's torso forward or diagonally downward as you reap, amplifying the destabilization created by removing their base leg. Uke falls backward."}],"principles":["The counter requires accepting the attack rather than fleeing from it. Stepping away from the reap validates the attack; stepping toward it sets up the counter.","Uke's moment of maximum commitment — both legs positioned for the reap — is the moment their support leg is most vulnerable.","The mechanics of the counter throw are identical to the original O-Soto-Gari — large outer reap, arm pull, body drive.","Speed of recognition is critical. The transition from absorbing the attack to launching the counter must be rapid enough that uke cannot retreat.","Body position during the counter must be upright — collapsing away from the attack removes the ability to counter."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Stepping away from uke's reaping leg","fix":"Move toward the attack, not away. Retreating allows the O-Soto-Gari to succeed. Shift weight onto the targeted leg instead."},{"mistake":"Counter-throwing too late — uke has already recovered","fix":"The window for the counter is the instant uke is fully committed. A half-second delay allows uke to pull their reaping leg back and reset."},{"mistake":"Losing upright posture when absorbing the attack","fix":"Maintain height and posture. Bending forward when uke attacks makes the counter reap from a mechanically weak position."},{"mistake":"Using arm strength alone without the full reap","fix":"The counter must include the leg sweep component. Pulling uke's arms without reaping their base leg is unlikely to complete the throw."}],"when":"Osoto-Gaeshi is most effective against opponents who commit heavily to O-Soto-Gari with a full body lean. The heavier uke's commitment, the more force is available for the counter. It is also applicable against O-Soto-Otoshi variations. In competition, drilling Osoto-Gaeshi as the automatic response to any O-Soto-Gari attack changes the risk calculus for opponents who rely on the technique.","setups":["osoto-gari","o-soto-otoshi"],"followUps":[],"counters":[],"related":["osoto-gari","o-soto-otoshi"],"source":"https://judolearn.com/techniques/osoto-gaeshi/"},{"id":"osoto-gari","name":"Osoto-Gari","kanji":"大外刈","translation":"Major Outer Reap","category":"Nage-Waza","subcategory":"Ashi-Waza","difficulty":1,"grade":"6th kyu","grades":["6th kyu","5th kyu"],"videos":[{"yt":"c-A_nP7mKAc","title":"大外刈 / O-soto-gari","channel":"KODOKAN"},{"yt":"KeIPk8O58zs","title":"Osoto-gari - Basic principles","channel":"Efficient Judo"},{"yt":"ommBYBxcCuE","title":"Osoto Gari's Most Important Detail","channel":"Shintaro Higashi"},{"yt":"x3cTRRspZ5M","title":"Top 10 Judo Osoto gari Compilation Highlights | 大外刈","channel":"Judo Spirit"}],"overview":"Osoto-Gari is one of the most recognized throws in judo — a powerful outer reap where tori hooks their leg behind uke's leg and drives them backward. It relies on breaking uke's backward balance and sweeping the supporting leg in a scythe-like motion. It is among the most frequently scored techniques in elite judo competition.","phases":[{"label":"Kuzushi · Off-balance","text":"Push uke backward and to their right rear corner. Your lapel hand (left) drives forward into uke's chest at throat level, while your sleeve hand (right) pulls upward. Uke's weight must shift back onto their right heel — this is the critical prerequisite."},{"label":"Tsukuri · Entry","text":"Step your left foot to the outside of uke's right foot, planting it firmly. Lean your chest forward into uke to maintain the backward pressure. Your right leg is free to sweep."},{"label":"Kake · Execution","text":"Swing your right leg forward (pendulum) and then powerfully back, hooking the back of uke's right thigh with the back of your right thigh. Simultaneously drive your chest forward and down into uke, and pull the sleeve arm upward. The reaping action and the forward body drive together topple uke backward."}],"steps":[{"title":"Push uke backward to their right rear corner (kuzushi)","text":"Drive your left lapel hand forward into uke's chest and upward, while pulling their sleeve arm up. Uke must be tipping backward with weight on their right heel before you step in."},{"title":"Step your left foot outside uke's right foot","text":"Plant your left foot firmly to the outside of uke's right foot. This is your base. Keep your chest driving forward into uke — do not let them regain upright posture."},{"title":"Swing your right leg forward as a pendulum","text":"Swing your right leg forward past uke's right leg. This wind-up phase adds power to the reap. Do not rush it — the pendulum swing is key to the technique's power."},{"title":"Reap the back of uke's thigh","text":"Drive your right leg backward in a powerful sweeping arc, contacting the back of uke's right thigh with the back of your right thigh. Your contact point is the thigh, not the ankle or calf."},{"title":"Drive forward with your upper body","text":"As your leg reaps, drive your upper body forward and downward into uke. Pull their sleeve arm upward. The backward reap and forward upper body drive work together to topple them."}],"principles":["Uke must be leaning backward before the reap. Attempting Osoto-Gari against an upright or forward-leaning uke results in a blocked, easily countered technique.","Thigh-to-thigh contact is the reaping point. Contacting at the calf or ankle reduces the sweep and gives uke a chance to hop over.","The pendulum swing of the right leg adds power — do not try to step directly into the reap.","Body drive forward is as important as the leg reap. Both elements together create the toppling effect."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Attacking without backward kuzushi","fix":"Push uke backward first. If they are standing upright, they can simply step back and escape. The technique only works when uke's weight is already moving backward."},{"mistake":"Contacting at the calf instead of the thigh","fix":"Drive your reaping leg higher to make thigh contact. Low contact allows uke to lift their leg and hop over the reap."},{"mistake":"Leaning backward instead of forward during kake","fix":"Drive your chest forward and down into uke while reaping. Leaning back negates the backward toppling force."},{"mistake":"No pendulum swing — stepping directly into the reap","fix":"Swing the leg forward first, then back. The pendulum motion generates significantly more reaping force than a direct backward leg drive."}],"when":"Osoto-Gari is highly effective when uke pushes into you or has stiff, upright posture. It is one of the best counter-attack throws — when uke attacks and misses, their recovery step backward creates the backward kuzushi window Osoto-Gari needs. Time the attack as they step back to regain balance. Works well in combination with O-Uchi-Gari: attack one direction, then the other.","setups":["ko-uchi-gari","uchi-mata"],"followUps":["o-uchi-gari"],"counters":["osoto-gaeshi","o-uchi-gari","seoi-nage"],"related":["ko-soto-gari","o-uchi-gari","harai-goshi"],"source":"https://judolearn.com/techniques/osoto-gari/"},{"id":"ryo-te-jime","name":"Ryo-Te-Jime","kanji":"両手絞","translation":"Two-Hand Strangle","category":"Katame-Waza","subcategory":"Shime-Waza","difficulty":1,"grade":"5th kyu","grades":["5th kyu","4th kyu"],"videos":[{"yt":"-RHC4V7TQiY","title":"両手絞 /  Ryote-jime","channel":"KODOKAN"},{"yt":"phte12QUGwI","title":"BaseBall Choke from Sidemount - Ryo-Te Jime BJJ Judo Basics Submission Jujijime Collar Attack Ryo Te","channel":"Peter Mettler Martial Arts"},{"yt":"V0Vv7nVvBRs","title":"Judo ground technique: Ryo-te-jime (two hands strangle 两手绞) - variation from top position","channel":"Judo Link by Lincoln Han"}],"overview":"Ryo-Te-Jime is one of the simplest strangling techniques — tori uses both hands directly around uke's neck to apply a choke. \"Ryo-te\" means both hands. While straightforward in concept, effective application requires proper thumb and finger placement to compress the carotid arteries rather than the trachea. It is an introductory shime-waza technique.","phases":[],"steps":[{"title":"Establish a top control position","text":"Be in mount, kesa-gatame, or any position where your hands can reach both sides of uke's neck."},{"title":"Place both hands on the neck","text":"Bring both hands to uke's neck. Fingers are on the back or sides of the neck; thumbs are positioned at the front and sides of the neck."},{"title":"Position thumbs on carotid arteries","text":"Press the thumbs inward on the sides of the neck, targeting the carotid arteries. The goal is to compress the blood supply to the brain, not to squeeze the trachea."},{"title":"Apply simultaneous bilateral pressure","text":"Both thumbs press inward and upward simultaneously. Both hands work together to create a bilateral carotid compression."},{"title":"Maintain position and apply consistent pressure","text":"Hold the pressure consistently. Do not pulse or pump — steady compression is more effective and less exhausting."}],"principles":["Both hands must work together — simultaneous bilateral carotid compression is the mechanism.","Thumbs target the carotid arteries on the sides of the neck, not the trachea in the front. Trachea pressure is illegal and dangerous.","Ryo-Te-Jime teaches the fundamental principle of all carotid-based strangling techniques.","An effective position with good posture is required — hunched over with weak arms reduces pressure significantly."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Pressing on the trachea with the thumbs","fix":"Move the thumbs to the sides of the neck. The carotid arteries are at the sides, not the front. Tracheal pressure is dangerous and illegal in competition."},{"mistake":"Only using one hand effectively","fix":"Both hands must apply equal pressure. An asymmetric choke compresses one carotid but allows the other to supply blood, making the choke ineffective."},{"mistake":"Poor control position — uke can push tori away","fix":"Establish solid control before applying the choke. From a weak position, uke can simply push your arms away."},{"mistake":"Applying with straight, extended arms","fix":"Bend the elbows and drive body weight through the arms. Straight arms lock out and reduce effective pressure."}],"when":"Ryo-Te-Jime is applied from a mounted or top control position when both hands are free to reach the neck. It is a fundamental technique for beginners to develop the principle of bilateral carotid compression. In advanced practice, more sophisticated chokes are usually preferred, but Ryo-Te-Jime remains valid.","setups":[],"followUps":["kesa-gatame","tate-shiho-gatame"],"counters":[],"related":["hadaka-jime","tsukkomi-jime","nami-juji-jime"],"source":"https://judolearn.com/techniques/ryo-te-jime/"},{"id":"sankaku-garami","name":"Sankaku-Garami","kanji":"三角絡","translation":"Triangular Entanglement","category":"Katame-Waza","subcategory":"Kansetsu-Waza","difficulty":3,"grade":"1st kyu","grades":["1st kyu","1st dan"],"videos":[{"yt":"nnatLPDqwnY","title":"Judo Grappling: Sankaku Garami","channel":"Kaze Uta Budo Kai / Windsong Dojo"},{"yt":"yu8rIqf5VCw","title":"Ashi sankaku garami (Omoplata)","channel":"Antonsson Shihan"},{"yt":"wuZSyuWI-uk","title":"HUIZINGA turnover ROLL  JUDO;      ashi-sankaku-garami, 三角緘","channel":"Judo Life"}],"overview":"Sankaku-Garami is an advanced arm entanglement technique that uses the legs in a triangular lock — similar to Sankaku-Jime — but targets the arm for a joint lock rather than choking the neck. The legs form a triangle that traps uke's arm (typically at the elbow), and the combined leg force and body mechanics create an armlock by bending the arm against its natural direction. It is closely related to Sankaku-Jime and often transitions between the two.","phases":[],"steps":[{"title":"Control uke's arm and position legs","text":"Gain control of one of uke's arms. Position your legs to form a triangle around the arm — one leg over the arm, the other hooking behind the first knee."},{"title":"Form the triangle around the arm","text":"Lock the triangular leg position around uke's arm at the elbow — similar to Sankaku-Jime's leg lock but targeted at the arm rather than the neck."},{"title":"Trap the arm within the triangle","text":"The arm is inside the triangular leg lock. Squeeze the legs to tighten the entanglement around uke's arm."},{"title":"Apply mechanical force to the elbow","text":"Rotate the hips or adjust body position to apply bending force on the trapped elbow joint. The leg triangle provides the frame; the hip mechanics provide the pressure."},{"title":"Maintain until submission","text":"Apply consistent pressure on the elbow joint. Uke taps when the joint pressure reaches the submission point."}],"principles":["The triangular leg lock structure is identical to Sankaku-Jime but targets the arm, not the neck. Understanding Sankaku-Jime helps with Sankaku-Garami.","Hip mechanics provide the joint-locking force. The legs trap and frame the arm; the hips generate the bending pressure.","Sankaku-Garami and Sankaku-Jime are closely related — from the sankaku leg position, either can be applied depending on what uke's body presents.","Arm positioning within the triangle determines the direction of joint stress. Precise arm capture is required."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Triangle formed incorrectly — too loose or wrong part of arm","fix":"The triangle must lock around the arm at or near the elbow. Too loose and the arm can slip through; too far up the arm is ineffective."},{"mistake":"No hip involvement — relying only on leg squeeze","fix":"The hips must engage to drive the joint-locking force. Leg squeezing alone is insufficient — hip rotation or elevation provides the leveraged pressure."},{"mistake":"Confusing with Sankaku-Jime during execution","fix":"Know whether you are targeting the neck (Sankaku-Jime) or the arm (Sankaku-Garami) before committing. The leg lock structure is similar but the target and finishing mechanics differ."},{"mistake":"Allowing uke to rotate the arm and escape","fix":"Keep tight control of the arm's rotation. Uke escaping by rotating the forearm means the entanglement needs to be tighter."}],"when":"Sankaku-Garami is applied from the sankaku leg-lock position when uke's arm is accessible rather than their neck, or when transitioning from a failed Sankaku-Jime. It is a high-level technique requiring extensive newaza experience.","setups":[],"followUps":["sankaku-jime","juji-gatame"],"counters":[],"related":["sankaku-jime","juji-gatame","ude-garami","hiza-gatame"],"source":"https://judolearn.com/techniques/sankaku-garami/"},{"id":"sankaku-jime","name":"Sankaku-Jime","kanji":"三角絞","translation":"Triangular Strangle","category":"Katame-Waza","subcategory":"Shime-Waza","difficulty":3,"grade":"2nd kyu","grades":["2nd kyu","1st kyu","1st dan"],"videos":[{"yt":"lq1CUBRAm7s","title":"三角絞 /  Sankaku-jime","channel":"KODOKAN"},{"yt":"-HCgIIrAaWI","title":"Sankaku Jime Part I: The Basics","channel":"Higashi Canada"},{"yt":"2U5AotsbEGU","title":"Sankaku-Jime Tips | Riki Judo Dojo","channel":"Riki Judo Dojo"},{"yt":"BObuSheNoLg","title":"Sankaku-jime Compilation","channel":"Ochiru"},{"yt":"9dSLsxUpUz4","title":"Sankaku-Jime (Osaekomi variation) compilation","channel":"Ochiru"}],"overview":"Sankaku-Jime is an advanced and highly effective strangle where tori creates a triangular lock with their legs around uke's head and one arm, squeezing the carotid arteries with the combined force of the thigh and the opponent's own arm. Known internationally as the \"triangle choke,\" it is one of the most powerful and versatile strangling techniques in grappling arts.","phases":[],"steps":[{"title":"Control uke's posture and positioning","text":"Establish a position where uke's head and one arm are accessible — typically from guard, a top position, or after a transition."},{"title":"Trap one arm and exclude the other","text":"Pull one of uke's arms across their centerline. One arm should be inside the triangle; the other outside. Having both inside or both outside negates the choke."},{"title":"Place the leg over uke's neck","text":"Swing one leg over the back of uke's neck. The calf or back of the knee should rest against the back of uke's neck."},{"title":"Lock the triangle — hook the ankle","text":"Bend the other leg and hook it under the first leg's knee. The legs lock in a triangular shape around uke's head and trapped arm."},{"title":"Squeeze and adjust angle","text":"Squeeze the legs together like scissors. Cut your body at 90 degrees to uke's body for the most effective angle. Pull uke's head down to tighten the triangle."}],"principles":["One arm in, one arm out — this is the fundamental setup requirement. Both arms inside or both outside completely negates the choke.","The angle of attack matters — positioning your body perpendicular to uke (cutting the angle) significantly amplifies the squeeze.","The trapped arm's shoulder adds to the pressure on the carotid. The more it presses into the neck, the tighter the choke.","Sankaku-Jime can be applied from many positions — guard, top, mount, from behind — making it extremely versatile."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Both arms inside — choke is negated","fix":"Ensure only one arm is inside the triangle. The inside arm presses on the carotid; if both are inside there is no compression."},{"mistake":"No angle cut — facing directly up or down","fix":"Cut your body to 90 degrees relative to uke. Facing straight up or down reduces the squeeze effectiveness dramatically."},{"mistake":"Triangle not locked — legs open","fix":"Ensure the ankle is properly hooked behind the opposite knee. An unlocked triangle loses pressure and is easier to escape."},{"mistake":"Pulling the head up instead of down","fix":"Pull uke's head down into the triangle. Pulling upward loosens the choke; pulling down tightens it."}],"when":"Sankaku-Jime is applicable from a wide variety of positions and is one of the most versatile submissions in judo. It is set up from guard, arm attacks, scrambles, and transitions. Advanced practitioners create sankaku entries from standing — the sankaku-style leg lock trap (Sankaku-Garami) also exists. Mastery requires extensive drilling of the setup and angle-cutting.","setups":["gyaku-juji-jime"],"followUps":["juji-gatame","ude-garami"],"counters":[],"related":["sankaku-garami","juji-gatame","gyaku-juji-jime","kataha-jime"],"source":"https://judolearn.com/techniques/sankaku-jime/"},{"id":"sasae-tsurikomi-ashi","name":"Sasae-Tsurikomi-Ashi","kanji":"支釣込足","translation":"Propping Drawing Ankle Throw","category":"Nage-Waza","subcategory":"Ashi-Waza","difficulty":2,"grade":"4th kyu","grades":["4th kyu","3rd kyu"],"videos":[{"yt":"699i--pvYmE","title":"支釣込足 / Sasae-tsurikomi-ashi","channel":"KODOKAN"},{"yt":"MiGljHOokvE","title":"Sasae-tsurikomi-ashi - Demo","channel":"Efficient Judo"},{"yt":"OaV3mMXLg2I","title":"Sasae Tsurikomi Ashi","channel":"Shintaro Nakano"},{"yt":"mxsAhIh_RVU","title":"Sasae-Tsurikomi-Ashi Tips | Riki Judo Dojo","channel":"Riki Judo Dojo"},{"yt":"zow2y-cWMbM","title":"Sasae tsurikomi ashi compilation","channel":"judo-compilations"}],"overview":"Sasae-Tsurikomi-Ashi is an ankle throw that combines a lifting-pulling (tsuri-komi) hand action with a propping (sasae) foot placement. Tori places the sole of their foot against uke's ankle as a prop while simultaneously pulling uke's upper body forward and upward — uke's forward movement is blocked at the ankle while their upper body continues, projecting them in a forward arc. It is one of the most technically precise ashi-waza.","phases":[{"label":"Kuzushi · Off-balance","text":"Create forward kuzushi with a strong tsuri-komi pull — collar grip lifts upward while sleeve grip pulls forward. This is identical to the kuzushi of Tsurikomi-Goshi. Uke must be drawn forward with weight shifting to their front foot — the propped ankle is the stepping foot."},{"label":"Tsukuri · Entry","text":"As uke steps forward with their right foot, place the sole of your left foot against the inside of uke's right ankle from the front. This is the \"sasae\" — the prop. The foot placement blocks uke's forward movement at ankle level. Your body pivots slightly to the side to assist the rotational throw."},{"label":"Kake · Execution","text":"As the sole of your foot contacts uke's ankle (propping it), simultaneously pull uke's upper body forward and upward with the tsuri-komi grip action. Uke's ankle is blocked and their upper body continues forward — they rotate over the propped ankle and land on the mat in front of tori."}],"steps":[{"title":"Apply tsuri-komi kuzushi","text":"Lift the collar grip upward and pull the sleeve forward. Uke is drawn onto their right front corner, stepping forward with their right foot."},{"title":"Time the step","text":"Watch for uke's right foot to begin its forward step. The prop must be placed as the foot is in motion, just before it lands."},{"title":"Place the sole against uke's ankle","text":"Place the sole of your left foot flat against the inside of uke's right ankle from the front. This is the blocking prop — firm but not a kick."},{"title":"Pull with tsuri-komi simultaneously","text":"As the foot contacts uke's ankle, simultaneously execute the full tsuri-komi pull — lift collar up, pull sleeve forward. The two actions must happen together."},{"title":"Project uke forward","text":"Uke's ankle is blocked while their upper body continues forward over the prop. They land on the mat in a forward arc."}],"principles":["Timing is everything — the prop must be placed at the exact moment uke's foot is stepping forward. Too early or too late and the technique fails.","The sole of the foot makes contact, not the ankle or heel. A flat sole contact creates a solid prop; other contact points slip.","The tsuri-komi pull and the foot prop must be simultaneous. Sequential execution gives uke time to stop their step.","Sasae-Tsurikomi-Ashi is primarily a foot-blocking throw — the arm action does the throwing, the foot simply blocks the stepping foot."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Propping too late — uke's foot has already landed","fix":"The prop must contact uke's ankle while it is still in the forward step. Once the foot has planted, the block is ineffective."},{"mistake":"Using the ankle or toes instead of the sole","fix":"Place the full sole of the foot flat against uke's ankle. Using the toe or instep creates an unstable contact that slips under load."},{"mistake":"Weak arm pull — no upper body projection force","fix":"The arms must create strong tsuri-komi pull simultaneously with the prop. The foot alone does not throw uke — the arms project them over it."},{"mistake":"Propping the wrong foot — propping the rear foot","fix":"The prop targets uke's stepping (front) foot. Propping the rear or planted foot achieves nothing — the weight is already on the prop foot."}],"when":"Sasae-Tsurikomi-Ashi is most effective when uke is moving forward and committed to a step. It is an excellent setup technique — get uke moving forward with pushes or feints and time the prop. It is also used as a counter when uke steps forward to attack. The technique works on both sides.","setups":["o-uchi-gari","ko-uchi-gari"],"followUps":["harai-goshi"],"counters":["o-uchi-gari"],"related":["de-ashi-barai","okuri-ashi-barai","harai-tsurikomi-ashi","tsurikomi-goshi"],"source":"https://judolearn.com/techniques/sasae-tsurikomi-ashi/"},{"id":"seoi-nage","name":"Seoi-Nage","kanji":"背負い投げ","translation":"Shoulder Throw","category":"Nage-Waza","subcategory":"Te-Waza","difficulty":2,"grade":"5th kyu","grades":["5th kyu","4th kyu"],"videos":[{"yt":"zIq0xI0ogxk","title":"背負投 / Seoi-nage","channel":"KODOKAN"},{"yt":"eWEW9SfI5xg","title":"Seoi-nage - Basic principles","channel":"Efficient Judo"},{"yt":"JJm_gduHLwA","title":"3 levels of Ippon Seoi Nage","channel":"Shintaro Nakano"},{"yt":"mzJScxzp68A","title":"Top 10 Seoi Nage Experts Of All Time 背負い投げの達人　トップ１０","channel":"Judo Highlights"}],"overview":"Seoi-Nage is one of the most iconic and widely used throws in judo. The thrower (tori) enters beneath the opponent (uke), loads them onto their back, and rotates forward to throw them over the shoulder. It is a cornerstone technique taught early in judo training and remains devastating at the highest levels of competition.","phases":[{"label":"Kuzushi · Off-balance","text":"Pull uke sharply forward and upward with your sleeve hand (right hand in right-handed grip), breaking their balance to their right front corner. Simultaneously drive upward with your lapel hand to elevate their posture. The goal is to get uke rising onto their toes."},{"label":"Tsukuri · Entry","text":"Step your right foot across in front of uke's right foot, toes pointing in the same direction. Then pivot on your right foot, bringing your left foot in to complete the turn so your back is fully against uke's chest. Bend your knees and lower your hips below uke's hip line."},{"label":"Kake · Execution","text":"Straighten your legs explosively to lift uke off the ground, simultaneously driving your right elbow forward and down while bending at the waist. Rotate your entire body over your right hip to complete the throw, projecting uke forward over your shoulder."}],"steps":[{"title":"Establish kuzushi — break uke's balance forward","text":"Using your right sleeve grip, pull uke's arm sharply upward and forward toward their right front corner. Your left lapel hand drives upward simultaneously. Uke should be rising onto their toes, leaning forward."},{"title":"Step across with your right foot","text":"Step your right foot in front of and across uke's right foot. Your foot should point in the same direction uke is facing. This first step begins rotating your body into the throw."},{"title":"Pivot and bring in your left foot","text":"Pivot on your right foot and swing your left foot around so your feet are parallel and your back faces uke's chest. At the moment of entry, your hips must be lower than uke's hips — bend your knees significantly."},{"title":"Secure the arm and lower your center of gravity","text":"Pull uke's right arm tightly over your right shoulder. Your right arm drives under their armpit (morote grip) or around the arm (ippon seoi variation). Your back should be flush against uke's chest."},{"title":"Execute kake — the throw","text":"Straighten both legs powerfully to load uke's weight onto your back, then bend forward at the waist while driving your throwing arm forward and down. Rotate through your hip to project uke over your right shoulder and onto the mat."}],"principles":["Hips must be lower than uke's hips at the moment of entry — if you are the same height or higher, the throw will not work.","Kuzushi must precede entry. Attempting tsukuri before breaking balance gives uke time to resist.","The throw is powered by leg drive, not just arm strength. Straightening the legs is the primary lifting force.","Keep uke's arm pulled tightly over your shoulder throughout — do not let it slip away during the rotation."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Hips too high during entry","fix":"Bend your knees deeply before stepping in. A common drill is to practice the entry alone until hip height feels natural."},{"mistake":"Failing to bend the knees at entry","fix":"Many practitioners step in but stay upright, relying on arm strength alone. The knees must bend enough to get your hips below uke's — without this, there is no lift."},{"mistake":"Insufficient kuzushi before stepping in","fix":"Pull upward and forward before your first step. If uke has both feet planted flat and their posture is upright, you will be blocked."},{"mistake":"Back not fully turned into uke","fix":"Your entire back should contact uke's chest. A half-turn leaves a gap that uke can use to create distance and escape."},{"mistake":"Bending forward at the waist too early","fix":"Straighten your legs first to load uke, then bend forward. Bending before loading means you only throw your upper body, not uke."},{"mistake":"Elbow pain from Morote-Seoi-Nage entry","fix":"Forcing the arm over the shoulder when uke is resisting puts excessive strain on tori's elbow. Ensure uke's arm is already broken upward via kuzushi before the entry — never force the arm down onto a bent or resistant elbow."},{"mistake":"Wrist pain from the lapel grip","fix":"Gripping the lapel too tightly with a locked wrist during the rotation strains the joint. Keep the wrist relaxed and drive with the whole arm rather than the hand alone."}],"when":"Seoi-Nage is most effective when uke is walking or pulling backward (which creates forward momentum you can redirect). It suits practitioners with a lower center of gravity than their opponent — shorter judoka find particular success. It is highly effective in combination as a secondary attack after a failed ashi-waza attempt, or as a follow-up to Ko-Uchi-Gari. Works best in a high, upright grip with sleeve control.","setups":["ko-uchi-gari","de-ashi-barai"],"followUps":[],"counters":["tani-otoshi","ude-garami"],"related":["tai-otoshi","seoi-otoshi","harai-goshi","ko-uchi-gari","o-goshi"],"source":"https://judolearn.com/techniques/seoi-nage/"},{"id":"seoi-otoshi","name":"Seoi-Otoshi","kanji":"背負落","translation":"Shoulder Drop","category":"Nage-Waza","subcategory":"Te-Waza","difficulty":2,"grade":"4th kyu","grades":["4th kyu","3rd kyu","2nd kyu"],"videos":[{"yt":"vu1TMVNnq34","title":"背負落 / Seoi-otoshi","channel":"KODOKAN"},{"yt":"780NuzpDYLY","title":"Craig Fallon - Seoi Otoshi - Arms and legs | SUPERSTAR JUDO","channel":"Fighting Films"},{"yt":"OqcEsriaI1Y","title":"Seoi-nage to Seoi-otoshi - Combination demo","channel":"Efficient Judo"},{"yt":"XvF0OyWkKRI","title":"Top 10 Drop Knee Seoi Nage / Seoi Otoshi Highlights Compilation","channel":"Judo Spirit"}],"overview":"Seoi-Otoshi is a shoulder throw variant where tori drops to both knees during execution rather than remaining standing. It shares the entry mechanics of Seoi-Nage but the drop to the knees generates a more vertical, powerful projection particularly effective against taller opponents. It is widely used in competition and is one of the most reliable throwing techniques at all levels.","phases":[{"label":"Kuzushi · Off-balance","text":"Pull uke forward and upward onto their right front corner with the sleeve grip. The sleeve arm elevates uke's right arm to open space under their armpit for the entry arm. Uke's weight must be loading forward onto the balls of their feet before the drop begins."},{"label":"Tsukuri · Entry","text":"Step your right foot in between uke's feet, toes pointing in the same direction as uke's. Simultaneously drive your right arm under uke's right armpit — elbow bends upward, forearm contacts uke's chest. Begin bending your knees to initiate the drop. Your back must be tight to uke's chest with no gap."},{"label":"Kake · Execution","text":"Drop to both knees simultaneously, driving your back down and into uke. As you drop, pull uke's sleeve sharply downward and forward with your left hand. Your entry arm pushes upward under uke's armpit. The combination of the knee drop, sleeve pull, and entry arm lift projects uke forward and over your right shoulder onto the mat."}],"steps":[{"title":"Create forward kuzushi","text":"Pull the sleeve grip upward and forward. Uke's right elbow rises. Their weight shifts forward onto their toes."},{"title":"Step the right foot in","text":"Step your right foot between uke's feet with toes pointing in uke's direction. This anchors your entry."},{"title":"Drive the right arm under uke's armpit","text":"Slide your right arm under uke's right arm with your elbow pointing upward. Your forearm contacts uke's chest across their sternum."},{"title":"Drop to both knees","text":"Drop explosively to both knees. This creates the projection angle that lifts uke off their feet. Your back remains in contact with uke's chest."},{"title":"Project uke forward","text":"Pull the sleeve arm down and forward while the entry arm drives upward. Uke's body is projected forward and over your shoulder in an arc to the mat."}],"principles":["The drop to both knees must be explosive, not gradual. A slow descent gives uke time to adjust and allows them to step over.","Back-to-chest contact must be maintained throughout. Any gap during the drop lets uke's weight miss your shoulder.","The sleeve pull is critical — it keeps uke's arm trapped and prevents them from posting out to break the fall wide.","Seoi-Otoshi is particularly effective against tall opponents where a standing Seoi-Nage is difficult due to height difference."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Dropping one knee at a time instead of both simultaneously","fix":"Both knees must hit simultaneously to create a level projection surface. Dropping one knee first rotates the shoulder and directs uke to the side, losing power."},{"mistake":"Entry arm too low — wrapping around uke's waist","fix":"The entry arm must go under the armpit with the elbow driving upward. Wrapping the waist is O-Goshi mechanics, not Seoi entry."},{"mistake":"Not completing the body turn before dropping","fix":"Your back must face uke's chest before dropping. Dropping while half-turned sends uke sideways rather than forward."},{"mistake":"Releasing the sleeve grip on the drop","fix":"Maintain the sleeve grip throughout the throw. It controls uke's arm and directs the arc of their fall."}],"when":"Seoi-Otoshi is particularly valuable when tori is shorter than uke, making the standing Seoi-Nage hip entry difficult. The knee drop adjusts the effective height of the entry. It is also effective against opponents who have a bent-forward defensive posture, as the drop creates the necessary angle. Widely used in shiai at all belt levels.","setups":[],"followUps":["ko-uchi-gari","o-uchi-gari","juji-gatame"],"counters":["tani-otoshi","ura-nage"],"related":["uki-otoshi","o-guruma","sukui-nage","tai-otoshi"],"source":"https://judolearn.com/techniques/seoi-otoshi/"},{"id":"sode-guruma-jime","name":"Sode-Guruma-Jime","kanji":"袖車絞","translation":"Sleeve Wheel Strangle","category":"Katame-Waza","subcategory":"Shime-Waza","difficulty":3,"grade":"1st kyu","grades":["1st kyu","1st dan"],"videos":[{"yt":"E3nvQzClcAU","title":"袖車絞 /  Sode-guruma-jime","channel":"KODOKAN"},{"yt":"LekSGVJHHis","title":"Neil Adams slides in a slick shime waza: Sode Guruma Jime","channel":"Neil Adams Judo"},{"yt":"QI0az7u1JAs","title":"Sode Guruma Jime Variation with Elbow","channel":"Sampson Judo"},{"yt":"_snDTpedAp4","title":"More than just a CHOKE... and how to counter it! - Sode-Guruma-Jime","channel":"non-stop newaza"}],"overview":"Sode-Guruma-Jime is an advanced strangle that uses the sleeves of uke's judogi to apply a wheel-like choking mechanism. Tori grips uke's sleeves and brings them across uke's throat, creating a crushing circular (guruma — wheel) strangle using the fabric of the sleeves rather than direct forearm or collar contact. It is one of the more inventive and unusual strangling techniques in judo.","phases":[],"steps":[{"title":"Position over or beside uke","text":"Be in a top position or kneeling beside uke where you can reach their sleeves on both sides."},{"title":"Grip both of uke's sleeves","text":"Grip the cuffs or sleeves of uke's judogi with both hands. The sleeve fabric will be the constricting element."},{"title":"Thread sleeves toward uke's throat","text":"Begin bringing the sleeve grips toward uke's neck — one sleeve from each side, converging at the throat."},{"title":"Cross the sleeves at the throat","text":"Cross the sleeves in a wheel-like (guruma) pattern across uke's throat. The crossing sleeves create pressure on the sides of the neck."},{"title":"Apply the wheel pressure","text":"Rotate or tighten the sleeve crossing to increase the strangling pressure. The wheel action compresses the carotids via the sleeve fabric."}],"principles":["The sleeve fabric is the choking element — grip deep enough in the sleeve for the fabric to have reach to the neck.","The wheel (guruma) crossing pattern is distinctive — the two sleeves cross at the throat in a circular compression.","Body position must give you leverage over the sleeve grips. A position where uke cannot easily reach your hands is ideal.","Sode-Guruma-Jime is a rare technique requiring specific sleeve access. Practice entry mechanics carefully."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Gripping sleeve too far from uke's wrist — insufficient reach","fix":"Grip the cuff area of the sleeve for maximum sleeve fabric reach toward the neck."},{"mistake":"Sleeves not crossing at the throat — wrong placement","fix":"The wheel action requires the sleeves to cross at the sides of the neck. If they cross at the chin or sternum, the technique is ineffective."},{"mistake":"No body weight control — uke can displace tori","fix":"Establish positional control before attempting the strangle. Applying from a weak position allows uke to push tori away."},{"mistake":"Pulling sleeves outward instead of crossing them inward","fix":"The crossing action brings the sleeves in and across the throat. Pulling outward moves the sleeves away from the neck."}],"when":"Sode-Guruma-Jime requires specific sleeve access — typically from a top position where uke's sleeves are reachable and the position allows the crossing action. It is an unusual technique that benefits from the element of surprise, as defenders expect more conventional choke setups.","setups":[],"followUps":["kami-shiho-gatame","kataha-jime"],"counters":[],"related":["okuri-eri-jime","hadaka-jime","kataha-jime"],"source":"https://judolearn.com/techniques/sode-guruma-jime/"},{"id":"sode-tsuri-komi-goshi","name":"Sode-Tsuri-Komi-Goshi","kanji":"袖釣込腰","translation":"Sleeve Lift-Pull Hip Throw","category":"Nage-Waza","subcategory":"Koshi-Waza","difficulty":3,"grade":"2nd kyu","grades":["2nd kyu","1st kyu","1st dan"],"videos":[{"yt":"QsmAxpmYLOI","title":"袖釣込腰 / Sode-tsurikomi-goshi","channel":"KODOKAN"},{"yt":"CkFAFbXgRM4","title":"瀧本誠② 「袖釣込腰」 / TAKIMOTO Makoto② \"Sode-tsurikomi-goshi\"","channel":"KODOKAN"},{"yt":"lOSaCqhWUkg","title":"Sode tsurikomi goshi tips","channel":"Shintaro Higashi"},{"yt":"wjP0FMXnyag","title":"Sode-Tsurikomi-Goshi Compilation","channel":"Ochiru"},{"yt":"7wfEARpxpd8","title":"Sode Tsurikomi Goshi　袖釣込腰 [Judo Techniques, Judo Throws, and Judo Ippons!]","channel":"Judo Highlights"}],"overview":"Sode-Tsuri-Komi-Goshi is an advanced hip throw executed from a two-sleeve grip. Instead of a standard lapel-sleeve hold, tori grips both of uke's sleeves — the right hand gripping uke's left sleeve at the elbow, the left hand gripping uke's right sleeve. Tori then pivots, loads uke's weight on their hip, and uses both arm pulls to rotate uke over. This grip bypasses uke's lapel defence entirely and is a favourite technique among players with a powerful two-sleeve kumi-kata style.","phases":[{"label":"Kuzushi · Off-balance","text":"From a two-sleeve grip, pull uke's left arm upward and across to their right front corner while simultaneously pulling their right arm inward. The cross-pulling action destabilises uke's frame diagonally, drawing them forward and onto their toes. The double-sleeve control prevents uke from posting a stabilising grip."},{"label":"Tsukuri · Entry","text":"Step your right foot in deep between uke's feet, toes turned outward. Pivot 180 degrees, swinging the left foot around to face the same direction as uke. Both sleeve grips are maintained throughout the turn. As you complete the entry, raise both arms to lift uke slightly, positioning your hip below their centre of gravity. Your back should press against uke's chest."},{"label":"Kake · Execution","text":"Drive your hip upward and forward while bending sharply at the waist. Both sleeve arms pull uke downward and across simultaneously — the combined pulling forces rotate uke over the hip. Release the throw with a sharp forward bend, projecting uke in a high arc to the mat."}],"steps":[{"title":"Establish two-sleeve grip","text":"Grip uke's left sleeve at the elbow with your right hand, and uke's right sleeve near the cuff or elbow with your left hand. Maintain active tension on both grips."},{"title":"Apply cross-directional kuzushi","text":"Pull uke's left sleeve upward and to their right front corner while drawing their right sleeve inward and downward. Uke's balance breaks diagonally to their right front."},{"title":"Step in and pivot","text":"Drive your right foot in front of and between uke's feet. Pivot sharply on the right foot, swinging the left foot around 180 degrees. Maintain the double-sleeve tension throughout."},{"title":"Raise arms and load the hip","text":"Lift both sleeve grips upward to raise uke onto your hip. Ensure your hip presses firmly into uke's hip region as you complete the entry."},{"title":"Execute the throw with combined arm pull","text":"Bend sharply forward while pulling both sleeves downward and across. Uke rotates over your hip and is projected to the mat."}],"principles":["The two-sleeve grip must remain active throughout — both arms work together to control uke's frame and create rotational force.","Hip placement follows the same principle as all koshi-waza: your hip must be in contact with uke's hip before the throw.","The cross-directional kuzushi is more complex than a simple forward pull — the diagonal break exposes uke's front corner.","This technique requires a fast, committed entry; a slow pivot gives uke time to post and block the hip."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Gripping too close to uke's cuff (loss of leverage)","fix":"Right hand should grip at the elbow of uke's left sleeve for the most leverage. Too close to the hand reduces the mechanical advantage on uke's arm."},{"mistake":"Arms pulling in the same direction instead of cross-direction","fix":"The kuzushi is cross-directional — one arm pulls upward, the other pulls inward. Pulling both in the same direction fails to break uke's balance."},{"mistake":"Incomplete hip contact during entry","fix":"The pivot must be fully committed, placing your back flat against uke's chest with hip contact. A partial turn exposes your side and fails to generate hip leverage."},{"mistake":"Attempting the throw without sufficient kuzushi","fix":"Sode-Tsuri-Komi-Goshi requires uke to be destabilised diagonally before entry. Attempting entry against a balanced uke results in a blocked throw."}],"when":"Sode-Tsuri-Komi-Goshi is most effective against opponents who defend against traditional lapel grips by keeping their elbows down. The two-sleeve entry circumvents this defence. It is commonly used by players whose primary grip strategy involves sleeve control. Best applied when uke overextends a sleeve grip, opening the entry.","setups":[],"followUps":["ko-uchi-gari","harai-goshi"],"counters":["tani-otoshi","ura-nage","sumi-otoshi"],"related":["tsuri-goshi","koshi-guruma","harai-goshi"],"source":"https://judolearn.com/techniques/sode-tsuri-komi-goshi/"},{"id":"soto-makikomi","name":"Soto-Makikomi","kanji":"外巻込","translation":"Outer Wraparound","category":"Nage-Waza","subcategory":"Yoko-Sutemi-Waza","difficulty":2,"grade":"3rd kyu","grades":["3rd kyu","2nd kyu"],"videos":[{"yt":"bWG9O1BVKtQ","title":"外巻込 / Soto-makikomi","channel":"KODOKAN"},{"yt":"563nrQTsYVc","title":"Soto-Makikomi","channel":"American Judo"},{"yt":"SbK5wiaeJP8","title":"Dominate Your Opponent with Soto Makikomi: Pro-Level Tips Revealed","channel":"DadBod Judo"},{"yt":"BcfGFR1CWss","title":"Soto-makikomi - Demo","channel":"Efficient Judo"}],"overview":"Soto-Makikomi is an outer wraparound sacrifice throw where tori wraps uke's arm from the outside and falls to the side, using the outer arm wrap (as opposed to the inner wraps of other makikomi throws) to control uke during the sacrifice. The \"soto\" (outer) refers to the outer position of the wrapping arm relative to uke's body.","phases":[{"label":"Kuzushi · Off-balance","text":"Pull uke to the outside and forward — to the right front corner. The sleeve grip pulls uke's arm forward and creates the opening for the outer arm wrap. Uke's balance shifts to their right front corner."},{"label":"Tsukuri · Entry","text":"Step in to uke's side. The outer arm — the arm wrapping from outside uke's sleeve arm — wraps around uke's right arm from the outside, pulling it against tori's body. This outer wrap position distinguishes Soto-Makikomi from other makikomi throws where the wrap is typically inner (between uke's arms)."},{"label":"Kake · Execution","text":"Execute the side fall with the outer arm maintaining the wrap. The fall pulls uke over and downward via the outer arm wrap. Uke's arm is trapped on the outside and they are thrown to the mat as tori falls."}],"steps":[{"title":"Pull uke forward and to the outside","text":"Pull uke's right arm forward and outward with the sleeve grip. Uke's balance shifts to their right front corner."},{"title":"Wrap the outside of uke's arm","text":"Your outer arm wraps around the outside of uke's right arm — from the outside rather than reaching under."},{"title":"Pull the arm tightly to your body","text":"Draw the wrapped arm tightly against your torso. The outer wrap controls uke's arm from the lateral side."},{"title":"Fall to the side","text":"Commit to the side sacrifice fall, maintaining the outer arm wrap throughout."},{"title":"Throw uke via the outer wrap","text":"The falling body weight and the outer arm wrap pull uke over. Uke is thrown to the mat."}],"principles":["The outer wrap position requires different entry mechanics from inner makikomi throws. The arm wraps around the outside of uke's arm, not beneath it.","Body proximity is key — you must be close enough for the outer wrap to create a secure hold.","The direction of the fall and the outer wrap must align. The fall pulls uke in the direction the outer wrap is applying force.","As a sacrifice, complete commitment is required. Half-hearted falls do not generate the throw force."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Confusing with inner makikomi wrap mechanics","fix":"In Soto-Makikomi the arm wraps from the outside — the wrapping arm is on the lateral (outer) side of uke's arm. Practice the entry specifically to ingrain the outer wrap."},{"mistake":"Loose outer wrap allowing arm escape","fix":"Pull the wrapped arm tight against your body. A loose outer wrap slides off easily."},{"mistake":"Falling away from uke","fix":"The fall must take uke with you. Fall toward uke's falling direction, not away."},{"mistake":"No forward kuzushi before the outer wrap","fix":"Pull uke forward and off-balance before entering for the wrap. The wrap alone without kuzushi cannot throw an upright uke."}],"when":"Soto-Makikomi is used when uke's arm position allows an outer wrap, or as an alternative makikomi variant when inner wraps are defended. It can also be set up from a specific grip exchange where tori's arm is positioned to the outside of uke's sleeve arm.","setups":["osoto-gari"],"followUps":["kesa-gatame"],"counters":[],"related":["harai-makikomi","hane-makikomi","uchi-mata-makikomi","o-soto-makikomi"],"source":"https://judolearn.com/techniques/soto-makikomi/"},{"id":"sukui-nage","name":"Sukui-Nage","kanji":"掬投","translation":"Scooping Throw","category":"Nage-Waza","subcategory":"Te-Waza","difficulty":2,"grade":"3rd kyu","grades":["3rd kyu","2nd kyu"],"videos":[{"yt":"vU6aJ2kFxoI","title":"掬投 / Sukui-nage","channel":"KODOKAN"},{"yt":"r_xtXYbuLqY","title":"Modern Sukui Nage for Judo / BJJ | JFAUK Sussex Judo Summer Camp","channel":"Sampson Judo"},{"yt":"Zop-etAEsFE","title":"Sukui Nage || Scooping Throw 2.0","channel":"The Judo Way of Life"},{"yt":"MPf6_ExXkY8","title":"Sukui-Nage Tips | Riki Judo Dojo","channel":"Riki Judo Dojo"}],"overview":"Sukui-Nage is a scooping throw where tori reaches between or around uke's legs to scoop one or both thighs upward while simultaneously pushing uke's upper body backward. It is a powerful counter-technique often used when uke bends deeply forward — tori exploits the bent posture to scoop the legs and dump uke onto their back.","phases":[{"label":"Kuzushi · Off-balance","text":"Uke's balance is broken to their rear. This technique typically arises when uke has bent forward exposing their legs, or when tori pushes uke's upper body backward with one arm while scooping upward with the other. The goal is to simultaneously displace uke's feet (via the scoop) and their upper body (via the push) in opposite directions."},{"label":"Tsukuri · Entry","text":"When uke is bent forward, lower your level with bent knees and reach your arm under uke's thigh or between the legs. Your free arm controls uke's upper body — either pushing the chest backward or pulling the collar down. Body position should be low and close, with your weight centered over your feet."},{"label":"Kake · Execution","text":"Drive the scooping arm upward and forward, lifting uke's thigh(s) off the ground. Simultaneously push or press uke's upper body backward. Uke's legs go one direction and their upper body the other — they are lifted and dumped to the mat. Follow through by stepping in to maintain balance."}],"steps":[{"title":"Identify the opening","text":"Sukui-Nage is typically applied when uke bends deeply forward, exposes their legs in a low posture, or attempts a leg grab of their own."},{"title":"Lower your level","text":"Bend your knees and lower your center of gravity. You must get below and inside uke's posture to reach the scooping position."},{"title":"Reach and scoop under the thigh","text":"Drive one arm under uke's near thigh from the inside. Your palm faces upward. The scoop should contact the thigh just above the back of the knee."},{"title":"Control the upper body","text":"With your free arm, push uke's chest or grip their collar to push backward, or wrap their upper body. This neutralizes their ability to post and resist."},{"title":"Lift and project to the rear","text":"Drive the scooping arm upward, lifting uke's leg(s) off the mat. Simultaneously push their upper body backward. Uke lands on their back or side."}],"principles":["Timing is crucial — Sukui-Nage works when uke has bent forward and committed their weight. Do not attempt against an upright uke.","The scoop must lift the thigh, not the ankle. A thigh scoop tips the hip; an ankle grab is easily stepped over.","Keep your own back straight during the scoop. Rounding your back during the lift reduces force and risks injury.","Upper-body control is as important as the scoop. Without a backward push or grip on uke's torso, they can post with their hands and escape."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Scooping the ankle instead of the thigh","fix":"The scoop should contact behind the knee or upper thigh. Scooping the ankle lifts the lower leg only — uke simply hops over."},{"mistake":"Neglecting upper body control","fix":"Always push or control uke's upper body while scooping. Without this, uke leans into you or posts to prevent the fall."},{"mistake":"Attempting the throw while uke is upright","fix":"Sukui-Nage requires uke to be in a bent-forward posture. Against an upright stance, the scoop is impossible to establish cleanly."},{"mistake":"Rounding the back during the lift","fix":"Maintain a straight back and drive from the legs when lifting. A rounded back transfers no power and risks lower-back strain."}],"when":"Sukui-Nage is primarily a counter-throw used when uke bends forward to attempt a leg grab, a low single-leg, or a double-leg takedown. It is also used proactively when uke adopts a heavily bent-forward defensive posture. In newaza transitions, it can be used to dump uke from a crouched position.","setups":["o-uchi-gari","ko-uchi-gari"],"followUps":[],"counters":["tani-otoshi"],"related":["morote-gari","kibisu-gaeshi","uki-otoshi"],"source":"https://judolearn.com/techniques/sukui-nage/"},{"id":"sumi-gaeshi","name":"Sumi-Gaeshi","kanji":"隅返","translation":"Corner Reversal","category":"Nage-Waza","subcategory":"Ma-Sutemi-Waza","difficulty":2,"grade":"3rd kyu","grades":["3rd kyu","2nd kyu","1st kyu"],"videos":[{"yt":"5VhduA5xkbA","title":"隅返 / Sumi-gaeshi","channel":"KODOKAN"},{"yt":"0BuqVnafVSg","title":"Sumi-gaeshi - Basic principles","channel":"Efficient Judo"},{"yt":"25tm1NQl6g0","title":"Sumi gaeshi in depth","channel":"Shintaro Higashi"},{"yt":"HNtpb3yp6Do","title":"Can you hit all 3 of these Sumi Gaeshi?","channel":"Shintaro Higashi"},{"yt":"HjpFyANDRas","title":"Sumi gaeshi / Hikikomi gaeshi Compilation","channel":"Ochiru"}],"overview":"Sumi-Gaeshi is a rear sacrifice throw in which tori falls backward, places their foot in uke's inner thigh or groin area, and uses a rolling motion to lever uke over their body to the mat. The \"reversal\" component describes how tori uses uke's forward-pressing weight against them — as uke presses in, tori falls back and redirects that energy upward and over. It is a highly effective counter technique but requires precise timing and the confidence to commit to a backward fall.","phases":[{"label":"Kuzushi · Off-balance","text":"Allow uke to press into you, or pull uke forward and into you, creating a strong forward commitment from uke. As uke's weight comes forward and onto you, begin your backward fall to redirect that energy. Uke's forward momentum must be established before the sacrifice begins."},{"label":"Tsukuri · Entry","text":"As uke presses forward, step your right foot to the outside of uke's right foot and begin falling backward. As you fall, place your left foot against the inside of uke's right thigh — the sole of the foot contacts uke's inner thigh above the knee. Your right foot hooks outside uke's right leg or posts on the mat for control."},{"label":"Kake · Execution","text":"As your back meets the mat, extend your left leg powerfully, pushing uke's thigh upward and over your body. Simultaneously pull uke's sleeve arm forward and over, and roll your own body backward. Uke's forward momentum combined with the leg extension and arm pull rolls them over your body and lands them on the mat beyond your head."}],"steps":[{"title":"Invite or absorb uke's forward pressure","text":"Either draw uke forward with your arms or allow uke to press into you. You need uke's weight committed forward."},{"title":"Begin the backward sacrifice","text":"Step outside uke's right foot and begin falling backward in a controlled drop. Stay connected to uke through your grips."},{"title":"Place the foot on the inner thigh","text":"As you fall, lift your left leg and place the sole of the foot against uke's inner right thigh, just above the knee. Maintain firm foot contact."},{"title":"Fall to your back and extend the leg","text":"As your back reaches the mat, extend your left leg powerfully upward and over, levering uke's thigh. Your back acts as the pivot point."},{"title":"Complete the roll with arm pull","text":"Pull the sleeve arm forward and over your body while rolling your torso. Uke's body rolls over you and lands on the mat."}],"principles":["The sacrifice is the technique — you must commit to falling backward. A hesitant half-fall leaves you in an exposed position on one knee.","The foot placement on the inner thigh must be established before your back hits the mat.","Uke's forward momentum is the fuel — without uke pressing in, the throw lacks the energy to complete the roll.","Stay connected through your grips throughout the throw; if you lose grip during the fall, control of the throw is lost."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Placing the foot on the knee instead of the inner thigh","fix":"The foot contact must be on the inner thigh, above the knee. Contact on the knee itself risks injury and reduces the lever length."},{"mistake":"Falling back without uke's forward pressure established","fix":"Without uke pressing in, you simply fall backward with uke standing over you. Ensure uke's weight is committed forward before initiating the sacrifice."},{"mistake":"Not extending the leg fully during kake","fix":"The leg extension must be powerful and complete — push uke's thigh up and over. A limp leg extension fails to generate the rotation needed to roll uke over."},{"mistake":"Rolling too far and landing on your stomach","fix":"Control the roll through the arm grip and leg extension. Overshoot places you in a disadvantaged ground position."}],"when":"Sumi-Gaeshi is excellent as a counter to an aggressive forward-pressing opponent. It is also used when uke bears down for a double-leg attack or a grip-fighting forward charge. In transition to ne-waza, it naturally leads to a dominant ground position if uke lands partially on their side. It is particularly effective as a counter to poorly timed hip throw attempts.","setups":[],"followUps":["juji-gatame","kesa-gatame"],"counters":["tani-otoshi","osoto-gari"],"related":["tomoe-nage","sumi-otoshi","ura-nage"],"source":"https://judolearn.com/techniques/sumi-gaeshi/"},{"id":"sumi-otoshi","name":"Sumi-Otoshi","kanji":"隅落","translation":"Corner Drop","category":"Nage-Waza","subcategory":"Te-Waza","difficulty":2,"grade":"4th kyu","grades":["4th kyu","3rd kyu","2nd kyu"],"videos":[{"yt":"lLU9wv52ni0","title":"隅落 / Sumi-otoshi","channel":"KODOKAN"},{"yt":"zdHHlcEbxE4","title":"Sumi-Otoshi Tips | Riki Judo Dojo","channel":"Riki Judo Dojo"},{"yt":"sXnRJ3u278c","title":"Sumi-Otoshi Variations | Riki Judo Dojo","channel":"Riki Judo Dojo"},{"yt":"j15R6hRHdd4","title":"Unlock the Secrets: Sumi Otoshi - The Game-Changer You Need in Your Arsenal\"","channel":"DadBod Judo"},{"yt":"XLRsUBejxso","title":"Maisuradze's Sumi-Otoshi","channel":"JudoCrazy"}],"overview":"Sumi-Otoshi is a hand technique in which tori drops uke to their rear corner without the use of hip or leg contact. The throw is executed entirely through arm and body control — tori breaks uke's balance to one of their rear corners and then drives their weight backward and downward in that direction. The absence of hip or leg contact makes Sumi-Otoshi unusual among throwing techniques and allows it to be applied from unusual angles and distances.","phases":[{"label":"Kuzushi · Off-balance","text":"Draw uke forward strongly to commit their weight forward. As uke resists by pushing back, or as tori steps diagonally to uke's side, redirect that backward energy toward uke's right rear corner. The kuzushi is to the rear corner — uke's weight shifts to their right heel with no leg to recover onto."},{"label":"Tsukuri · Entry","text":"Step diagonally to uke's right side, moving your right foot behind and to the outside of uke's right foot. As you step, use both arms to steer uke's upper body toward their right rear corner. Your body faces uke's side with no hip contact."},{"label":"Kake · Execution","text":"Push uke's upper body toward their right rear corner with both arms — the sleeve arm pulls and drives downward and back, the lapel arm steers and pushes. Without a leg or hip to stop their backward fall, uke drops directly to their right rear corner and lands on the mat. Maintain arm tension throughout to direct the fall."}],"steps":[{"title":"Draw uke forward to commit their weight","text":"Pull uke forward with both arms, encouraging them to step in and commit their weight toward you."},{"title":"Redirect to the rear corner","text":"As uke steps forward, step your right foot to the side and behind uke's right foot. Simultaneously redirect your arm pull from forward to backward and toward uke's right rear corner."},{"title":"Position beside uke","text":"Move to uke's right side, facing their shoulder. There is no hip contact — your body is beside uke's."},{"title":"Drive uke's upper body backward","text":"Push uke's right shoulder backward and down with the lapel arm, while pulling the sleeve arm across and down. Uke's centre of gravity moves toward their right rear corner."},{"title":"Drop uke to the mat","text":"Maintain driving pressure as uke's balance tips past the point of recovery. Uke drops to their right rear, landing on the mat."}],"principles":["There is no hip or leg contact — the entire throw is arm and body steering. This is rare in judo and requires precise directional control.","The rear corner is the target — uke's right rear (or left rear for a left-side variation). The direction must be precise.","Effective kuzushi is created by first drawing uke forward, then suddenly redirecting to the rear. The misdirection unbalances uke.","Speed and commitment are essential — a slow, tentative push allows uke to step back and recover."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Trying to push uke straight back instead of to the rear corner","fix":"Uke can easily step back to recover from a straight backward push. The throw only works toward the rear corner — where there is no foot to step to."},{"mistake":"Using hip contact during the throw","fix":"Sumi-Otoshi is specifically a hand/arm technique with no hip involvement. Adding hip contact changes it to a different technique."},{"mistake":"Failing to first draw uke forward","fix":"The misdirection from forward-to-backward is what unbalances uke. Attempting a pure backward push from the start gives uke full resistance from their planted stance."},{"mistake":"Loose arm control during the drop","fix":"Both arms must maintain firm, directional control throughout. Loose grips reduce the directional precision and uke can adjust."}],"when":"Sumi-Otoshi is most effective when uke is pushing back against tori's forward attack — redirecting their backward resistance toward a rear corner. It is also used from a side-step position when uke overextends an attack. It works as a counter to aggressive forward throws and as a surprise attack from unusual angles.","setups":["harai-goshi","o-goshi"],"followUps":[],"counters":["o-uchi-gari","ko-uchi-gari"],"related":["tani-otoshi","o-soto-otoshi","sumi-gaeshi"],"source":"https://judolearn.com/techniques/sumi-otoshi/"},{"id":"tai-otoshi","name":"Tai-Otoshi","kanji":"体落","translation":"Body Drop","category":"Nage-Waza","subcategory":"Te-Waza","difficulty":2,"grade":"4th kyu","grades":["4th kyu","3rd kyu"],"videos":[{"yt":"4x6S3Q-Ktv8","title":"体落 / Tai-otoshi","channel":"KODOKAN"},{"yt":"u34wRSY-suM","title":"Tai-otoshi - Set up combinations","channel":"Efficient Judo"},{"yt":"8oxA5l3o420","title":"Tai-otoshi - Follow up combinations","channel":"Efficient Judo"},{"yt":"c4F1czSL7pE","title":"3 Types of Tai Otoshi","channel":"American Judo"},{"yt":"MUH56emboZs","title":"Best of Tai Otoshi (Compilation)","channel":"Grappler Kingdom"}],"overview":"Tai-Otoshi is a hand throw that uses a leg barrier rather than a hip load to unbalance and rotate the opponent to the mat. Unlike hip throws, tori does not lift uke — instead, tori's extended right leg acts as a blocking post while rotational force spins uke over it.","phases":[{"label":"Kuzushi · Off-balance","text":"Pull uke sharply forward and to their right front corner. Your sleeve hand (right) drives upward and outward, while your lapel hand (left) pulls forward across your body. Uke must be leaning forward with weight shifting to their right foot."},{"label":"Tsukuri · Entry","text":"Step your right foot across to the right side of uke's right foot, then pivot and bring your left foot around so your back faces uke's chest. Extend your right leg across in front of uke's right shin — this is the barrier. Your right leg should be nearly straight, placed low across uke's lower leg."},{"label":"Kake · Execution","text":"Rotate your upper body sharply to your left while pulling strongly with both hands — your sleeve hand downward, your lapel hand across and down. This rotation sweeps uke over your extended right leg and to the mat. The throw is powered entirely by rotation, not by lifting."}],"steps":[{"title":"Break uke's balance forward-right (kuzushi)","text":"Pull uke's sleeve arm upward and toward their right front corner. Your lapel hand drives forward across your centerline. Uke's weight should be transferring to their right foot."},{"title":"Step your right foot across","text":"Step your right foot to the outside of uke's right foot. Your foot should be placed past their foot, not beside it — this sets the angle for the leg barrier."},{"title":"Pivot and place the leg barrier","text":"Pivot on your right foot and swing your left foot around until your back faces uke's chest. Extend your right leg across in front of uke's right shin, close to the ground. Keep it relatively straight."},{"title":"Apply rotational throwing force","text":"Twist your upper body strongly to the left. Pull uke's sleeve downward with your right hand and pull their lapel forward and around with your left. This rotation — not a lift — drives uke over your leg barrier."},{"title":"Complete the throw","text":"Continue rotating until uke's body clears your right leg and they land on the mat to your right side. Maintain grip control throughout to guide the fall."}],"principles":["The right leg is a barrier, not a trip. Place it and hold it; the rotation of your upper body does the work.","Unlike Seoi-Nage, you do not lift uke. Tai-Otoshi is purely rotational.","The leg must be placed across uke's shin, not against their thigh — placing too high reduces leverage.","Your body turn must be complete before applying the throw — a half-turn results in poor angle and escape for uke."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Trying to lift uke instead of rotating","fix":"Tai-Otoshi throws through rotation. Think of turning a steering wheel, not lifting a box."},{"mistake":"Leg barrier placed too high (against the thigh)","fix":"The right leg should block the shin or lower leg. Contact too high removes the mechanical advantage."},{"mistake":"Insufficient rotation — body turn is incomplete","fix":"Your chest and hips must rotate fully away from uke before pulling. Drill the entry movement until the turn is automatic."},{"mistake":"Pulling with arms only, no body rotation","fix":"The power comes from rotating your torso. Arms just transmit the force — they should not initiate it."}],"when":"Tai-Otoshi works well against taller opponents where loading them onto your back (as in hip throws) is difficult. It is extremely effective when uke steps to their right (tori's left) or when they push into you — you redirect that forward energy over the leg barrier. Excellent combination throw following O-Uchi-Gari or Ko-Uchi-Gari.","setups":[],"followUps":["ko-uchi-gari","o-uchi-gari"],"counters":["tani-otoshi","o-uchi-gari"],"related":["seoi-nage","harai-goshi","ko-uchi-gari"],"source":"https://judolearn.com/techniques/tai-otoshi/"},{"id":"tani-otoshi","name":"Tani-Otoshi","kanji":"谷落","translation":"Valley Drop","category":"Nage-Waza","subcategory":"Yoko-Sutemi-Waza","difficulty":2,"grade":"3rd kyu","grades":["3rd kyu","2nd kyu"],"videos":[{"yt":"3b9Me3Fohpk","title":"谷落 / Tani-otoshi","channel":"KODOKAN"},{"yt":"i_yvO3ICpgs","title":"Tani-otoshi - Technical Breakdown","channel":"Efficient Judo"},{"yt":"tv3CpZYB0c4","title":"Tani otoshi in Depth","channel":"Shintaro Higashi"},{"yt":"FIm6V2sAgb8","title":"Tani-Otoshi Compilation","channel":"Ochiru"}],"overview":"Tani-Otoshi is a side sacrifice throw primarily used as a counter technique. When uke bends forward — particularly during the entry for a hip throw or Seoi-Nage — tori steps behind them, loads their weight against uke's back, and falls to the side while pulling uke over and backward. It is one of the most reliable counter throws in judo.","phases":[{"label":"Kuzushi · Off-balance","text":"Tani-Otoshi creates its kuzushi reactively: uke's own forward bend (during their attack entry) creates the backward imbalance that Tani-Otoshi exploits."},{"label":"Tsukuri · Entry","text":"Step your right foot to the outside-rear of uke's stance, behind and to the side of both their feet. Lean your body weight into uke's lower back. Keep your hips against their lower body."},{"label":"Kake · Execution","text":"Drop to your right side, pulling uke's arms and rotating their upper body toward you while your right leg sweeps both of uke's legs forward. The combination of your falling weight, arm pull, and leg sweep topples uke backward over your falling body."}],"steps":[{"title":"Recognize and react to uke's forward entry","text":"Tani-Otoshi is primarily a counter. As uke enters for a hip throw or other forward technique and bends forward, the opportunity window opens."},{"title":"Step behind uke with your right foot","text":"Step your right foot to the outside-rear of uke's stance, behind and to the side of both their feet. Your leg creates the surface onto which you will fall."},{"title":"Load your weight into uke's lower back","text":"Drive your hips and body weight into uke's lower back. Your chest should press against their back, pushing them forward over your foot."},{"title":"Drop to your right side and pull","text":"Fall to your right side while simultaneously pulling both of uke's arms downward and toward you. The drop and pull work together."},{"title":"Sweep with the right leg as you fall","text":"As you drop, sweep your right leg into uke's legs or ankle to remove their base. Combined with the arm pull and body drop, uke falls backward."}],"principles":["Timing is critical — Tani-Otoshi must react to uke's forward movement, not attack a stable upright uke.","The body weight load into uke's back is essential — without it, uke simply steps away.","Pull uke's arms downward and across your body during the fall, not just backward.","This is a sacrifice throw — you are dropping to the mat deliberately."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Attempting on an upright, stable uke","fix":"Tani-Otoshi requires uke to be bent forward. Against an upright uke, they simply step backward and you fall alone."},{"mistake":"Stepping beside uke rather than behind them","fix":"Your right foot must go behind both of uke's feet. Beside them means no leg barrier."},{"mistake":"Not driving body weight into uke's back","fix":"Your body weight pressing into their back is what prevents them from stepping away. Without this, the throw fails."}],"when":"Tani-Otoshi is most commonly used as a counter to Seoi-Nage, hip throws, or any technique that requires uke to bend forward. As uke bends forward into their throw, their weight is committed forward — step behind them and drop. Also works proactively when uke is bent over in a defensive posture.","setups":[],"followUps":["seoi-nage"],"counters":[],"related":["tomoe-nage","seoi-nage","osoto-gari"],"source":"https://judolearn.com/techniques/tani-otoshi/"},{"id":"tate-shiho-gatame","name":"Tate-Shiho-Gatame","kanji":"縦四方固","translation":"Vertical Four-Corner Hold","category":"Katame-Waza","subcategory":"Osaekomi-Waza","difficulty":2,"grade":"4th kyu","grades":["4th kyu","3rd kyu","2nd kyu"],"videos":[{"yt":"55-rFmBx53g","title":"縦四方固 /  Tate-shiho-gatame","channel":"KODOKAN"},{"yt":"JMJBjnst_DA","title":"縦四方固の逃れ方 /  Tate-shiho-gatame Escapes","channel":"KODOKAN"},{"yt":"R5rzCcaMB6c","title":"Tate-Shiho-Gatame Tips | Riki Judo Dojo","channel":"Riki Judo Dojo"},{"yt":"_BL7xDMpJCg","title":"How to do Tate-Shiho-Gatame | Vertical Four Quarter Hold | 縦四方固 | Full Mount","channel":"Sampson Judo"},{"yt":"F_diI6O6zZ0","title":"Tate-Shiho-Gatame Variations | Riki Judo Dojo","channel":"Riki Judo Dojo"}],"overview":"Tate-Shiho-Gatame is a mount position hold in which tori straddles uke face-to-face, with their legs straddling uke's torso and their arms controlling uke's head and upper body. The \"vertical four-corner hold\" describes how tori's body occupies the vertical axis above uke, with limbs controlling the four corners. It is the most dominant pin in judo ground work — once established, it is extremely difficult to escape and provides direct access to choke and armlock attacks.","phases":[{"label":"Kuzushi · Off-balance","text":"From a mounted position, keep your hips low and centred on uke's torso. Control uke's head by hooking under the neck with one arm and gripping the collar with the other. Uke's escape options are severely limited when the head is controlled and the hips are low."},{"label":"Tsukuri · Entry","text":"Straddle uke's torso with your knees on the mat beside their hips. Tuck your feet under uke's thighs (hooks) or cross your feet at uke's lower back. Slide one arm under uke's neck from one side and grip the collar on the far side. The other arm controls uke's near arm or grips the lapel."},{"label":"Kake · Execution","text":"Sink your hips low onto uke's torso. Drive your chest toward uke's face, keeping your head low. The weight distribution over uke's centre of mass makes the pin extremely difficult to dislodge. Hooks under uke's thighs prevent the bridge-and-roll escape."}],"steps":[{"title":"Establish mount position","text":"Straddle uke's torso with knees on the mat at their hip level. Your hips sit directly above uke's lower abdomen."},{"title":"Hook the feet under uke's thighs","text":"Tuck your feet under uke's thighs (grapevine hooks) or cross your ankles at uke's lower back. Hooks prevent bridging escape."},{"title":"Control uke's head and neck","text":"Slide your right arm under uke's neck and grip uke's collar on the left side. This controls uke's head rotation."},{"title":"Control uke's near arm","text":"With your left arm, control uke's right arm — grip their sleeve, trap their elbow, or control their wrist. Prevent arm-based escapes."},{"title":"Sink hips and apply weight","text":"Lower your hips as much as possible onto uke's torso. Drive your chest forward and keep your head low beside uke's head. Maintain for 10 seconds for waza-ari, 20 seconds for ippon."}],"principles":["Low hips are the foundation — the lower your hips, the harder the pin is to escape. Raised hips invite the bridge-and-roll.","Head control prevents uke from turning into or away from you to create escape leverage.","Hooks under uke's thighs (or crossed feet) neutralise the bridge escape, which is uke's primary tool from this position.","Chest pressure toward uke's face keeps uke's upper back flat on the mat."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Hips too high (kneeling tall)","fix":"Sit your hips down as low as possible onto uke's abdomen. High hips create space that allows uke to bridge and roll."},{"mistake":"No hooks on uke's thighs","fix":"Without hooks, uke can bridge and roll. Tuck your feet under uke's thighs or apply another hooking control."},{"mistake":"Leaning too far forward (over uke's head)","fix":"Leaning past uke's head shifts your weight off their chest and allows them to work free underneath you. Stay centred."},{"mistake":"Arms supporting weight instead of controlling uke","fix":"Arms should control uke's head and arm, not prop you up. If you need your arms for balance, your hips are too high."}],"when":"Tate-Shiho-Gatame is the natural landing position after many front-throw combinations, and is an excellent transition from side holds when uke attempts to roll. It is the most advantageous position in ground work because it offers direct access to chokes (applying from the mount) and armlocks (Juji-Gatame from mount) while maintaining the pin.","setups":[],"followUps":["juji-gatame","gyaku-juji-jime"],"counters":["ura-nage","sumi-gaeshi"],"related":["kesa-gatame","kami-shiho-gatame","kata-gatame"],"source":"https://judolearn.com/techniques/tate-shiho-gatame/"},{"id":"tawara-gaeshi","name":"Tawara-Gaeshi","kanji":"俵返","translation":"Rice-Bale Reversal","category":"Nage-Waza","subcategory":"Ma-Sutemi-Waza","difficulty":2,"grade":"3rd kyu","grades":["3rd kyu","2nd kyu"],"videos":[{"yt":"TmTWgrmViZc","title":"俵返 / Tawara-gaeshi","channel":"KODOKAN"},{"yt":"gXGuRFwmBls","title":"Tawara Gaeshi by Matt D'Aquino","channel":"BeyondGrappling"},{"yt":"zORVnrrPnmM","title":"Tawara-gaeshi - Demo","channel":"Efficient Judo"}],"overview":"Tawara-Gaeshi is a sacrifice throw named for its resemblance to rolling a heavy rice bale. Tori picks up uke bodily — wrapping both arms around uke's torso — and falls backward while tipping uke over, rolling them like a heavy barrel or rice bale to land on their back. It is classified as ma-sutemi-waza because tori falls directly backward.","phases":[{"label":"Kuzushi · Off-balance","text":"Kuzushi is created by the lifting and tipping action. Tori wraps around uke's torso and lifts them — uke's feet may leave the ground. Once lifted, uke's balance is completely broken as their center of mass is in tori's arms. The lift and subsequent backward fall are the combined kuzushi-kake action."},{"label":"Tsukuri · Entry","text":"Secure both arms firmly around uke's torso, typically under the arms or around the waist. The grip should be deep — hands clasped or gripping the belt at the back. Position your hips against or below uke's hips to use them as the pivot point for lifting."},{"label":"Kake · Execution","text":"Lift uke using the leg drive (standing up) and arm wrap. Once uke is lifted, fall backward — the sacrifice. As tori falls back with uke in their arms, the momentum tips uke up and over. Tori rolls back and uke is deposited onto the mat, resembling the rolling of a heavy rice bale."}],"steps":[{"title":"Wrap both arms around uke's torso","text":"Secure a deep two-arm wrap around uke's midsection — under the arms or around the waist. Clasped hands or a belt grip at the back."},{"title":"Position hips below uke's center","text":"Bend your knees and place your hips lower than uke's hips to create the lever point for the lift."},{"title":"Lift uke off the ground","text":"Drive upward through your legs, lifting uke entirely off the ground. The arms maintain the encircling grip."},{"title":"Fall backward — the sacrifice","text":"With uke lifted, fall straight backward. Your backward fall takes uke with you."},{"title":"Roll and deposit uke to the mat","text":"As you fall, tip uke up and over your body. Roll through and uke lands on the mat beside or in front of you."}],"principles":["The grip must encircle uke fully — a partial wrap allows uke to squirm free during the lift.","Lift first, then sacrifice. Attempting the fall before lifting merely drops you under uke with no throw.","The fall should be controlled — not a crash. Tori uses the ground to roll through the technique safely.","Follow through to a hold-down. After depositing uke, transition quickly to osaekomi-waza."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Falling without lifting uke first","fix":"Lift uke off the ground before sacrificing. Falling without the lift just puts you on your back under uke."},{"mistake":"Loose wrap — arms not fully encircling","fix":"The wrap must be secure all the way around uke's torso. A loose grip fails when uke resists the lift."},{"mistake":"No follow-through to hold-down","fix":"After the roll and throw, immediately transition to a hold-down. The throw is incomplete if uke is simply deposited and released."},{"mistake":"Falling to the side instead of straight back","fix":"Fall straight backward (ma-sutemi). Falling to the side changes the throw direction and is less controlled."}],"when":"Tawara-Gaeshi is used when uke is defensively bent forward with a strong lowered posture, or when uke has attempted a leg grab and is in a head-down position. The encircling grip takes advantage of uke's bent-over posture. It is also useful in newaza standing transitions when uke is on all fours.","setups":["morote-gari"],"followUps":["kesa-gatame"],"counters":[],"related":["hikikomi-gaeshi","sumi-gaeshi","tomoe-nage"],"source":"https://judolearn.com/techniques/tawara-gaeshi/"},{"id":"tomoe-nage","name":"Tomoe-Nage","kanji":"巴投","translation":"Circle Throw","category":"Nage-Waza","subcategory":"Ma-Sutemi-Waza","difficulty":2,"grade":"3rd kyu","grades":["3rd kyu","2nd kyu"],"videos":[{"yt":"880WbHvHv6A","title":"巴投 / Tomoe-nage","channel":"KODOKAN"},{"yt":"bOCHpINOE68","title":"Three Tomoe Nages You Must Know","channel":"Shintaro Higashi"},{"yt":"aYbhIenm_y0","title":"Tomoe Nage Secrets!! – 3 Variations | #judo #nyc #shintarohigashi","channel":"Shintaro Higashi"},{"yt":"7kxSClwagmU","title":"Top 10 Tomoe Nage and Yoko Tomoe Nage Compilation | 巴投","channel":"Judo Spirit"}],"overview":"Tomoe-Nage is a rear sacrifice throw where tori falls backward, places their foot in uke's stomach, and uses leg extension and arm pulling to project uke up and over in a circular arc. It is highly spectacular, scoring frequently in competition when executed well. As a sacrifice throw, tori deliberately sacrifices their own standing position to throw uke.","phases":[{"label":"Kuzushi · Off-balance","text":"Pull uke sharply forward to break their balance to the front. Your arms drive uke's upper body forward toward you as you fall backward, loading uke's weight forward over your falling body."},{"label":"Tsukuri · Entry","text":"As you pull uke forward, step your right foot in front of uke and begin falling backward. As your back contacts the mat, plant your right foot against uke's lower abdomen — this is the launch post. The foot placement is at the abdomen, not necessarily between their feet."},{"label":"Kake · Execution","text":"From your back, extend your right leg powerfully upward, using uke's loaded body weight as resistance. Pull both grips toward your hips. The foot in uke's stomach combined with full leg extension projects uke over your head in a circular arc."}],"steps":[{"title":"Pull uke sharply forward and step in","text":"Pull both hands toward you. Step your right foot between uke's feet and begin falling backward."},{"title":"Plant your right foot in uke's abdomen as you fall","text":"As your back meets the ground, ensure your right foot is planted firmly in uke's lower abdomen (just below the belt). This foot is your launch post."},{"title":"Extend your leg powerfully upward","text":"Extend your right leg, driving uke upward and over. The extension amplifies the momentum of uke's forward-falling body."},{"title":"Pull with both arms toward your hips","text":"Pull both grips down toward your hips simultaneously. The arm pull and leg push work in opposite directions to rotate uke over your body."},{"title":"Guide uke's landing","text":"The circular motion should project uke over your head to land on the mat behind you. Maintain grip for ne-waza transition."}],"principles":["Foot placement in the lower abdomen (not chest) gives the best mechanical leverage for projection.","The fall must be controlled — you are choosing to go to the mat, not falling accidentally.","Full kuzushi forward is essential before you fall. If uke has backward posture, they will simply step over you.","Leg extension must be powerful and timed with the arm pull — both elements together create the circular projection."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Foot too high (at the chest instead of abdomen)","fix":"Place the foot at the lower abdomen/belt level. Too high reduces the lever distance."},{"mistake":"Falling without forward kuzushi","fix":"Pull uke forward before you fall. If uke is balanced, they step over you and you are in a bad position."},{"mistake":"Arm pull too late — not synchronized with leg extension","fix":"The arm pull and leg extension must be simultaneous. The combination creates the circular throw."}],"when":"Tomoe-Nage is most effective as a reactive or counter throw — when uke rushes in aggressively or pushes forward, their own momentum combines with your technique. Also effective when the opponent is squarely in front of you with a stiff, forward posture.","setups":["seoi-nage"],"followUps":[],"counters":["tani-otoshi"],"related":["sumi-gaeshi","tani-otoshi","seoi-nage"],"source":"https://judolearn.com/techniques/tomoe-nage/"},{"id":"tsubame-gaeshi","name":"Tsubame-Gaeshi","kanji":"燕返し","translation":"Swallow Counter","category":"Nage-Waza","subcategory":"Ashi-Waza","difficulty":3,"grade":"2nd kyu","grades":["2nd kyu","1st kyu"],"videos":[{"yt":"GwweWqqFB5g","title":"燕返 / Tsubame-gaeshi","channel":"KODOKAN"},{"yt":"FQ3jP6fKlIo","title":"Concept of Tsubame Gaeshi Swallow Counter by Travis Stevens","channel":"JudoFanatics"},{"yt":"m_HEibNx3Gw","title":"Tsubame-gaeshi - Demo","channel":"Efficient Judo"},{"yt":"vAGzSVCA8VU","title":"One of the hardest techniques in Judo - Tsubame Gaeshi","channel":"Grappler Kingdom"}],"overview":"Tsubame-Gaeshi (Swallow Counter) is a counter-throw specifically designed to defeat De-Ashi-Barai. When tori attempts De-Ashi-Barai against uke's advancing foot, uke lifts the targeted foot up and over tori's sweeping leg in a rapid swallow-like arc, then immediately sweeps tori's own standing (base) foot in the same flowing motion. The name evokes the swift, curved flight of a swallow in flight. It is part of the Kodokan Shinmeisho no Waza and represents one of the most elegant counter-sweep sequences in judo.","phases":[{"label":"Kuzushi · Off-balance","text":"Kuzushi is derived entirely from uke's commitment to the original De-Ashi-Barai sweep. As tori sweeps and shifts their body weight to the sweeping side, their remaining base foot becomes the vulnerable target. Uke's evasion leaves tori momentarily overextended and single-legged — an ideal condition to sweep the standing foot backward."},{"label":"Tsukuri · Entry","text":"As tori's sweeping foot approaches, lift the targeted foot rapidly upward and across tori's sweeping leg. Your weight shifts fully to the opposite (standing) foot as you arc over. Immediately redirect your lifted foot downward in a sweeping path toward tori's base ankle. The transition from evasion to sweep is continuous — there is no pause."},{"label":"Kake · Execution","text":"Sweep tori's standing ankle or foot with the sole of your foot in a lateral or backward-lateral arc, mirroring the mechanics of De-Ashi-Barai itself. Simultaneously pull tori's arms (or release your grips to redirect momentum) to amplify the toppling force. The sweep removes tori's only ground contact at the precise moment their weight is fully committed to it."}],"steps":[{"title":"Detect the incoming De-Ashi-Barai","text":"Read tori's grip tension and body positioning. A De-Ashi-Barai attempt is typically preceded by a pull on the sleeve grip as tori loads for the sweep. Visual and tactile cues give a fraction-of-a-second warning."},{"title":"Lift the targeted foot up and over the sweep","text":"As tori's sweeping foot arrives, rapidly elevate your targeted foot, arcing it upward and over tori's sweeping leg in a smooth curve. The motion should be swift and continuous — think of a swallow banking in flight."},{"title":"Land your weight on the opposite (base) foot","text":"As your lifted foot crosses over tori's sweep, all your weight momentarily rests on the opposite foot. This is also the foot you will sweep from, so the transition must be immediate."},{"title":"Redirect into the counter sweep","text":"Without pausing, redirect the arcing foot downward into a sweep toward tori's now-loaded base ankle. The path from evasion into sweep is a single arc — the foot goes up and over, then comes down and sweeps."},{"title":"Coordinate the arm pull to complete the throw","text":"Pull tori's arms (sleeve and lapel) in the direction of the sweep to unbalance their upper body in concert with the foot sweep. Tori's weight is committed to the sweep side — the arm pull and counter sweep combine to topple them."}],"principles":["The counter must flow as one continuous motion — foot lifts, arcs over, and sweeps in a single swallow-like curve. Any hesitation between evasion and sweep allows tori to recover.","Timing the evasion requires reading tori's intentions before the sweep arrives. Reacting to the sweep after contact is too late.","Your own counter sweep uses the same mechanics as De-Ashi-Barai — sole of foot, lateral sweep, at peak weight transfer. The principles that make De-Ashi-Barai effective apply equally here.","This technique demonstrates a fundamental judo principle: an attacker who over-commits to a technique creates the opening for their opponent to counter.","The swallow imagery is instructional — think of the light, curved, decisive motion of a bird in flight, not a heavy kick."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Lifting the foot too slowly — swept before evading","fix":"The evasion must be triggered at the first signal of the incoming sweep, not after contact. Train the foot-lift as a rapid reflex response to the grip tension that precedes De-Ashi-Barai."},{"mistake":"Pausing between evasion and counter sweep","fix":"The arc over and the counter sweep are one motion. Practice the combined sequence as a single fluid action until the pause disappears."},{"mistake":"Sweeping the wrong foot — sweeping toward tori's sweeping side instead of the base","fix":"Target tori's base foot — the foot they are standing on while they sweep. The sweeping foot is already mobile and carries no weight."},{"mistake":"No arm control — counter sweep has no upper-body amplification","fix":"Coordinate arm pull with the sweep. The foot sweep alone may not be sufficient; the arm direction collapses tori's upper body in concert."}],"when":"Tsubame-Gaeshi is the dedicated counter to De-Ashi-Barai and is most effective when an opponent repeatedly attacks with foot sweeps. It can also be applied against Okuri-Ashi-Barai and similar lateral foot sweeps where one foot is lifted and the other is loaded. Competitors who drill this counter shift the dynamic of any foot-sweep heavy game entirely.","setups":[],"followUps":["de-ashi-barai","okuri-ashi-barai"],"counters":[],"related":["de-ashi-barai","okuri-ashi-barai","harai-tsurikomi-ashi"],"source":"https://judolearn.com/techniques/tsubame-gaeshi/"},{"id":"tsukkomi-jime","name":"Tsukkomi-Jime","kanji":"突込絞","translation":"Thrusting Choke","category":"Katame-Waza","subcategory":"Shime-Waza","difficulty":2,"grade":"3rd kyu","grades":["3rd kyu","2nd kyu"],"videos":[{"yt":"dKKpnD3eLcY","title":"突込絞 /  Tsukkomi-jime","channel":"KODOKAN"},{"yt":"2q8YhcvVT2w","title":"GOKYO - Clip 088 : Tsukkomi-jime","channel":"Judo Canada"},{"yt":"ajGnlLcMiB0","title":"Tsukkomi Jime (Thrust Choke) - Judo technique for BJJ","channel":"Seiryoku Zenyo"}],"overview":"Tsukkomi-Jime is a collar choke where one or both hands thrust into uke's collar in a specific way to create a choking pressure. \"Tsukkomi\" means to thrust in. Tori grips uke's collar with cross-grip or palm-upward grip and drives the knuckle or forearm into the side of uke's neck, compressing the carotid. It is a technically precise choke that leverages the collar for additional control.","phases":[],"steps":[{"title":"Establish collar grip","text":"Grip uke's lapel near the collar with a cross-grip (right hand reaching to uke's right collar). A deep grip close to the collar is required for the thrusting action."},{"title":"Position the gripping hand — palm up or cross grip","text":"Turn the gripping hand palm-upward. The edge of the forearm near the thumb side should face the neck. Or use a cross-grip where one hand reaches deep to the opposite collar."},{"title":"Thrust the grip into uke's neck","text":"Drive the forearm edge or knuckles into the side of uke's neck — compressing the carotid. The thrusting motion gives the technique its name."},{"title":"Add secondary grip or pressure","text":"The free hand can assist by pushing or pulling to increase neck pressure, or gripping the collar on the other side."},{"title":"Maintain consistent compression","text":"Hold the thrusting compression steady. Combined with body weight in a top position, this creates effective carotid pressure."}],"principles":["The \"thrust\" mechanics — driving the edge of the hand or forearm into the neck — is the defining feature. It is not a squeeze; it is a thrusting press.","Collar depth matters — a shallow collar grip cannot reach the neck effectively. Grip deep, near uke's collar.","The palm-upward grip position rotates the forearm to present its sharpest edge to the neck.","Body weight from a top position amplifies the thrusting pressure significantly."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Shallow collar grip — edge of forearm cannot reach neck","fix":"Grip deep into the collar — as close to uke's neck as possible. The forearm or knuckle must contact the carotid, not the lapel."},{"mistake":"Thrusting at the trachea instead of the carotid","fix":"Target the sides of the neck, not the front. Tracheal pressure is illegal and dangerous."},{"mistake":"No body weight addition","fix":"Apply the choke from a top position and drive body weight through the arms. Without weight, pure arm force is limited."},{"mistake":"Palm-down grip — wrong forearm rotation","fix":"The palm-up (or thumb-toward-neck) grip presents the correct edge of the forearm. Palm-down places the flat surface on the neck with less compression."}],"when":"Tsukkomi-Jime is applied from top positions — mount, side control, or knee-on-belly — where the collar is accessible. It is effective when uke's defense focuses on pushing tori's arms away, as the thrusting action drives inward despite outward resistance.","setups":[],"followUps":["tate-shiho-gatame","kesa-gatame"],"counters":[],"related":["nami-juji-jime","gyaku-juji-jime","kata-juji-jime","ryo-te-jime"],"source":"https://judolearn.com/techniques/tsukkomi-jime/"},{"id":"tsuri-goshi","name":"Tsuri-Goshi","kanji":"釣腰","translation":"Lifting Hip Throw","category":"Nage-Waza","subcategory":"Koshi-Waza","difficulty":2,"grade":"4th kyu","grades":["4th kyu","3rd kyu"],"videos":[{"yt":"51Htlp7xEvE","title":"釣腰 / Tsuri-goshi","channel":"KODOKAN"},{"yt":"wKx5sjOZ65I","title":"How To Do Tsuri Goshi || From The Lapel","channel":"The Judo Way of Life"},{"yt":"mrEGyOwJjXQ","title":"Tsuri-Goshi Tips | Riki Judo Dojo","channel":"Riki Judo Dojo"},{"yt":"2ucxMrlW2zw","title":"Tsuri-Goshi Compilation","channel":"Ochiru"}],"overview":"Tsuri-Goshi is a hip throw distinguished by a lifting grip on uke's belt at the back. Rather than wrapping the waist with the arm (as in O-Goshi), tori reaches around and grips uke's belt, then lifts and loads uke's weight onto the hip before projecting them forward. This belt grip provides exceptional control over uke's centre of gravity and makes the throw difficult to escape once established.","phases":[{"label":"Kuzushi · Off-balance","text":"Draw uke forward and upward with the sleeve grip, pulling them onto their right front corner. Simultaneously, the belt-reaching arm pulls uke's mid-section in close so their hips are pressed against yours. Uke should rise onto their toes with their weight shifted forward."},{"label":"Tsukuri · Entry","text":"Step your right foot in between uke's feet, then pivot 180 degrees to face the same direction as uke. As you pivot, reach your right arm behind uke and grip their belt at the back (or lower back of the judogi). Pull the belt grip toward you as you complete the turn, pressing uke's hips firmly into your hip region."},{"label":"Kake · Execution","text":"With the belt grip lifting uke's hips upward and the sleeve arm pulling down and across, bend forward from the waist. The combination of the belt lift and the forward bend pivots uke over your hip. Uke is projected in a high arc over your hip to land on the mat in front of you."}],"steps":[{"title":"Break balance with sleeve and pull","text":"Pull uke's sleeve upward and toward their right front corner. Your lapel hand simultaneously draws their upper body in close."},{"title":"Step in with the right foot","text":"Drive your right foot in front of uke's right foot, planting it between their feet with toes angled outward."},{"title":"Pivot and establish belt grip","text":"Pivot 180 degrees, swinging your left foot around. As you turn, reach your right arm behind uke and grip the back of their belt firmly."},{"title":"Pull belt and load uke's weight","text":"Draw the belt grip toward you while pressing your hip into uke. Bend your knees so your hip is slightly lower than uke's centre of gravity, then straighten to load their weight."},{"title":"Lift, bend, and project","text":"Lift with the belt grip while bending forward sharply. Uke is lifted and rotated over your hip, landing to the mat in front of you."}],"principles":["The belt grip is the defining feature — grip deep at the back of the belt for maximum lifting leverage.","Pull uke's hips in tight before the throw; any space between your hip and uke's breaks the lifting fulcrum.","Lift with the belt arm and bend with the body simultaneously — both actions together power the rotation.","Keep your back straight during entry; a rounded back during the pivot weakens hip placement."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Gripping the back of the jacket instead of the belt","fix":"The belt grip provides a rigid, high-leverage hold on uke's centre of gravity. Gripping loose fabric gives poor control."},{"mistake":"Hip placed too high, lifting uke off the ground inefficiently","fix":"Lower your body slightly during entry so the hip is at or below uke's hip. Straighten the legs to load rather than pressing up with the hip."},{"mistake":"Insufficient forward bend during kake","fix":"The throw requires the torso to drive forward and down. Staying upright with just a belt lift produces a weak, incomplete throw."},{"mistake":"Not completing the pivot before gripping the belt","fix":"Establish your full body position first, then secure the belt grip. Grabbing the belt mid-turn results in a poor hip angle."}],"when":"Tsuri-Goshi is particularly effective against opponents who drop their hips defensively or who grip low. The belt grip bypasses arm and upper body defences, going straight to their centre of mass. It is useful in situations where a standard lapel-to-waist entry (O-Goshi) is defended, and works well as a transition from a failed grip-fighting exchange.","setups":[],"followUps":["de-ashi-barai","o-uchi-gari"],"counters":["tani-otoshi","ura-nage"],"related":["o-goshi","koshi-guruma","sode-tsuri-komi-goshi"],"source":"https://judolearn.com/techniques/tsuri-goshi/"},{"id":"tsurikomi-goshi","name":"Tsurikomi-Goshi","kanji":"釣込腰","translation":"Lifting-Pulling Hip Throw","category":"Nage-Waza","subcategory":"Koshi-Waza","difficulty":2,"grade":"4th kyu","grades":["4th kyu","3rd kyu"],"videos":[{"yt":"McfzA0yRVt4","title":"釣込腰 / Tsurikomi-goshi","channel":"KODOKAN"},{"yt":"yPDjCvXE78Y","title":"Tsurikomi-Goshi Tips | Riki Judo Dojo","channel":"Riki Judo Dojo"},{"yt":"sOrhZaqFgMQ","title":"The BEST Way to Throw Tsurikomi Goshi (ON ANY OPPONENT!)","channel":"DadBod Judo"},{"yt":"rAKWA_HJFaM","title":"Tsurikomi-Goshi Compilation","channel":"Ochiru"}],"overview":"Tsurikomi-Goshi is a hip throw that adds a lifting collar grip to the standard hip throw mechanics. Tori grips uke's collar high with the lapel hand and simultaneously pulls the sleeve upward — this \"lifting-pulling\" (tsuri-komi) action on the collar drives uke onto their toes and breaks their balance more effectively than a standard grip. The hip then acts as the fulcrum for the throw.","phases":[{"label":"Kuzushi · Off-balance","text":"The tsuri-komi (lifting-pulling) action is the defining feature: tori pulls uke's lapel upward with the collar grip while simultaneously pulling the sleeve forward. The collar grip lifts uke's collar and chin, forcing them to rise up onto their toes. This combination of upward collar pressure and forward sleeve pull creates powerful forward kuzushi that is difficult for uke to resist."},{"label":"Tsukuri · Entry","text":"Step the right foot in between uke's feet and pivot to place your back against uke's chest. The collar hand grips high — near uke's right lapel — and pulls upward to keep uke rising. The entry is compact with back-to-chest contact. Bend the knees to position the hip below uke's hip."},{"label":"Kake · Execution","text":"Straighten the legs to load uke onto the hip, maintaining the upward collar pull throughout. Bend forward at the waist and pull both grips forward — the collar pull guides uke's upper body over the hip while the sleeve pull directs their arm. Uke is projected over the hip and to the mat."}],"steps":[{"title":"Grip the collar high and establish sleeve grip","text":"Grip uke's right lapel near the collar with your left hand. Grip the sleeve with your right hand. The collar grip should be high — near uke's chin level."},{"title":"Apply tsuri-komi — lift the collar, pull the sleeve","text":"Pull the collar upward while simultaneously pulling the sleeve forward. This drives uke's chin up and forces them onto their toes. This is the defining kuzushi of the technique."},{"title":"Step in and pivot","text":"Step right foot in between uke's feet. Pivot to place your back against uke's chest. Keep the collar pull active throughout the entry."},{"title":"Position the hip and bend the knees","text":"Your right hip must be below and in front of uke's hip. Bend your knees as you enter so the hip is at the correct level."},{"title":"Throw over the hip","text":"Straighten your legs, bend forward at the waist, and pull both grips forward. Uke is levered over your hip onto the mat."}],"principles":["The upward collar pull is the key feature — it forces uke to rise, making the hip entry much more effective than with a standard lapel grip.","The collar grip must stay high and pulling upward throughout the throw. Dropping the collar grip negates the tsuri-komi effect.","The two grips work together: collar lifts uke up, sleeve pulls uke forward. Both must be active simultaneously for maximum kuzushi.","The hip entry mechanics are the same as O-Goshi and Uki-Goshi — mastery of those techniques simplifies learning Tsurikomi-Goshi."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Collar grip too low — no lifting effect","fix":"The collar grip must be high, near uke's chin. A low grip cannot lift the collar and provides no tsuri-komi action."},{"mistake":"Dropping the collar pull during the entry step","fix":"Maintain the upward collar pull from the moment of kuzushi through to the completion of the throw. Dropping it allows uke to lower and defend."},{"mistake":"Collar and sleeve pulling in the same direction","fix":"The collar pulls upward, the sleeve pulls forward. They are two different vectors that together create the powerful rotational kuzushi."},{"mistake":"Hip too high during entry","fix":"Bend the knees sufficiently to place the hip below uke's hip. A high hip cannot act as a fulcrum for a throw."}],"when":"Tsurikomi-Goshi is particularly effective against opponents with a bent-forward posture — the upward collar pull straightens their posture and drives them up onto their toes, creating the kuzushi needed for the hip throw. It is also used when a standard hip throw grip has been blocked, as the high collar grip bypasses sleeve defenses.","setups":[],"followUps":["ko-uchi-gari","o-uchi-gari"],"counters":["tani-otoshi","ura-nage"],"related":["o-goshi","uki-goshi","hane-goshi","harai-goshi"],"source":"https://judolearn.com/techniques/tsurikomi-goshi/"},{"id":"uchi-mata","name":"Uchi-Mata","kanji":"内股","translation":"Inner Thigh Throw","category":"Nage-Waza","subcategory":"Ashi-Waza","difficulty":3,"grade":"2nd kyu","grades":["2nd kyu","1st kyu"],"videos":[{"yt":"iUpSu5J-bgw","title":"内股 / Uchi-mata","channel":"KODOKAN"},{"yt":"N9lpgFLKqvE","title":"Uchi-mata - Basic principles","channel":"Efficient Judo"},{"yt":"uliAqzasG4g","title":"1-Step, 2-Step & 3-Step Uchimata","channel":"Shintaro Higashi"},{"yt":"0aO_wKPmRsk","title":"7 Types of Japanese Judo Uchimata","channel":"Judo Highlights"},{"yt":"n6Fj7xkmFEI","title":"Best of JUDO UCHI MATA 【内股】","channel":"Judo Art"}],"overview":"Uchi-Mata is the most frequently scored technique in elite judo competition and widely regarded as one of the most powerful and versatile throws in judo. Tori enters from a forward-angled pivot and sweeps their leg upward between uke's legs, contacting the inner thigh to propel them forward and over. It can be executed from multiple grips, both sides, and both standing and drop entries.","phases":[{"label":"Kuzushi · Off-balance","text":"Pull uke forward and to their right front corner. Your sleeve hand pulls uke's elbow across your centerline and slightly upward. Your lapel hand drives forward and upward. Uke must be leaning forward with weight transferring to the balls of their feet."},{"label":"Tsukuri · Entry","text":"Step your right foot in front of uke at a slight angle, then pivot so your back faces uke at roughly 45 degrees. Unlike a full hip turn, Uchi-Mata uses a forward-angled position. Your left foot lands to give you a base, but the key is driving your right leg forward and up."},{"label":"Kake · Execution","text":"Drive your right leg forward and then sweep it powerfully upward between uke's legs, contacting the inner thigh of uke's right leg with the inner surface of your right thigh/hamstring. Simultaneously pull forward and rotate to project uke over your hip in a forward arc. The sweeping leg drives uke's legs upward while your arms project their upper body forward."}],"steps":[{"title":"Break uke's balance to their right front corner","text":"Pull the sleeve arm across your centerline and up. Drive the lapel arm forward. Uke's weight must be forward before entry."},{"title":"Step in at a 45-degree angle","text":"Step your right foot in front of uke but at a 45-degree angle — not a full hip turn. Your body faces forward-diagonally, not completely back-to-uke."},{"title":"Pivot and load on your left leg","text":"Pivot on your right foot, bringing your left foot to establish a one-legged base. Your weight loads onto your left leg — all sweeping power comes from this leg position."},{"title":"Drive right leg forward and sweep upward","text":"Swing your right leg forward (pendulum motion) and then drive it powerfully upward between uke's legs. Contact the inner surface of your right thigh against the inside of uke's thigh."},{"title":"Project uke forward over your hip","text":"Pull forward strongly with both hands while the leg sweep elevates uke's legs. Uke is projected forward in a high arc. Maintain grip for follow-through to ne-waza."}],"principles":["The sweep is between uke's legs, contacting the inner thigh — not a hip throw hitting the outer thigh.","The forward-angled pivot distinguishes Uchi-Mata from hip throws: you are not fully back-to-uke.","The leg sweep drives upward with force — the higher you can elevate uke's leg, the more complete the throw.","Uchi-Mata can be done from multiple grip configurations: standard sleeve-lapel, belt, or over-the-back grips all work.","Both sides of Uchi-Mata should be trained — switching from right to left is a high-level competition tactic."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Pivoting too fully — body turned too far like a hip throw","fix":"Uchi-Mata uses a partial turn. Over-rotating means your leg cannot penetrate between uke's legs effectively."},{"mistake":"Sweeping at the knee instead of the inner thigh","fix":"Drive the leg deep between uke's legs. Contact at the knee is less effective and can be illegal in competition."},{"mistake":"Not committing the whole body to the forward projection","fix":"The throw requires your entire body moving forward and down — not just the leg. Pull with both hands strongly during kake."},{"mistake":"Insufficient kuzushi — entering against an upright, grounded uke","fix":"Uchi-Mata against a stable uke results in a blocked, dangerous position. Never enter without prior kuzushi."}],"when":"Uchi-Mata is effective against all body types but particularly against same-height or taller opponents — the inner thigh target is easier to reach. It works best when uke is in a rounded or defensive posture (more inner thigh exposed). Classic combinations: Harai-Goshi → Uchi-Mata (switch outer to inner), Ko-Uchi-Gari → Uchi-Mata, Osoto-Gari → Uchi-Mata. Both standing and drop (drop knee to mat) entries should be trained — drop Uchi-Mata is heavily used in elite competition.","setups":[],"followUps":["harai-goshi","ko-uchi-gari","osoto-gari"],"counters":["uchi-mata-sukashi","uchi-mata-gaeshi","tani-otoshi"],"related":["harai-goshi","ko-uchi-gari","osoto-gari","o-goshi"],"source":"https://judolearn.com/techniques/uchi-mata/"},{"id":"uchi-mata-gaeshi","name":"Uchi-Mata-Gaeshi","kanji":"内股返","translation":"Uchi-Mata Counter","category":"Nage-Waza","subcategory":"Ashi-Waza","difficulty":3,"grade":"1st kyu","grades":["1st kyu","1st dan"],"videos":[{"yt":"Sy6sLWxkWYw","title":"内股返 / Uchi-mata-gaeshi","channel":"KODOKAN"},{"yt":"rzOSmEiuVtg","title":"Uchi-mata-gaeshi - Demo","channel":"Efficient Judo"},{"yt":"v1zv_EIqDZ4","title":"Uchi Mata Gaeshi - Judo - Uchi-Mata-Gaeshi","channel":"Passion Judo"},{"yt":"gF78XQekQJU","title":"Uchi Mata Gaeshi Classic","channel":"The Art of Balance Dojo"}],"overview":"Uchi-Mata-Gaeshi is the catching counter to Uchi-Mata. As uke penetrates between tori's legs with the sweeping leg, tori actively catches uke's sweeping leg by clamping their thighs together, immobilizing it. With uke now trapped in this committed position, tori steps forward and drives uke downward to the mat — forward or laterally. This technique should not be confused with Uchi-Mata-Sukashi, which counters by avoiding (slipping) the sweep. Uchi-Mata-Gaeshi counters by catching and using uke's committed sweeping leg as the mechanism of defeat. Part of the Kodokan Shinmeisho no Waza.","phases":[{"label":"Kuzushi · Off-balance","text":"Kuzushi comes from uke's absolute commitment to the Uchi-Mata penetration. When uke drives their sweeping leg between tori's legs and rotates their hip across, they are entirely single-legged and committed to the throw direction. By catching the leg and stepping forward, tori redirects this committed force downward, creating a forward off-balance that uke cannot recover from."},{"label":"Tsukuri · Entry","text":"As uke's sweeping leg rises between your thighs, clamp both thighs together to trap it. Simultaneously drop your own center of gravity slightly, sinking your hips to lower your base. Your grips stabilize uke's upper body to prevent them from adjusting or releasing."},{"label":"Kake · Execution","text":"With uke's sweeping leg trapped, step or drive your base forward toward uke's supporting foot. Press your body weight forward and downward through uke's trapped position, forcing uke to fall forward or to the side. The trapped leg prevents uke from stepping away to recover."}],"steps":[{"title":"Read uke's Uchi-Mata penetration","text":"Detect the hip rotation and inner thigh drive that characterize Uchi-Mata entry. The moment uke's leg begins to rise between your thighs, the catching counter window opens."},{"title":"Clamp your thighs to catch and trap uke's sweeping leg","text":"As uke's leg rises, actively squeeze both thighs together to trap uke's sweeping leg between them. The clamp must be firm — this is the core mechanic that immobilizes uke's attack."},{"title":"Sink your hips slightly to lower your center","text":"Drop your center of gravity slightly as you clamp, stabilizing your own base. This also makes the clamp more secure by lowering your thigh contact point."},{"title":"Step forward to drive your weight into uke","text":"Step forward toward uke's supporting foot or step diagonally. Drive your body weight forward through uke's upper body, loading uke's balance forward and downward."},{"title":"Direct uke down to the mat","text":"Continue pressing forward, using grip control to guide uke's fall — either directly forward or diagonally to the side. Uke's trapped leg prevents them from stepping out or recovering."}],"principles":["The distinction from Uchi-Mata-Sukashi is fundamental: Sukashi avoids the sweep (the leg slips through); Gaeshi catches it (the leg is clamped and held). Both counter Uchi-Mata but through opposite mechanisms.","The thigh clamp must be active and deliberate — not just closing legs together but actively squeezing to trap.","Once the leg is caught, uke is committed and immobile. The finish requires only forward pressure, not further technical complexity.","Speed of the catch is critical — if uke's leg rises too high before being clamped, the opportunity window narrows rapidly.","Grip control of uke's upper body during the catch prevents them from post-withdrawing the sweeping leg before the finish."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Confusing with Uchi-Mata-Sukashi and stepping aside instead of catching","fix":"Uchi-Mata-Gaeshi catches and uses the leg — you clamp your thighs together. Uchi-Mata-Sukashi avoids by stepping around. Decide which counter before uke attacks and commit to the choice."},{"mistake":"Insufficient thigh clamp — uke's leg escapes","fix":"The clamp must be firm and active. Practice the thigh squeeze independently until the muscle memory is strong."},{"mistake":"Stepping backward instead of forward during the finish","fix":"The finish requires driving forward toward uke's supporting leg base. Stepping backward removes your forward pressure and allows uke to withdraw."},{"mistake":"Releasing the grip on uke's upper body while clamping","fix":"Maintain grip control throughout. Losing the grip allows uke to adjust their upper body and possibly pull the sweeping leg free."}],"when":"Uchi-Mata-Gaeshi is most effective when uke drives deeply and commits heavily to Uchi-Mata. Opponents with a powerful, penetrating Uchi-Mata that rises high between the thighs are the ideal target. It can also be applied against Hane-Goshi and similar inner-leg raises. In competition, using Uchi-Mata-Gaeshi alongside Uchi-Mata-Sukashi as alternating counters creates unpredictability — uke cannot know whether their Uchi-Mata will be avoided or caught.","setups":[],"followUps":["uchi-mata","uchi-mata-sukashi"],"counters":[],"related":["uchi-mata","uchi-mata-sukashi"],"source":"https://judolearn.com/techniques/uchi-mata-gaeshi/"},{"id":"uchi-mata-makikomi","name":"Uchi-Mata-Makikomi","kanji":"内股巻込","translation":"Inner Thigh Wraparound","category":"Nage-Waza","subcategory":"Yoko-Sutemi-Waza","difficulty":2,"grade":"3rd kyu","grades":["3rd kyu","2nd kyu"],"videos":[{"yt":"jZXENTLpJCI","title":"内股巻込 / Uchi-mata-makikomi","channel":"KODOKAN"},{"yt":"AJy_muVxkaE","title":"Uchi-mata-makikomi - Demo","channel":"Efficient Judo"},{"yt":"YQbM82UrspQ","title":"Uchi Mata Maki Komi by Yarden Gerbi","channel":"JudoFanatics"},{"yt":"TLFlVOAQXQM","title":"Georgian Grip Uchi-mata-makikomi - Demo","channel":"Efficient Judo"}],"overview":"Uchi-Mata-Makikomi combines the inner thigh sweep of Uchi-Mata with the arm wrap and side fall of makikomi throws. Tori enters for Uchi-Mata and wraps uke's arm while falling to the side, adding sacrifice mechanics to the inner thigh technique. It is widely used in competition at all levels because Uchi-Mata is a foundational technique and the makikomi variant overcomes common defenses.","phases":[{"label":"Kuzushi · Off-balance","text":"Pull uke forward and upward — identical to Uchi-Mata kuzushi. The sleeve grip elevates and pulls forward while the lapel arm pulls uke's chest. Uke's weight is driven onto their right front corner. The added element of the arm wrap begins as the entry is made."},{"label":"Tsukuri · Entry","text":"Step in and pivot as for Uchi-Mata — right foot in between uke's feet, back to uke's chest. Simultaneously wrap the lapel-side arm around uke's arm (makikomi), pulling it against your chest. The inner thigh sweep leg is loaded for the entry."},{"label":"Kake · Execution","text":"Sweep the inner thigh with the left leg while wrapping the arm and falling to the side. The three forces converge: the sweep drives uke's legs sideways, the arm wrap controls their upper body, and the side fall provides the sacrificial throwing energy. Uke is thrown powerfully."}],"steps":[{"title":"Create forward kuzushi","text":"Pull uke forward and upward onto their front corner. Sleeve high and forward."},{"title":"Step in and pivot","text":"Step in with right foot between uke's feet. Pivot to back-facing. Begin wrapping uke's arm."},{"title":"Wrap the arm","text":"Your entry arm wraps around uke's arm and pulls it tightly to your chest. This is the makikomi element."},{"title":"Load the inner thigh sweep","text":"Swing the right leg back and up, targeting the inner thigh of uke's right leg."},{"title":"Sweep, wrap, and fall","text":"All three actions simultaneously: inner thigh sweep, arm wrap maintained, side fall. Uke is thrown to the mat."}],"principles":["This is one of the most frequently seen makikomi variants in competition — mastering it pays significant dividends.","The inner thigh sweep and the side fall must be simultaneous. The sweep generates the projection; the fall provides sacrifice energy.","Arm wrap tightness is critical. Loose wrap = uke posts out with that arm and defends.","Uchi-Mata-Makikomi is effective against defenders of standard Uchi-Mata, as the added sacrifice makes the throw harder to counter."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Not completing the sweep — relying only on the fall","fix":"The inner thigh sweep must be executed with full power. The fall amplifies the sweep but does not replace it."},{"mistake":"Falling before the sweep connects","fix":"The sweep must make contact before or simultaneously with the fall. Falling early sends you down without throwing uke."},{"mistake":"Loose arm wrap","fix":"Trap uke's arm firmly against your body. This is what distinguishes makikomi from the standard throw."},{"mistake":"Insufficient kuzushi","fix":"Forward kuzushi must be created before entry. Entering without kuzushi means uke is planted and the sweep meets a solid, resisting base."}],"when":"Uchi-Mata-Makikomi is used when standard Uchi-Mata is being countered by uke. The commitment of the sacrifice — combined with the arm wrap — overcomes many counter-attempts. It is widely used in shiai at all levels by practitioners whose base technique is Uchi-Mata.","setups":[],"followUps":["ko-uchi-gari","o-uchi-gari"],"counters":["tani-otoshi"],"related":["uchi-mata","harai-makikomi","hane-makikomi","o-soto-makikomi"],"source":"https://judolearn.com/techniques/uchi-mata-makikomi/"},{"id":"uchi-mata-sukashi","name":"Uchi-Mata-Sukashi","kanji":"内股透","translation":"Inner Thigh Void","category":"Nage-Waza","subcategory":"Te-Waza","difficulty":3,"grade":"1st kyu","grades":["1st kyu","1st dan"],"videos":[{"yt":"V-RS3uhtVWM","title":"内股すかし / Uchi-mata-sukashi","channel":"KODOKAN"},{"yt":"JvypPP1-5Iw","title":"The secret method to counter Uchi mata, uchi mata sukashi","channel":"FLUID JUDO JAPAN"},{"yt":"n2k0d5YKTHg","title":"Uchi mata sukashi (counter uchi mata) - Sumi otoshi - Uki otoshi","channel":"Aris judo Club"},{"yt":"Say5B5bNIcQ","title":"Uchi-mata Sukashi compilation","channel":"Ochiru"}],"overview":"Uchi-Mata-Sukashi is an advanced counter-technique specifically designed to neutralize and reverse Uchi-Mata. When uke attempts Uchi-Mata, tori steps over the sweeping leg (avoiding it), shifts their weight to the other side, and uses the momentum of uke's failed attack to throw them. It is one of judo's most sophisticated counters and requires an intimate understanding of Uchi-Mata mechanics.","phases":[{"label":"Kuzushi · Off-balance","text":"Kuzushi is provided by uke's own failed Uchi-Mata attempt. When uke commits to sweeping tori's inner thigh and tori voids the sweep by stepping over or lifting their leg, uke's sweeping leg misses and they are briefly off-balance. Tori exploits this moment of imbalance to project uke using their own committed force."},{"label":"Tsukuri · Entry","text":"As uke enters for Uchi-Mata, tori lifts the threatened leg slightly or steps it aside to allow uke's sweep to pass through the void. Simultaneously, tori maintains hand grips and shifts their weight to the opposite side. The position at this moment sets up the reversal — tori must be close, grips secure, and weight balanced."},{"label":"Kake · Execution","text":"Once uke's sweeping leg passes through without contact (the \"sukashi\" — void), tori pulls with the sleeve and lapel grips to accelerate uke's rotation in the direction they were already moving. Tori may also drive with the hip or use a leg block to assist. Uke, committed and off-balance, continues in their rotation and falls."}],"steps":[{"title":"Recognize uke's Uchi-Mata entry","text":"Read uke's body language as they step in, pivot, and initiate the inner thigh sweep. This requires experience reading Uchi-Mata setups."},{"title":"Void the sweep","text":"Lift the threatened leg or step it to the outside just as uke's sweeping leg rises. The sweep passes through empty space — the \"sukashi.\""},{"title":"Maintain grips and shift weight","text":"Keep both grips active throughout. Shift your weight toward uke's committed side — the side they were sweeping toward."},{"title":"Pull and guide uke's rotation","text":"Pull the sleeve and lapel grips to accelerate uke's forward-and-over rotation. They are already committed and falling; you are guiding that fall."},{"title":"Project uke to the mat","text":"Uke completes the rotation they started with Uchi-Mata — but without tori's body as a target, they fall past and onto the mat."}],"principles":["Uchi-Mata-Sukashi is classified as te-waza because the counter relies entirely on hand mechanics — the void itself and the guiding pull.","The timing of the void is precise — too early and uke pulls back, too late and the sweep connects. The void must coincide with uke's committed leg drive.","Maintaining strong grips throughout is essential. Losing grip during the void loses control of the counter-throw direction.","This technique requires genuine mastery of Uchi-Mata mechanics — you cannot effectively counter what you do not understand."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Stepping away from uke instead of voiding in place","fix":"The void is created by lifting the leg or shifting the hip, not by retreating. Retreating breaks grip contact and loses the counter opportunity."},{"mistake":"Voiding too early — uke adjusts and continues","fix":"Time the void to coincide with uke's full commitment of the sweeping leg. If you void too early, uke can redirect or pull back."},{"mistake":"Losing grip during the transition","fix":"Keep both grips active throughout. The grips guide the direction of uke's fall and provide the pulling force for the counter-throw."},{"mistake":"Attempting Uchi-Mata-Sukashi as a primary attack","fix":"This is a counter technique. It requires uke to attack first. Attempting it as a primary technique against a non-attacking uke makes no mechanical sense."}],"when":"Uchi-Mata-Sukashi is used specifically as a counter to Uchi-Mata. It is most practical at higher competitive levels where Uchi-Mata is common in an opponent's repertoire. Develop it in combination drills: partner attacks with Uchi-Mata, you practice the counter. It requires repetition to develop the timing reflex.","setups":[],"followUps":["uchi-mata","harai-goshi"],"counters":[],"related":["uchi-mata","o-uchi-gari","sukui-nage"],"source":"https://judolearn.com/techniques/uchi-mata-sukashi/"},{"id":"ude-garami","name":"Ude-Garami","kanji":"腕絡","translation":"Arm Entanglement (Kimura Lock)","category":"Katame-Waza","subcategory":"Kansetsu-Waza","difficulty":2,"grade":"3rd kyu","grades":["3rd kyu","2nd kyu"],"videos":[{"yt":"AIlTvZb4RlE","title":"腕緘 /  Ude-garami","channel":"KODOKAN"},{"yt":"BXun3qpzpDk","title":"How to do Ude Garami","channel":"Sampson Judo"},{"yt":"2bQhSqwhq3s","title":"How to Set Up Ude Garami (Kimura) From Standing - Miku Takaichi Study","channel":"non-stop newaza"}],"overview":"Ude-Garami attacks a bent arm using a figure-four grip to rotate the shoulder joint. Known as the \"Kimura lock\" in BJJ and MMA (named after Masahiko Kimura, who used it to defeat Hélio Gracie in 1951), it applies torque to the shoulder by rotating the forearm in a direction the joint cannot follow. It can be applied from mount, side control, guard, or as a counter to Seoi-Nage entry.","phases":[],"steps":[{"title":"Isolate uke's arm in a bent position","text":"Control uke's right arm bent at approximately 90 degrees, with their elbow on the mat and forearm pointing upward. Their hand should be beside their head, palm facing down."},{"title":"Grip uke's right wrist with your left hand","text":"Your left hand grips uke's right wrist from on top, securing the bent arm."},{"title":"Thread your right arm under uke's right upper arm","text":"Reach your right arm under uke's right upper arm/tricep area and grip your own left wrist to form the figure-four. This is the control grip."},{"title":"Apply the shoulder rotation","text":"Rotate uke's forearm upward (toward their head) while simultaneously pulling their upper arm inward with your right arm. The figure-four creates a lever that rotates the shoulder joint."},{"title":"Maintain posture and increase pressure progressively","text":"Drive uke's hand toward their head and beyond while maintaining the figure-four. Increase pressure incrementally and release immediately on uke's tap."}],"principles":["The figure-four grip is the core of the technique — without it, rotation is limited.","The forearm rotates upward toward uke's head — the direction that stresses the shoulder.","Keep uke's elbow close to their body. If the elbow flares out, the shoulder angle changes unfavorably.","This technique can be applied from multiple positions — mount, side control, North-South, even standing as a counter to Seoi-Nage entry."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Figure-four grip too shallow","fix":"Thread your arm deeply under uke's upper arm to get the figure-four wrist-to-wrist. Shallow grip means less control."},{"mistake":"Rotating the forearm toward the feet (wrong direction)","fix":"Rotate toward the head — driving uke's hand toward their shoulder and beyond. Downward rotation is away from the stress angle."},{"mistake":"Elbow allowed to flare outward","fix":"Keep uke's elbow pinned close to their side. Use your body weight to hold it in place."},{"mistake":"Applying suddenly without warning","fix":"Apply progressively. Ude-Garami can cause serious shoulder injury if torque is applied rapidly."}],"when":"Ude-Garami is effective whenever uke's arm is bent and you control their upper body. Classic setup: from side control, pin uke's near arm, thread the figure-four, apply. Also a powerful counter to Seoi-Nage — when tori enters for the throw and exposes their bent arm, uke can apply Ude-Garami around tori's shoulder. In BJJ/MMA it is known as the Kimura and is commonly used from the guard; note that guard-based ne-waza is restricted in IJF competition judo.","setups":[],"followUps":["yoko-shiho-gatame","juji-gatame"],"counters":[],"related":["juji-gatame","yoko-shiho-gatame","seoi-nage"],"source":"https://judolearn.com/techniques/ude-garami/"},{"id":"ude-gatame","name":"Ude-Gatame","kanji":"腕固","translation":"Arm Lock","category":"Katame-Waza","subcategory":"Kansetsu-Waza","difficulty":3,"grade":"2nd kyu","grades":["2nd kyu","1st kyu","1st dan"],"videos":[{"yt":"SBf0aTma1VI","title":"腕挫腕固 /  Ude-hishigi-ude-gatame","channel":"KODOKAN"},{"yt":"BnTsCwHEO_w","title":"How to do Ude Gatame","channel":"Sampson Judo"},{"yt":"AWYXr3UQeOk","title":"The Other Judo Armlock: Ude-Gatame","channel":"Shintaro Higashi"},{"yt":"zvLVDr3zj0U","title":"Ude Garami Into Ude Gatame by Matt D'Aquino","channel":"JudoFanatics"}],"overview":"Ude-Gatame is a straight armlock that hyperextends uke's elbow by pressing the arm against tori's chest or body using both hands on the wrist and forearm. Unlike Juji-Gatame which uses the hips as the fulcrum, Ude-Gatame uses tori's own body as a frame to lever the elbow joint. It can be applied from various positions including guard, side control, or while kneeling, and is characterised by the use of the body (rather than a body part placed at the elbow) to create the locking pressure.","phases":[{"label":"Kuzushi · Off-balance","text":"Secure uke's wrist firmly and extend their arm. Rotate uke's arm so the elbow faces upward or in the direction of intended leverage. Keep uke's shoulder controlled to prevent them from rotating the entire arm to escape."},{"label":"Tsukuri · Entry","text":"Grip uke's wrist with both hands — one hand gripping the wrist itself, the other gripping just above the wrist on the forearm. Pull uke's arm straight and press the back of their wrist against your chest, shoulder, or armpit. Your body provides the fulcrum against which uke's elbow is levered."},{"label":"Kake · Execution","text":"With uke's wrist pressed against your body and the arm extended, pull the wrist into your chest while preventing elbow flexion. Your body acts as the rigid surface against which the wrist is pressed; the elbow, unable to flex forward into your body, is forced into hyperextension. Maintain firm body contact throughout — if uke's wrist pulls free from your chest, the lock releases."}],"steps":[{"title":"Secure the wrist with both hands","text":"Grip uke's wrist with two hands — wrist and forearm grip. Pull the arm extended and away from uke's body."},{"title":"Supinate uke's arm","text":"Rotate uke's arm palm-up (supinate). This aligns the elbow for hyperextension and prevents uke from bending the elbow into a natural defensive position."},{"title":"Pull the wrist to your chest","text":"Draw uke's wrist toward your chest, pressing the back of the wrist against your sternum or armpit. Your body becomes the lever base."},{"title":"Ensure the arm is straight","text":"Confirm uke's arm is fully extended with the elbow pointing away from your body. Any bend gives uke room to flex before the lock activates."},{"title":"Apply pressure through the wrist","text":"Pull the wrist into your body while preventing elbow flexion. The locked arm hyperextends over your body frame. Control the pressure — apply gradually."}],"principles":["Your body is the lever base — unlike other armlocks, there is no separate fulcrum placed at the elbow; your chest/shoulder presses against the arm.","The arm must be fully extended and supinated to create the hyperextension angle.","Two-hand wrist control is mandatory — one-hand grips allow wrist rotation that defeats the lock.","Uke's shoulder must be controlled to prevent arm rotation; a free shoulder can rotate the arm from a bad angle to a safe one."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Arm not fully extended before applying pressure","fix":"Pull the arm fully straight first. Any residual elbow bend gives uke range of motion to resist the lock."},{"mistake":"Single-hand wrist grip","fix":"Two hands are required. One on the wrist, one on the forearm prevents rotation. Single-hand grips allow uke to twist their arm and escape."},{"mistake":"Applying lock toward the natural elbow bend direction (palm down)","fix":"Supinate the arm (palm up) before applying pressure. Locking toward the natural bend direction is weaker and risks pressing on the wrist rather than hyperextending the elbow."},{"mistake":"Not controlling uke's shoulder","fix":"If uke can freely rotate their shoulder, they can redirect the force. Use your body position or a second contact point to limit shoulder rotation."}],"when":"Ude-Gatame is applied when uke extends a straight arm — particularly when posting on a straight arm from the bottom, when pushing tori away in a ground scramble, or when uke's arm is caught outstretched during a transition. It is a high-speed application technique well-suited to competition transitions because it can be applied quickly without repositioning to a classical Juji-Gatame setup.","setups":["tate-shiho-gatame"],"followUps":["juji-gatame"],"counters":[],"related":["juji-gatame","ude-garami","hiza-gatame"],"source":"https://judolearn.com/techniques/ude-gatame/"},{"id":"uki-gatame","name":"Uki-Gatame","kanji":"浮固","translation":"Floating Hold","category":"Katame-Waza","subcategory":"Osaekomi-Waza","difficulty":2,"grade":"3rd kyu","grades":["3rd kyu","2nd kyu"],"videos":[{"yt":"e_lAjik1SUM","title":"浮固 /  Uki-gatame","channel":"KODOKAN"},{"yt":"ecq4BdQIOLM","title":"How to do Uki Gatame (Traditional Version) | Floating Hold | Knee on Belly | 浮固","channel":"Sampson Judo"},{"yt":"AMHWDFR4ryo","title":"浮固の逃れ方 /  Uki-gatame Escapes","channel":"KODOKAN"},{"yt":"Yogj-r1yFzo","title":"Uki Gatame (Contest Version) | Floating Hold | 浮固","channel":"Sampson Judo"}],"overview":"Uki-Gatame is a hold-down where tori kneels on or beside uke and uses upper body pressure to hold them down without the traditional low, spread-leg base. The \"floating\" quality refers to tori's lighter, more mobile position compared to ground-based holds. It provides a hold-down while allowing tori to transition more readily to submissions or other positions.","phases":[],"steps":[{"title":"Establish position beside or straddling uke","text":"Position yourself alongside uke or in a kneeling position over their chest area."},{"title":"Apply upper body pressure to uke's chest","text":"Drive your chest or body weight downward onto uke's chest or upper torso. The weight pressure is your primary holding force."},{"title":"Control uke's arms","text":"Pin one or both of uke's arms to prevent them from pushing or escaping. Knee pressure on an arm is effective."},{"title":"Maintain the \"floating\" position","text":"Unlike spread-leg holds, Uki-Gatame allows more mobility. Maintain position without deeply planting your legs — stay mobile."},{"title":"Be ready to transition","text":"Uki-Gatame's mobility advantage means you can flow into armlocks or chokes as opportunities arise."}],"principles":["The \"floating\" quality means tori is mobile — ready to transition, not just holding. Use this mobility.","Upper body pressure is the primary control mechanism. Drive your weight onto uke's chest.","Arm control is important — pin uke's arms to limit their escape options.","Uki-Gatame scores as a valid osaekomi in IJF competition if maintained for the required time."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Being too \"floating\" — not applying enough pressure","fix":"The hold must still have sufficient body weight pressure. Too light and uke simply rolls out. Balance mobility with weight application."},{"mistake":"Not controlling uke's arms","fix":"Pin uke's near arm with your knee or body. Uncontrolled arms allow uke to push or create escape space."},{"mistake":"Being pulled into guard position","fix":"Be aware of uke's guard — do not allow uke to pull your hips into their closed guard while in Uki-Gatame."},{"mistake":"Not using the mobility advantage","fix":"Uki-Gatame's value is in its mobility. If you are simply holding statically, a lower, more stable hold is preferable."}],"when":"Uki-Gatame arises naturally when tori lands in a more upright position after a throw, particularly from throws where tori ends up kneeling over uke. It is used when quick follow-up transitions are planned — armlocks, chokes, or transitions to other pins are accessible from this position.","setups":[],"followUps":["seoi-otoshi","tai-otoshi","juji-gatame"],"counters":[],"related":["kesa-gatame","ura-gatame","tate-shiho-gatame"],"source":"https://judolearn.com/techniques/uki-gatame/"},{"id":"uki-goshi","name":"Uki-Goshi","kanji":"浮腰","translation":"Floating Hip","category":"Nage-Waza","subcategory":"Koshi-Waza","difficulty":1,"grade":"6th kyu","grades":["6th kyu","5th kyu"],"videos":[{"yt":"bPKwtB4lyOQ","title":"浮腰 / Uki-goshi","channel":"KODOKAN"},{"yt":"CFNtxusTvu0","title":"How to Do Uki Goshi in Judo and BJJ | Half-Hip Throw | Floating Hip Throw | 浮腰","channel":"Sampson Judo"},{"yt":"KCqjolfeEWo","title":"Uki-Goshi Tips | Riki Judo Dojo","channel":"Riki Judo Dojo"},{"yt":"hZbf7HlnLsM","title":"How Uki Goshi fits into Modern Judo","channel":"Shintaro Higashi"},{"yt":"MVhCdiVdlbM","title":"Uki-Goshi Compilation (Competition variation)","channel":"Ochiru"}],"overview":"Uki-Goshi is one of the original techniques of the Kodokan and is closely related to O-Goshi — the key difference being that tori wraps around uke's waist and uses the hip as a floating point of contact rather than a fixed fulcrum. Jigoro Kano described Uki-Goshi as the technique from which he developed judo, having used it to throw a larger opponent. It teaches the floating, light quality of koshi-waza.","phases":[{"label":"Kuzushi · Off-balance","text":"Pull uke forward and onto their right front corner with the sleeve grip while the right arm wraps around uke's waist or hip area. The forward pull with the sleeve arm is the primary kuzushi action — uke is drawn onto their toes and their weight shifts forward. The feeling should be of uke floating forward."},{"label":"Tsukuri · Entry","text":"Step your right foot in front of uke's right foot and pivot to place your back against uke's chest. Unlike O-Goshi, the hip in Uki-Goshi does not make hard contact — it floats just in front of uke's hip without pressing firmly in. The right arm wraps around uke's waist with a light, guiding hold."},{"label":"Kake · Execution","text":"Rotate your upper body forward, pulling uke over your right hip with the sleeve arm. The waist arm guides rather than lifts. The floating hip acts as a pivot point. Bend forward at the waist to project uke. Because the contact is light, the throw has a more fluid, circular quality compared to O-Goshi."}],"steps":[{"title":"Pull uke forward with the sleeve grip","text":"Pull uke's right sleeve arm upward and forward, creating forward kuzushi. Uke rises onto their toes."},{"title":"Step in and pivot","text":"Step your right foot in front of uke's right foot. Pivot on it to bring your left foot around so your back faces uke. Wrap your right arm around uke's waist."},{"title":"Position the hip lightly — floating contact","text":"Your right hip should be just in front of uke's hip — light contact, not a hard press. This is the \"floating\" quality of Uki-Goshi."},{"title":"Rotate and pull with both arms","text":"Rotate your upper body forward. Pull the sleeve arm forward and down. The waist arm guides uke's body around and forward."},{"title":"Project uke forward","text":"Bend forward at the waist as you rotate, projecting uke forward and around your hip to land on the mat."}],"principles":["The \"floating\" quality distinguishes Uki-Goshi from O-Goshi — the hip contact is light and transitional, not a hard wedging fulcrum.","The sleeve arm does most of the work — it pulls uke forward and guides their arc. The waist arm is a guide, not a lift.","Uki-Goshi teaches the circular, flowing quality of judo that Kano emphasized. The throw should feel effortless when timed correctly.","This technique is one of the original Kodokan 40 techniques and appears in Nage-no-Kata."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Making hard hip contact — turning it into O-Goshi","fix":"The hip should float lightly in front of uke. If you press hard, you are using O-Goshi mechanics. Keep the contact light and let the rotation do the work."},{"mistake":"Insufficient forward kuzushi","fix":"Without uke being pulled forward, the hip has nothing to float against. The sleeve grip must pull uke forward strongly before the entry."},{"mistake":"Stopping the rotation at the hip","fix":"Continue rotating and bending forward through the throw. Stopping rotation after hip contact leaves uke upright on your back."},{"mistake":"Waist arm pulling uke down instead of guiding","fix":"The waist arm guides uke's forward arc — it does not yank downward. Think of it as shaping uke's movement, not forcing it."}],"when":"Uki-Goshi is most effective against opponents with upright posture and moderate height. Its value as a teaching technique is high — it develops the intuition for hip throws and the feeling of making uke \"float\" before the throw. In competition it is often a foundation for transitioning to more powerful hip throws like Harai-Goshi.","setups":["ko-uchi-gari","de-ashi-barai"],"followUps":[],"counters":["tani-otoshi","ura-nage"],"related":["o-goshi","harai-goshi","tsurikomi-goshi","utsuri-goshi"],"source":"https://judolearn.com/techniques/uki-goshi/"},{"id":"uki-otoshi","name":"Uki-Otoshi","kanji":"浮落","translation":"Floating Drop","category":"Nage-Waza","subcategory":"Te-Waza","difficulty":2,"grade":"4th kyu","grades":["4th kyu","3rd kyu"],"videos":[{"yt":"6H5tmncOY4Q","title":"浮落 / Uki-otoshi","channel":"KODOKAN"},{"yt":"37EdmIjz6Co","title":"Uki-Otoshi Tips | Riki Judo Dojo","channel":"Riki Judo Dojo"},{"yt":"DXvEwIlKNco","title":"No Gi, No problem. Uki Otoshi is a super easy powerful throw.","channel":"Nick Yonezuka - Cranford JKC"},{"yt":"luTP_v5FvEU","title":"Uki-Otoshi Variations | Riki Judo Dojo","channel":"Riki Judo Dojo"},{"yt":"jGb1qGl5uGg","title":"101: Uki-otoshi - Ismayilov (AZE) v Kohara (JPN) -81kg","channel":"Fighting Films"}],"overview":"Uki-Otoshi is one of the oldest and most elegant techniques in judo, appearing as the first throw in the Nage-no-Kata. Tori drops to one knee while pulling uke sharply forward and downward, using pure arm and body mechanics — no hip or leg contact with uke — to float them off balance and project them to the mat. It exemplifies the principle of ju (yielding) and minimal effort.","phases":[{"label":"Kuzushi · Off-balance","text":"Pull uke sharply forward and upward onto their right front corner with both hands. The sleeve grip elevates and pulls uke's arm forward; the lapel grip pulls their chest toward you. Uke must be committed forward with their weight fully on their toes before tori drops."},{"label":"Tsukuri · Entry","text":"As uke steps forward with their right foot, step back and to the side with your left foot. Begin lowering yourself by bending your left knee. Your entire body retreats and drops in one smooth motion — you are not simply stepping back, you are guiding uke's forward momentum into the throw."},{"label":"Kake · Execution","text":"Drop to your left knee as you pull uke's weight forward and down. Your hands guide uke's upper body in a large arc — forward, down, and to the right. There is no direct body contact with uke's torso. Uke floats over the void created by your dropping body and lands on the mat in front of you."}],"steps":[{"title":"Establish forward kuzushi","text":"Pull uke's sleeve arm upward and forward. The lapel hand pulls uke's chest toward you. Uke's weight shifts to their right front corner and onto their toes."},{"title":"Step back and begin the drop","text":"As uke steps forward, retreat with your left foot diagonally to your left rear. Begin lowering your body by bending the left knee."},{"title":"Drop to the left knee","text":"Lower your left knee to the mat in a controlled drop. This sudden lowering creates a void beneath uke's forward-falling body."},{"title":"Guide uke in a forward arc","text":"Pull both hands in a large circular arc — forward, downward, and to the right front. Your arm pull guides uke's upper body over and down."},{"title":"Complete the projection","text":"Uke's forward momentum carries them over the void and onto the mat. Maintain grip control and follow through the full arc to drive uke completely to the ground."}],"principles":["Timing is everything — the drop must coincide exactly with uke's committed forward step. Too early and uke simply stops; too late and the moment is lost.","There is no hip or leg contact with uke. The throw is pure arm mechanics and body drop, making timing and kuzushi the only factors.","The arc of the arm pull is large and circular, not a simple downward yank. Think of drawing a large circle from uke's chest to the ground.","Uki-Otoshi works best when uke is moving forward with momentum — it uses that momentum rather than generating power."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Dropping before uke is fully committed forward","fix":"Wait until uke has fully stepped forward and their weight is on their front foot. Dropping early lets uke simply stop or step around you."},{"mistake":"Pulling straight down instead of in an arc","fix":"The pull must guide uke forward first, then down and to the right. A straight downward pull has no projection force."},{"mistake":"Dropping too close to uke (no space created)","fix":"Step back sufficiently before dropping. You must create a void for uke to fall into. Staying close negates the floating effect."},{"mistake":"Using muscle to force the throw","fix":"Uki-Otoshi relies entirely on uke's forward momentum. If uke is not moving forward, attempting to muscle the throw will fail. Create movement first."}],"when":"Uki-Otoshi is most effective when uke is advancing aggressively or has been lured into a strong forward push. It is a classical Nage-no-Kata technique and teaches the principle of using uke's energy. In randori or shiai it is used as a surprise drop when uke charges in, or as a counter to aggressive forward pressure.","setups":["de-ashi-barai","o-uchi-gari"],"followUps":[],"counters":["tani-otoshi"],"related":["seoi-otoshi","sumi-otoshi","sukui-nage"],"source":"https://judolearn.com/techniques/uki-otoshi/"},{"id":"uki-waza","name":"Uki-Waza","kanji":"浮技","translation":"Floating Throw","category":"Nage-Waza","subcategory":"Yoko-Sutemi-Waza","difficulty":2,"grade":"3rd kyu","grades":["3rd kyu","2nd kyu"],"videos":[{"yt":"weVOpJ63gII","title":"浮技 / Uki-waza","channel":"KODOKAN"},{"yt":"wKuGUtAm7V8","title":"Ukiwaza by Nobuyoshi Higashi","channel":"Shintaro Higashi"},{"yt":"YY7Twc3350c","title":"Uki-Waza Tips | Riki Judo Dojo","channel":"Riki Judo Dojo"},{"yt":"9j0Rc_rAO48","title":"Uki-Waza variation Compilation","channel":"Ochiru"}],"overview":"Uki-Waza is a side sacrifice throw where tori falls to the side while pulling uke's upper body in the same direction, creating a \"floating\" throw effect. It is one of the five classical side sacrifice throws of judo and appears in the Nage-no-Kata. The technique is characterized by a smooth, floating lateral pull that lifts uke off their base and dumps them to the mat.","phases":[{"label":"Kuzushi · Off-balance","text":"Pull uke strongly to the side — in the direction tori will fall. Both sleeve and lapel grips pull uke's upper body to the side, breaking balance to uke's front corner. Uke's weight floats to one side and they begin to lose their stable base."},{"label":"Tsukuri · Entry","text":"Step forward with your right foot past uke's right foot — placing it at uke's side. Begin falling to your left as you step, pivoting your body to face uke from the side. Your body leans into the falling direction as you draw uke with the grips."},{"label":"Kake · Execution","text":"Fall fully to the side, pulling uke's upper body with both grips as you fall. Your left side falls to the mat while your grips bring uke's upper body floating over and to the mat beside you. The \"floating\" quality comes from the smooth, continuous pull that lifts uke over rather than forcing them down."}],"steps":[{"title":"Pull uke to the side with both grips","text":"Pull the sleeve and lapel grips to the side — toward your falling direction. Uke floats off their base."},{"title":"Step forward and to the side of uke","text":"Step your right foot forward past uke's right foot, positioning it to uke's side. Begin leaning into the fall."},{"title":"Fall to your left side","text":"Commit to the side fall, bringing your left side fully to the ground."},{"title":"Pull uke over and down","text":"As you fall, both grips pull uke's upper body in the direction of the fall — forward and to the side — floating them over."},{"title":"Land and allow uke to complete the arc","text":"Tori lands on the side while uke, pulled forward and sideways, continues in their arc to land on the mat."}],"principles":["The \"floating\" quality is created by the smooth, continuous pull of the grips as tori falls. Think of drawing uke through the air horizontally.","The step placement is critical — your stepping foot gives the directional base for the fall.","Total commitment to the side fall is required. Stopping partway through leaves tori in a vulnerable, half-fallen position.","Uki-Waza is particularly valuable as a classical technique demonstrating the sutemi-waza principle."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Stopping the fall partway","fix":"Commit fully to the side fall. Any hesitation leaves you stuck at an angle without completing the throw."},{"mistake":"Pulling uke down instead of floating them sideways","fix":"The pull should have a lateral, floating quality — pulling uke out and over, not directly downward."},{"mistake":"Stepping foot placed behind uke instead of to the side","fix":"The step should place your foot beside or slightly ahead of uke's foot, not behind. Behind-the-foot placement changes the fall angle."},{"mistake":"Losing grip during the fall","fix":"Maintain both grips throughout. The grips are the connection that pulls uke over."}],"when":"Uki-Waza is effective when uke is advancing or has forward momentum — the floating pull redirects their energy sideways. It is most commonly practiced in kata and used in randori as a flowing sacrifice when uke's lateral balance is broken.","setups":["de-ashi-barai"],"followUps":["yoko-shiho-gatame"],"counters":[],"related":["yoko-wakare","yoko-gake","yoko-guruma"],"source":"https://judolearn.com/techniques/uki-waza/"},{"id":"ura-gatame","name":"Ura-Gatame","kanji":"裏固","translation":"Rear Hold","category":"Katame-Waza","subcategory":"Osaekomi-Waza","difficulty":2,"grade":"3rd kyu","grades":["3rd kyu","2nd kyu"],"videos":[{"yt":"eeAHZB0v3XY","title":"裏固 /  Ura-gatame","channel":"KODOKAN"},{"yt":"U6MgZoeqy5g","title":"URA GATAME PRACTICAL & EFFECTIVE","channel":"JBBA Judo Education Channel"},{"yt":"c-l89_CB6sA","title":"URA GATAME BASIC SKILLS","channel":"Judo Advantage"},{"yt":"0aY2Tmchzs8","title":"裏固の逃れ方 /  Ura-gatame Escapes","channel":"KODOKAN"}],"overview":"Ura-Gatame is a rear hold-down where tori controls uke from behind — from the opposite side of uke's chest. Tori is on their back or side behind uke, with uke's back against tori's chest, applying a hold that controls uke's arms and upper body. It is a less conventional hold arising from specific throw and transition scenarios where tori ends up behind uke.","phases":[],"steps":[{"title":"End up behind uke after a throw or transition","text":"Ura-Gatame typically arises when tori ends up on uke's back side after a throw — particularly from rear throws or rolling transitions."},{"title":"Press uke's back against your chest","text":"Uke's back is against tori's front. Use your body weight to keep uke pressed to the mat."},{"title":"Control uke's arms from behind","text":"Reach around uke to control their arms — trapping them or gripping their sleeves to prevent escape."},{"title":"Wrap and secure uke's body","text":"Wrap your arms around uke from behind to create the holding force. Body weight from behind presses uke to the mat."},{"title":"Maintain position and prevent rolling","text":"Keep uke from rolling. Your body behind them prevents one direction; your arm and leg positioning prevent the other."}],"principles":["Ura-Gatame uses the rear position — tori's chest against uke's back — to apply holding force.","Arm control from behind is essential. Uke has full range of motion for both arms; secure them.","The hold requires body weight applied from behind and to the side — not just arms wrapping around uke.","Ura-Gatame often leads naturally to rear choke applications (Hadaka-Jime, Okuri-Eri-Jime)."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Insufficient body weight — relying only on arms","fix":"Apply body weight from behind to press uke to the mat. Arms alone cannot hold an active uke."},{"mistake":"Not securing uke's arms","fix":"With uke's back exposed, their arms have full freedom. Control them to prevent pushes and escapes."},{"mistake":"Allowing uke to roll through to face you","fix":"Use leg positioning to block uke's forward roll. Hook their leg or block with your knee."},{"mistake":"Not transitioning to choke when available","fix":"Ura-Gatame presents clear access to rear chokes. If a choke setup is available, consider transitioning."}],"when":"Ura-Gatame arises from throws that send tori to uke's rear — certain sacrifice throws, transition scrambles, or when uke rolls and tori maintains contact. It is a transitional hold often used briefly before transitioning to a more dominant position or a rear choke.","setups":[],"followUps":["tani-otoshi","hadaka-jime","okuri-eri-jime"],"counters":[],"related":["uki-gatame","kesa-gatame","hadaka-jime"],"source":"https://judolearn.com/techniques/ura-gatame/"},{"id":"ura-nage","name":"Ura-Nage","kanji":"裏投","translation":"Rear Throw","category":"Nage-Waza","subcategory":"Ma-Sutemi-Waza","difficulty":3,"grade":"2nd kyu","grades":["2nd kyu","1st kyu","1st dan"],"videos":[{"yt":"Fgi9b8DJ5sQ","title":"裏投 / Ura-nage","channel":"KODOKAN"},{"yt":"8l5RdJR6TLk","title":"Judo's Biggest Throw: Ura-Nage","channel":"Shintaro Higashi"},{"yt":"w39ERgAJtjE","title":"Ura nage Judo from Georgian grip","channel":"Shintaro Higashi"},{"yt":"pr5bdntb2y0","title":"Ura nage compilation","channel":"Ochiru"}],"overview":"Ura-Nage is a powerful rear sacrifice throw in which tori wraps both arms around uke's body from behind or from the side, falls backward, and arches their back to project uke over and backward to the mat. It is one of the most spectacular throws in judo, capable of landing uke directly on their back for ippon. Ura-Nage is primarily used as a counter technique — catching uke mid-throw — and requires the courage to commit to a full backward arch with uke's weight on top of you.","phases":[{"label":"Kuzushi · Off-balance","text":"Ura-Nage is typically applied when uke has already committed to a forward throw attempt. Uke's own forward commitment and body positioning create the kuzushi condition — as uke enters for their throw (often a hip throw or shoulder throw), they load their weight onto tori, giving tori the leverage to apply Ura-Nage."},{"label":"Tsukuri · Entry","text":"As uke enters for their throw (for example, a hip throw entry), step to the side and slightly behind uke, wrapping both arms around uke's waist from behind or from the side. Your chest presses against uke's back or side. Both arms lock around uke's midsection firmly. Your feet should be close to uke's feet."},{"label":"Kake · Execution","text":"With uke locked in your arms, fall backward and simultaneously arch your back powerfully. The arch of the back throws uke upward and over, projecting them in an arc over your body to land on the mat behind your head. The combination of the backward fall and the arching motion provides the rotational power."}],"steps":[{"title":"Recognise uke's throw entry","text":"Identify the moment uke commits to a throw entry — particularly when they turn their back to you for a hip or shoulder throw."},{"title":"Step behind and wrap both arms","text":"Step to uke's side and slightly behind, wrapping both arms firmly around uke's waist. Your chest presses against uke's back or side."},{"title":"Lock the grip","text":"Clasp your hands together firmly at uke's waist or lower chest. The grip must be secure before initiating the fall."},{"title":"Fall backward with commitment","text":"Commit to a full backward fall, keeping uke wrapped tightly in your arms. Your back arches as you fall."},{"title":"Arch the back and project uke","text":"As you hit the mat, arch your back powerfully, driving your hips and chest upward. Uke's body is thrown upward and arcs over your body to land behind you."}],"principles":["Commitment is non-negotiable — a partial backward fall with hesitation results in you landing under uke with no throw.","The back arch during kake is the throwing power source — without a full arch, uke is only dropped, not thrown.","Both arms must lock firmly around uke before the fall; if uke slips free during the fall, the throw is lost and you are on your back.","Timing with uke's throw entry is critical — the window when uke is mid-entry and their weight is committed is the only effective entry point."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Half-committing to the backward fall","fix":"Ura-Nage requires full commitment. A hesitant half-fall places tori on their back with uke on top — a terrible position. Either commit fully or don't attempt it."},{"mistake":"Gripping only with the arms rather than locking the hands","fix":"Clasping hands together provides a rigid lock that holds uke even during the arch. Loose arm pressure allows uke to escape."},{"mistake":"Attempting the throw when uke is not committed to an entry","fix":"Ura-Nage applied against a balanced, defensive uke is exhausting and rarely successful. Wait for uke to commit to an attack."},{"mistake":"Not arching the back during kake","fix":"Without the back arch, uke is only dropped onto the mat in a forward direction. The arch is what sends uke over in the powerful arcing trajectory."}],"when":"Ura-Nage is almost exclusively a counter technique in competition. It is most effective when countering hip throws (koshi-waza) and shoulder throws (seoi-nage), where uke turns their back directly into your arms. It can also counter sacrifice throws when uke ducks under. It is a high-reward, high-risk technique — when it scores, it typically scores ippon.","setups":[],"followUps":["kesa-gatame","juji-gatame"],"counters":[],"related":["sumi-gaeshi","tomoe-nage","tani-otoshi"],"source":"https://judolearn.com/techniques/ura-nage/"},{"id":"utsuri-goshi","name":"Utsuri-Goshi","kanji":"移腰","translation":"Hip Shift","category":"Nage-Waza","subcategory":"Koshi-Waza","difficulty":3,"grade":"2nd kyu","grades":["2nd kyu","1st kyu","1st dan"],"videos":[{"yt":"4pQd_bEnlf0","title":"移腰 / Utsuri-goshi","channel":"KODOKAN"},{"yt":"pyzYfHXivs4","title":"Utsuri-Goshi: The Ultimate Counter against All Turn Throws","channel":"Shintaro Higashi"},{"yt":"dVf7pvdXQXg","title":"Utsuri Goshi for smaller people","channel":"Shintaro Higashi"},{"yt":"35UD3FdXobQ","title":"Utsuri-Goshi Compilation","channel":"Ochiru"}],"overview":"Utsuri-Goshi is an advanced counter-throw — specifically designed to counter an opponent's hip throw attempt. When uke enters for a hip throw (such as O-Goshi or Harai-Goshi), tori lifts uke onto their own hip, shifting (utsuri) their position, and then projects uke with a throw from the opposite side. It is one of judo's most sophisticated counter-techniques requiring precise timing and strength.","phases":[{"label":"Kuzushi · Off-balance","text":"Kuzushi is applied to uke after countering their attack. When uke enters for a hip throw, tori wraps both arms around uke's waist from behind and lifts upward — shifting uke's weight off the ground entirely. With uke lifted, their balance is completely broken. Tori then shifts (utsuri) and projects uke to the opposite side."},{"label":"Tsukuri · Entry","text":"As uke enters for their hip throw and places their back against tori's chest, tori wraps both arms firmly around uke's waist from behind. The grip must be deep and secure — hands interlocked or gripping the belt. Tori bends slightly at the knees to generate lifting power."},{"label":"Kake · Execution","text":"Lift uke off the ground by straightening the knees and driving the hips forward. Once uke is airborne, shift your hip position and project uke to the mat on the opposite side from their original attack direction. The throw can go forward (dropping uke in front of tori) or to the side depending on tori's position."}],"steps":[{"title":"Recognize uke's hip throw entry","text":"As uke steps in and pivots for a hip throw, read the entry and prepare to counter. Uke's back will be against your chest."},{"title":"Wrap both arms around uke's waist","text":"Secure both arms around uke's waist from behind — wrap tightly with hands interlocked or gripping the belt at the front."},{"title":"Drop your hips and load your legs","text":"Bend your knees and position your hips below uke's center of gravity. Load your legs for the lift."},{"title":"Lift uke off the ground","text":"Extend your legs and hips explosively, lifting uke completely off the mat. The lift must be vertical enough to take uke's feet off the ground."},{"title":"Shift and project uke","text":"Shift your hip or body position and project uke to the mat — typically forward (in front of you) or to the side opposite their attack."}],"principles":["Timing is critical — the wrap must be established as uke enters, not after they have fully loaded onto the hip.","The lift requires leg drive, not just arm pulling. Use your legs to stand up through uke, generating vertical force.","Utsuri-Goshi requires strength and good technique — it is one of the techniques that emphasizes physical attributes at the advanced level.","The \"shift\" (utsuri) element means tori redirects uke's momentum to a new direction. Simply lifting and dropping is not the full technique."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Wrapping after uke has already loaded onto their hip","fix":"The wrap must be established during uke's entry, before they load. Once uke is fully on the hip and throwing, the window to counter is closed."},{"mistake":"Lifting with the arms alone — no leg drive","fix":"The lift comes from straightening the knees and driving the hips forward. Arm-only lifts lack the power to get uke fully airborne."},{"mistake":"No hip shift — simply dropping uke straight down","fix":"After lifting, shift your hip position to create a directional projection. Dropping straight down without a shift results in uke landing on or beside you."},{"mistake":"Grip too high — around the chest instead of the waist","fix":"The grip should wrap around uke's waist or belt. A chest-level grip is too high and reduces the lifting leverage."}],"when":"Utsuri-Goshi is specifically used as a counter to hip throws. Develop this technique as a response to training partners who favor O-Goshi, Harai-Goshi, or Tsurikomi-Goshi. In competition it is a high-risk, high-reward counter that requires the physical attributes and timing to execute. It is rarely used as a primary attack.","setups":[],"followUps":["harai-goshi","o-goshi"],"counters":[],"related":["o-goshi","uki-goshi","tsurikomi-goshi","hane-goshi"],"source":"https://judolearn.com/techniques/utsuri-goshi/"},{"id":"waki-gatame","name":"Waki-Gatame","kanji":"腋固","translation":"Armpit Hold","category":"Katame-Waza","subcategory":"Kansetsu-Waza","difficulty":2,"grade":"3rd kyu","grades":["3rd kyu","2nd kyu"],"videos":[{"yt":"8F5p1zuJRG0","title":"腕挫腋固 /  Ude-hishigi-waki-gatame","channel":"KODOKAN"},{"yt":"bskOxop0c90","title":"Waki Gatame - when and how to use it.","channel":"Robert Silas (Aiki_and_Jiu)"},{"yt":"o-fhukJfI8s","title":"Waki Gatame","channel":"Shintaro Higashi"},{"yt":"ZI6Xon56ntU","title":"Waki-Gatame","channel":"JudoCrazy"}],"overview":"Waki-Gatame is an armlock that hyperextends uke's elbow by trapping uke's straightened arm under tori's armpit. Tori clamps the armpit over uke's upper arm and drives downward on the locked elbow. It can be applied both standing and on the ground, and is one of the few kansetsu-waza that has application in standing judo as well as ne-waza.","phases":[{"label":"Kuzushi · Off-balance","text":"In standing application: disrupts uke's balance through the arm control — pulling uke's arm and tipping them forward. In ne-waza: not a primary concern."},{"label":"Tsukuri · Entry","text":"Trap uke's straight arm by clamping it under tori's armpit with the elbow pressed into the joint. The arm must be straightened for the lock to work."},{"label":"Kake · Execution","text":"Drive the clamped armpit downward against uke's elbow joint while the trapped forearm is pushed upward — creating hyperextension at the elbow."}],"steps":[{"title":"Control uke's arm and straighten it","text":"Grab uke's wrist or sleeve and pull the arm straight. A bent elbow cannot be locked — the arm must be fully extended."},{"title":"Clamp the armpit over uke's upper arm","text":"Drive your armpit down over uke's straightened arm just above the elbow. Your upper arm and body clamp uke's arm from above."},{"title":"Secure uke's wrist or hand","text":"Keep uke's hand controlled — pull it up toward your shoulder or chest to straighten the arm and prevent uke from bending it."},{"title":"Drive downward on the elbow","text":"Apply downward pressure through the armpit onto uke's elbow joint. Combined with the wrist pull, this hyperextends the elbow."},{"title":"Maintain position and apply consistent pressure","text":"Hold the lock firmly. In standing application, apply enough pressure to force a submission or take uke down. In ne-waza, increase pressure until submission."}],"principles":["The arm must be fully straightened. Any bend in the elbow means no hyperextension pressure exists.","The armpit clamp must be over the upper arm just proximal to the elbow — too far up the arm is ineffective.","Waki-Gatame has standing application — it can be applied during grip fighting when tori controls uke's arm and has an opportunity to clamp.","Control of the wrist and the armpit pressure must work together — losing wrist control allows uke to bend the elbow and escape."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Elbow not fully extended before clamping","fix":"Pull the arm straight before clamping. If the elbow is bent, re-straighten it by pulling the wrist or sleeve."},{"mistake":"Armpit positioned on the shoulder instead of upper arm","fix":"The clamp should be on the upper arm, just above the elbow. Clamping on the shoulder has no joint-lock leverage."},{"mistake":"Not controlling the wrist","fix":"Secure the wrist at all times. A free wrist allows uke to rotate their arm or bend the elbow to escape."},{"mistake":"In standing application — leaning too far forward","fix":"Maintain your own balance while applying the lock. Leaning excessively forward makes you vulnerable to being thrown."}],"when":"Waki-Gatame is applied opportunistically — when uke's arm is straight and accessible for the armpit clamp. In standing, it arises during grip fighting when tori can trap the arm. In ne-waza, it appears in scrambles and transitions when the elbow presents. It is one of the few kansetsu-waza with meaningful standing application. Note: in IJF competition, falling directly to the mat while applying a standing Waki-Gatame results in hansoku-make (disqualification).","setups":[],"followUps":["juji-gatame","ude-garami"],"counters":[],"related":["ude-gatame","juji-gatame","ude-garami","hiza-gatame"],"source":"https://judolearn.com/techniques/waki-gatame/"},{"id":"yama-arashi","name":"Yama-Arashi","kanji":"山嵐","translation":"Mountain Storm","category":"Nage-Waza","subcategory":"Te-Waza","difficulty":3,"grade":"2nd kyu","grades":["2nd kyu","1st kyu","1st dan"],"videos":[{"yt":"MGlyKmSuzdc","title":"山嵐 / Yama-arashi","channel":"KODOKAN"},{"yt":"AYKlETQ1Pn4","title":"This Is Technically Its Own Judo Throw - Yama Arashi","channel":"Shintaro Higashi"},{"yt":"DyCYjzJD7_c","title":"Yama-Arashi Tips | Riki Judo Dojo","channel":"Riki Judo Dojo"},{"yt":"qfGzJKSYMI8","title":"On the Mat with Sensei Jose: Yama Arashi, Forwards and Backwards","channel":"Sensei Jose Eudes Monteiro"}],"overview":"Yama-Arashi is a powerful and technically demanding te-waza associated with the legendary judoka Saigo Shiro. Tori applies a high sleeve grip and a deep collar or sleeve grip, then executes a sweeping rotational throw that combines elements of Seoi-Nage and a large sweeping arm action — projecting uke with tremendous force in a storm-like arc. It is classified as te-waza but has characteristics of both shoulder throws and sweeping throws.","phases":[{"label":"Kuzushi · Off-balance","text":"Kuzushi is created by a high, pulling action on uke's right sleeve — lifting uke's arm high and pulling them forward and to their right front corner. The lapel or second grip is used to drive uke's upper body into rotation. The high sleeve creates an off-balance that uke cannot easily counter by lowering their hips."},{"label":"Tsukuri · Entry","text":"Step deeply in with the right foot, rotating to face the same direction as uke. The entry is similar to Seoi-Nage, but the left arm — gripping high on the sleeve or lapel — maintains an elevated, sweeping position rather than wrapping under the armpit. The body entry is tight against uke's chest."},{"label":"Kake · Execution","text":"Execute a powerful rotational movement — tori's body rotates forward while the high sleeve arm sweeps uke's right arm in a large downward arc. The combination of body rotation and the sweeping arm action creates a storm-like force that projects uke forward and over. The throw can be completed standing or with a partial drop."}],"steps":[{"title":"Establish a high sleeve grip","text":"Grip uke's right sleeve as high as possible — near the elbow or even the upper arm. This elevated grip is the key mechanical feature of Yama-Arashi."},{"title":"Create kuzushi with upward pull","text":"Pull uke's right arm upward and toward their right front corner. The high grip amplifies the destabilizing effect. Uke's right shoulder rises."},{"title":"Step in and turn","text":"Step the right foot in front of uke, between their feet, and pivot to face the same direction. Your back presses to uke's chest as in Seoi-Nage entry."},{"title":"Initiate the sweeping arm action","text":"With the high sleeve grip, begin a large downward sweeping arc — bringing uke's arm forward, down, and across. This creates the rotational storm force."},{"title":"Complete the rotational throw","text":"Your body rotates fully forward as the sweeping arm drives uke's upper body over. Uke is projected in a large arc to the mat."}],"principles":["The high sleeve grip is the defining feature — without it, the throw becomes a standard Seoi-Nage. The elevation of the grip creates the unique sweeping arc.","The sweeping arm action must be large and continuous — like a storm sweeping through. Short, jerky pulls do not generate the necessary rotational force.","Body rotation and the arm sweep must be synchronized. Arm-only or body-only effort produces a weak, easily-countered throw.","Yama-Arashi requires exceptional timing and sensitivity — it is a high-level throw that should not be attempted without solid foundation in shoulder throw mechanics."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Gripping at the wrist instead of high on the sleeve","fix":"The grip must be at the elbow or higher. A wrist grip lacks the leverage needed for the sweeping arc and limits the technique to a standard Seoi entry."},{"mistake":"Insufficient body rotation","fix":"The full body must rotate forward through the throw. Half-rotations leave uke on top of tori rather than projected forward."},{"mistake":"Treating Yama-Arashi as simply a strong Seoi-Nage","fix":"The sweeping arm arc is distinctive. Focus on the large circular motion of the sleeve arm rather than just a forward shoulder entry."},{"mistake":"Applying without sufficient kuzushi","fix":"Yama-Arashi requires uke's arm and shoulder to be elevated before entry. Attempting without the high kuzushi means uke can resist the entry."}],"when":"Yama-Arashi is most effective against opponents with a stiff, upright posture where the high sleeve grip can be established. It is a specialized technique for practitioners who have mastered Seoi-Nage and shoulder throw mechanics and want to develop a high-power variation. In competition it is used as a surprise element against opponents who have defended standard Seoi-Nage entries.","setups":["ko-uchi-gari","o-uchi-gari"],"followUps":[],"counters":["tani-otoshi","ura-nage"],"related":["seoi-otoshi","uki-otoshi","o-guruma"],"source":"https://judolearn.com/techniques/yama-arashi/"},{"id":"yoko-gake","name":"Yoko-Gake","kanji":"横掛","translation":"Side Hook","category":"Nage-Waza","subcategory":"Yoko-Sutemi-Waza","difficulty":2,"grade":"3rd kyu","grades":["3rd kyu","2nd kyu","1st kyu"],"videos":[{"yt":"tP1Sj1uDfSo","title":"横掛 / Yoko-gake","channel":"KODOKAN"},{"yt":"Ti8tudRBPSQ","title":"Neil Adams spots a trend on the WJT: Yoko Gake","channel":"Neil Adams Judo"},{"yt":"vn1pR7CoCNg","title":"How To Yoko Gake || Competition Variation","channel":"The Judo Way of Life"},{"yt":"H9KNVBQ_n04","title":"Nage-no-kata: Yoko-Gake Tips | Riki Judo Dojo","channel":"Riki Judo Dojo"},{"yt":"PhQ2J7YMpXw","title":"judo Yoko-Gake Compilation","channel":"Ochiru"}],"overview":"Yoko-Gake is a side sacrifice throw in which tori steps across uke's front, hooks their foot against uke's near ankle, and falls to the side to sweep uke's foot and drop them laterally. Unlike rear sacrifice throws where tori falls backward, in Yoko-Gake tori falls directly to the side. The foot hook and side fall combine to take both tori and uke down together, with uke landing on the mat beside or slightly ahead of tori. It is a versatile technique that can be applied from various angles.","phases":[{"label":"Kuzushi · Off-balance","text":"Pull uke's sleeve arm sharply to your right and slightly downward, breaking their balance to their right front corner. The lapel arm lifts and draws uke's upper body forward and to your right. Uke's weight shifts onto their right foot in preparation for their right foot to be hooked."},{"label":"Tsukuri · Entry","text":"Step your left foot to the outside of uke's right foot, crossing in front of uke. As you step, begin your sideward falling motion to the right. Hook your right foot against the outside of uke's right ankle — the hooking action places your right foot on the mat and sweeps uke's ankle inward."},{"label":"Kake · Execution","text":"Fall to your right side while simultaneously hooking uke's right ankle with your right foot in a lateral sweeping motion. Pull the sleeve arm sharply downward and to your right as you fall. The side fall with the ankle hook takes uke's right foot out from under them and uke falls laterally to land on the mat beside you."}],"steps":[{"title":"Break balance to uke's right front","text":"Pull the sleeve arm forward and to the right while the lapel arm draws uke's upper body toward you. Shift uke's weight onto their right foot."},{"title":"Step across uke's front","text":"Step your left foot to the outside of and in front of uke's right foot. You are now crossing in front of uke's body."},{"title":"Hook the ankle","text":"Hook your right foot against the outside of uke's right ankle. The hook is low and lateral — your foot sweeps uke's ankle inward."},{"title":"Initiate the side fall","text":"Commit to falling directly to your right side — not backward, not forward, directly sideward. Keep your body connected to uke."},{"title":"Complete with sleeve pull","text":"As you fall, pull the sleeve arm sharply downward and to your right, directing uke's fall laterally. Uke lands on the mat beside you."}],"principles":["The fall must be directly to the side — a backward lean converts this to a poor sacrifice throw that puts tori in danger.","The ankle hook must be low and contact the outside of uke's ankle, not the shin or calf.","The sleeve arm pull is essential — without it, uke simply steps over your falling body.","The hook must be applied as uke's weight is shifting onto the right foot but before it is fully loaded — a fully planted, heavily weighted foot resists the lateral sweep."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Falling backward instead of directly to the side","fix":"Yoko-Gake is a side sacrifice, not rear. Falling backward removes the ankle hook from uke's foot path and destroys the direction of the throw."},{"mistake":"Hooking too high on the shin instead of the ankle","fix":"The hook should contact uke's ankle. A shin hook has less leverage and is easier for uke to step over."},{"mistake":"Losing grip contact during the fall","fix":"Maintain firm grips on sleeve and lapel throughout the fall and throw. Releasing during the sacrifice leaves uke free to step over you."},{"mistake":"Attempting on an opponent moving toward you","fix":"Yoko-Gake works best when uke is stationary or moving to the side. Against an opponent advancing directly toward you, the step-across is difficult to time."}],"when":"Yoko-Gake is effective against a stationary or laterally moving opponent. It works well from a circular movement pattern where uke's weight is momentarily on one foot. It is often used as a counter when uke resists a lateral pull, and as a complement to harai-goshi or de-ashi-barai combinations where lateral footwork is established.","setups":["harai-goshi","o-uchi-gari"],"followUps":[],"counters":["tai-otoshi"],"related":["tani-otoshi","sumi-gaeshi","de-ashi-barai"],"source":"https://judolearn.com/techniques/yoko-gake/"},{"id":"yoko-guruma","name":"Yoko-Guruma","kanji":"横車","translation":"Side Wheel","category":"Nage-Waza","subcategory":"Yoko-Sutemi-Waza","difficulty":3,"grade":"2nd kyu","grades":["2nd kyu","1st kyu","1st dan"],"videos":[{"yt":"MehP6I5cY2c","title":"横車 / Yoko-guruma","channel":"KODOKAN"},{"yt":"h3cf9lXzQtU","title":"Yoko-Guruma Tips | Riki Judo Dojo","channel":"Riki Judo Dojo"},{"yt":"TczJkFiuxaw","title":"3 Yoko Guruma Variations in 1 Minute","channel":"Shintaro Higashi"},{"yt":"dyTge_EvjaU","title":"yoko-guruma compilation","channel":"Ochiru"}],"overview":"Yoko-Guruma is an advanced side sacrifice throw where tori dives under uke's arm or across uke's body, falling to the side and creating a wheel-like rotational throw. Tori's body acts as the axle around which uke is wheeled over in a large arc. It is one of the more spectacular throws in judo and requires significant practice to time correctly.","phases":[{"label":"Kuzushi · Off-balance","text":"Kuzushi for Yoko-Guruma involves driving uke upward and to the side, opening space for tori to enter below uke's center of gravity. A high pulling action on the sleeve drives uke's arm upward while tori dives under it. The upward pull and the diving entry create the kuzushi by disturbing uke's upright equilibrium."},{"label":"Tsukuri · Entry","text":"Tori dives or steps rapidly under uke's elevated arm, sliding their body across and below uke. One arm may wrap around uke's waist or thigh as tori slides through. The entry is low and across uke's body, setting up the side sacrifice position with uke above tori."},{"label":"Kake · Execution","text":"Fall to the side while pulling uke over with the grips and body contact. Tori's body rolling to the side creates the wheel motion that carries uke over in a large arc. Uke is wheeled sideways over tori's body and lands on the mat."}],"steps":[{"title":"Elevate uke's arm with sleeve grip","text":"Pull the sleeve arm high, elevating uke's arm to create space below it for tori's entry."},{"title":"Drive under uke's arm","text":"Rapidly dive under or across uke's elevated arm, sliding your body below uke's center of gravity."},{"title":"Wrap uke's waist or thigh","text":"As you slide under, wrap one arm around uke's waist or thigh to establish the contact point for the wheel."},{"title":"Fall to the side — the sacrifice","text":"Fall to the side decisively. Your body becomes the axle of the wheel."},{"title":"Wheel uke over","text":"Pull uke with grips and body as you fall, wheeling them over in a large arc. Uke lands on the mat beside or beyond tori."}],"principles":["The entry under uke's arm must be fast and committed. Hesitating in the entry exposes tori to counters.","Body wrap at the waist or thigh creates the wheel axle. Without this contact point, tori falls away without the wheel mechanics.","The \"wheel\" effect comes from tori's side fall creating rotational force on uke. The fall direction must be perpendicular to uke's standing axis.","Yoko-Guruma is an advanced technique best drilled from specific setups or counters rather than as an arbitrary attack."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Not diving deep enough — staying above uke's hip","fix":"The entry must get tori's body below uke's center of gravity. Staying at hip height or above gives uke leverage to counter."},{"mistake":"Falling backward instead of directly sideways","fix":"The sacrifice should be directly to the side to create the wheel motion. Falling backward creates a different (less effective) vector."},{"mistake":"No body wrap — losing contact with uke","fix":"Establish a wrap on uke's waist or thigh during the entry. Without contact, tori's fall is disconnected from uke and no throw occurs."},{"mistake":"Entry without elevated sleeve grip opening","fix":"Create the space under uke's arm first by elevating the sleeve. Diving into a closed arm position leaves no room for the entry."}],"when":"Yoko-Guruma is used as an advanced counter when uke is pushing or pressing forward, or as a high-level attack from a grip fight. It is particularly striking when used as a counter to uke's forward throw attempts. The diving entry under the arm is a hallmark of advanced judo.","setups":[],"followUps":["harai-goshi","kesa-gatame"],"counters":[],"related":["yoko-wakare","yoko-gake","uki-waza","daki-wakare"],"source":"https://judolearn.com/techniques/yoko-guruma/"},{"id":"yoko-otoshi","name":"Yoko-Otoshi","kanji":"横落","translation":"Side Drop","category":"Nage-Waza","subcategory":"Yoko-Sutemi-Waza","difficulty":2,"grade":"4th kyu","grades":["4th kyu","3rd kyu"],"videos":[{"yt":"MnNG67pF_a0","title":"横落 / Yoko-otoshi","channel":"KODOKAN"},{"yt":"BJoVSHpdzWU","title":"Yoko-Otoshi Tips | Riki Judo Dojo","channel":"Riki Judo Dojo"},{"yt":"yI35wlhAIik","title":"How and why Yoko-Otoshi works so well.","channel":"Nick Yonezuka - Cranford JKC"},{"yt":"l6bFPdNjx8E","title":"Yoko-Otoshi Variations | Riki Judo Dojo","channel":"Riki Judo Dojo"},{"yt":"dHwExvZDKJo","title":"Yoko Otoshi | Side Sacrifice Throwing While Sliding","channel":"Shintaro Nakano"}],"overview":"Yoko-Otoshi is a side sacrifice throw in which tori steps across in front of uke's advancing leg or both legs, drops laterally to the side, and extends their own leg to block uke's shins or ankles while using an arm pull to topple uke sideways. The throw is from the classical Gokyo no Waza (originally placed in the second group). It differs from Tani-Otoshi in that the drop is to the side — perpendicular to uke's line of travel — rather than directly behind. The side drop mechanics mean tori falls perpendicular to uke while the blocking leg intercepts forward movement, creating a lateral topple.","phases":[{"label":"Kuzushi · Off-balance","text":"Pull uke forward and diagonally to the side — typically with the sleeve grip pulling across tori's front — to displace uke's weight laterally. As uke's weight shifts in that direction and their forward movement is already committed, the combination of lateral pull and forward shin block creates a kuzushi that is both sideways and rotational."},{"label":"Tsukuri · Entry","text":"Step your lead foot across and in front of uke's advancing shin or ankle — positioning it as a lateral barrier. Drop your body to the side, extending your body perpendicular to uke. Your extended arm (sleeve grip) creates the blocking line along uke's advancing legs."},{"label":"Kake · Execution","text":"As you drop to the side, your extended leg (or arm along the mat) blocks uke's shin-level forward progress. The sleeve-grip pull topples uke's upper body sideways and forward over the blocking barrier. Uke falls to the side — perpendicular to their original direction of travel."}],"steps":[{"title":"Create forward movement in uke","text":"Use your grips to pull uke forward and toward the side you intend to throw. Uke must be stepping forward — Yoko-Otoshi requires forward momentum to exploit."},{"title":"Step across in front of uke's advancing leg","text":"Step your foot across and in front of uke's forward leg (or between uke's legs for a two-legged block). This step positions your leg as a lateral shin-level barrier."},{"title":"Drop to the side perpendicular to uke","text":"Drop your body to the same side as your blocking leg, falling perpendicular to uke's line of movement. This is a sacrifice — you are deliberately going to the mat to create the throw."},{"title":"Extend your body and arm as a block","text":"As you fall, extend your leg and/or arm along the mat at uke's shin level to create the barrier. Your extended position across uke's path is the structural element of the throw."},{"title":"Pull uke's arm to complete the sideways topple","text":"With your sleeve grip, pull uke's arm laterally and downward as you fall. Uke's forward momentum is redirected sideways over your blocking limb, and they fall beside you."}],"principles":["Yoko-Otoshi is a side throw — the drop is perpendicular to uke's forward movement, not behind them (which would be Tani-Otoshi).","The throw requires forward momentum in uke. Against a static uke, the side drop is easily avoided by stepping.","The blocking element is at shin or ankle level — the barrier is low and intercepts forward movement of the legs.","This is a sutemi (sacrifice) throw — tori must commit fully to the drop. A hesitant, half-committed fall fails to create the blocking barrier.","The arm pull direction is the same as the fall direction — lateral and forward, not straight backward."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Dropping directly behind uke instead of to the side","fix":"Yoko-Otoshi falls to the side (perpendicular). Falling directly behind creates Tani-Otoshi mechanics. Position your body perpendicular to uke's line of travel."},{"mistake":"Attempting on a static, non-moving uke","fix":"Create forward movement with your grips before dropping. Without uke's forward momentum, they simply step over the blocking leg."},{"mistake":"Block leg too high — uke steps over it","fix":"The blocking barrier should be at shin or ankle level. A thigh-level block is too high and can be stepped over."},{"mistake":"Half-hearted sacrifice — falling slowly and failing to create the barrier","fix":"Commit to the drop fully. Yoko-Otoshi must be a decisive lateral fall to create an effective blocking position."}],"when":"Yoko-Otoshi is most effective when uke is stepping aggressively forward — during an attack entry, after a failed grip fight, or when uke is charging into you. The throw works as a counter to powerful forward pressure. It can also be applied as a sacrifice option when uke's guard is very tight from above but their forward step exposes the shin level.","setups":["seoi-nage","osoto-gari"],"followUps":[],"counters":[],"related":["tani-otoshi","yoko-gake","yoko-wakare"],"source":"https://judolearn.com/techniques/yoko-otoshi/"},{"id":"yoko-shiho-gatame","name":"Yoko-Shiho-Gatame","kanji":"横四方固","translation":"Side Four-Corner Hold","category":"Katame-Waza","subcategory":"Osaekomi-Waza","difficulty":1,"grade":"5th kyu","grades":["5th kyu","4th kyu"],"videos":[{"yt":"TT7XJVSEQxA","title":"横四方固 /  Yoko-shiho-gatame","channel":"KODOKAN"},{"yt":"lOMiYZzvH-s","title":"Escape Any Yoko Shiho Gatame - Travis Stevens Basic Judo Techniques","channel":"Travis Stevens"},{"yt":"1BLni2dgtXA","title":"Yoko-Shiho-Gatame Tips | Riki Judo Dojo","channel":"Riki Judo Dojo"},{"yt":"WtdxlNh6JiI","title":"Yoko-Shiho-Gatame Variations | Riki Judo Dojo","channel":"Riki Judo Dojo"}],"overview":"Yoko-Shiho-Gatame is the most common hold-down used in competition judo. Tori controls uke from the side, pinning both their shoulders to the mat. One arm traps between uke's legs gripping their belt or trousers, the other controls uke's near arm and neck. The four-point contact of hands gives stable, rotational control.","phases":[],"steps":[{"title":"Position at uke's right side, perpendicular to their body","text":"Kneel at uke's right side with your chest facing toward their feet. You are perpendicular to uke, facing toward their legs."},{"title":"Thread your left arm between uke's legs","text":"Slide your left arm between uke's legs and grip their belt, trouser hem, or the mat. This controls uke's lower body and prevents the bridge escape."},{"title":"Wrap your right arm around uke's neck/head","text":"Wrap your right arm under uke's neck, reaching to control their head, or trap uke's near arm under your armpit. The head control version is stronger for preventing uke's rotation. Grip their far collar or lapel."},{"title":"Drive your chest onto uke's chest","text":"Lower your chest down onto uke's sternum, applying body weight directly. Spread your knees wide for a stable base."},{"title":"Maintain position and adjust to uke's escape attempts","text":"Keep your weight on uke's chest. If uke turns into you, flow to Kami-Shiho-Gatame or mount. If they turn away, switch to the other side."}],"principles":["The arm between the legs prevents the hip bridge escape — this is the critical control point.","Chest-to-chest contact: your body weight on uke's chest pins them.","Wide knee base: spread your knees to lower your center of gravity.","Follow uke's movement — if they roll, you roll with them rather than fighting their motion."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Not threading the arm between the legs","fix":"This arm is essential for holding the lower body. Without it, uke bridges and rolls you off."},{"mistake":"Kneeling rather than spreading the legs wide","fix":"Spread your knees wide on the mat. A high kneeling position is easily rolled."},{"mistake":"Holding too rigidly — fighting uke's roll","fix":"If uke rolls one way, flow to a different pin. Fighting directly against rotation is tiring and unsuccessful."},{"mistake":"Placing a knee or hip on top of uke","fix":"In competition, kneeling or sitting on uke is prohibited (interpreted as kakaekomi). Keep your knees on the mat and your weight distributed through your chest and arms, not on top of uke's body."}],"when":"Yoko-Shiho-Gatame transitions naturally from throws where uke lands on their back and tori is at their side. It is the most versatile hold-down for transitioning to pins on either side. From Yoko-Shiho-Gatame, tori can easily transition to mount, Kami-Shiho-Gatame, or arm locks.","setups":[],"followUps":["juji-gatame","ude-garami"],"counters":[],"related":["kesa-gatame","kami-shiho-gatame","juji-gatame"],"source":"https://judolearn.com/techniques/yoko-shiho-gatame/"},{"id":"yoko-wakare","name":"Yoko-Wakare","kanji":"横分","translation":"Side Separation","category":"Nage-Waza","subcategory":"Yoko-Sutemi-Waza","difficulty":2,"grade":"3rd kyu","grades":["3rd kyu","2nd kyu"],"videos":[{"yt":"bp1tscHlePI","title":"横分 / Yoko-wakare","channel":"KODOKAN"},{"yt":"zQl47PBXqGI","title":"Yoko-Wakare Tips | Riki Judo Dojo","channel":"Riki Judo Dojo"},{"yt":"6Mh0JSyirKw","title":"Yoko Wakare is highly effective when your opponent is not expecting it.","channel":"Nick Yonezuka - Cranford JKC"},{"yt":"fHXIAmbHzOY","title":"Overhook Yoko-wakare - Demo","channel":"Efficient Judo"},{"yt":"blsH2YhUK8Y","title":"yoko wakare compilation","channel":"Ochiru"}],"overview":"Yoko-Wakare is a side sacrifice throw where tori falls to the side perpendicular to uke, using the fall and arm pull to sweep uke over and separate (wakare) from them as uke is thrown. It is one of the classical side sacrifice throws appearing in the Nage-no-Kata and illustrates the principle of using body weight and momentum from a side fall to achieve a throw.","phases":[{"label":"Kuzushi · Off-balance","text":"Pull uke strongly to the side — in the direction tori will fall. The sleeve and lapel grips pull uke's upper body toward tori's falling side. Uke's balance is broken sideways onto their front corner in the direction of the sacrifice."},{"label":"Tsukuri · Entry","text":"Step across in front of uke and position your body perpendicular to uke's direction. As you begin to fall to the side, your body slides across uke's path. The sleeve grip maintains contact to guide uke's arm as you fall."},{"label":"Kake · Execution","text":"Fall directly to the side — the sacrifice. As you fall, pull uke's upper body over and in the direction of your fall using the grips. Your falling body weight and the arm pull together sweep uke over and separate you both — uke lands on the mat while tori is on their side."}],"steps":[{"title":"Create sideward kuzushi","text":"Pull uke toward your intended falling side with both grips. Uke's balance shifts sideways."},{"title":"Step across uke's path","text":"Step or swing your body across uke's front, perpendicular to them."},{"title":"Fall to the side — the sacrifice","text":"Fall directly to the side, committing fully. The fall must be decisive and take you fully to the ground."},{"title":"Pull uke with both grips","text":"As you fall, pull uke's upper body with the grips, directing them over and in the same direction as your fall."},{"title":"Separate from uke as they land","text":"Uke is thrown over and to the mat. The \"wakare\" (separation) occurs as your bodies separate at the landing — tori rolls clear."}],"principles":["The fall must be complete and committed. A half-hearted fall leaves you partially upright and the throw incomplete.","The grips pull uke in the same direction as tori's fall. Both forces work together.","Yoko-Wakare is a classical technique — focus on clean execution and the principle of side sacrifice.","The \"separation\" (wakare) is built into the technique — tori and uke separate cleanly as the throw is completed."],"mistakes":[{"mistake":"Falling too far behind uke — no directional leverage","fix":"Position your body perpendicular to uke before falling. The side fall must sweep across uke's path, not behind them."},{"mistake":"Not committing fully to the fall","fix":"Yoko-Wakare requires total sacrifice. A partial fall results in a tangled position, not a clean throw."},{"mistake":"Losing grip during the fall","fix":"Maintain grips throughout the fall. The grips direct uke's trajectory and prevent them from simply stepping over tori."},{"mistake":"Insufficient kuzushi before falling","fix":"Pull uke off balance before sacrificing. Falling when uke is upright and balanced means they simply step over you."}],"when":"Yoko-Wakare is most practical when uke is moving to the side or when the position of uke relative to tori makes a side sacrifice more accessible than a forward throw. It is a Nage-no-Kata technique and is regularly practiced in kata training.","setups":["de-ashi-barai"],"followUps":["kesa-gatame"],"counters":[],"related":["yoko-gake","uki-waza","yoko-guruma"],"source":"https://judolearn.com/techniques/yoko-wakare/"}];
